// Every rule, term, guide, diagram and form, for docs/search.js; written by bin/build
window.SEARCH_INDEX = [
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CORE","code":"CORE","name":"Uniformity","text":"core uniformity the rule behind the rules."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CORE-01","code":"CORE-01","name":"Explicit rules take priority for new code","text":"core-01 explicit rules take priority for new code. follow the surrounding code only where no explicit rule applies. a new rule does not require rewriting existing code; that refactoring can happen separately. an explicit rule requires error : use function (error) in new code even beside older function (err) handlers. a written rule beside older code: the old handler keeps function (err), the new one is written function (error); rewriting the old is separate work docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CORE-02","code":"CORE-02","name":"Unwritten conventions bind too","text":"core-02 unwritten conventions bind too. an unwritten convention is still a convention. file names, css classes, formatting and even attribute order follow the same systematic approach, even where it is not written down. no rule names it, yet every file in components/ is kebab-case: user-menu.vue follows them, usermenu.vue does not; the same for classes, formatting and attribute order docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CORE-03","code":"CORE-03","name":"An option is \"value\" and \"label\"","text":"core-03 an option is \"value\" and \"label\". a selectable option is always {value, label} — value is internal, label is displayed. select, dropdown, radio, tabs alike. {value: , label: } one list of {value, label} options shown as a select, radio buttons and tabs; the user reads netherlands, the code sends country=nl value_label.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CORE-04","code":"CORE-04","name":"Opposites come from the established pairs","text":"core-04 opposites come from the established pairs. req/res is express-only; res/rej is valid for promise executors, as are the full names resolve/reject . since/until is begin/end said of time. construct/destruct create/destroy open/close begin/end start/finish first/last next/previous get/put src/dest source/destination res/rej (promise) resolve/reject req/res (express) request/response setup/teardown push/pull enabled/disabled import/export since/until (time) made-up pairs and the established ones they should be: begin/finish to begin/end, create/remove to create/destroy, open/shut to open/close; and the narrow pairs with where each holds docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME","code":"NAME","name":"Naming grammar","text":"name naming grammar verb morphology decides function or data."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-01","code":"NAME-01","name":"Functions are verbs; data are nouns","text":"name-01 functions are verbs; data are nouns. functions are verb phrases, data are noun phrases. each assignment reads as a sentence — the verb performs, the noun holds. const users_by_role = users_group_by_role(users); users flows into users_group_by_role(), a verb, which makes users_by_role, a noun; the line read as a sentence docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-02","code":"NAME-02","name":"The verb form marks function or data","text":"name-02 the verb form marks function or data. english verb morphology is the marker. an imperative verb makes a function; a past participle makes data. where there is no verb, a function marker — _from_ , _to_ , _of_ , check_ — makes a function. one exception: a linter rule's function is named after its rule id, as eslint does, block_layout(context) for block-layout . items_sort_by_time(items) // function items_sorted_by_time // data items_sort_by_time(items), an imperative, is a function; items_sorted_by_time, a past participle, is data; with no verb, _from_, _to_, _of_ or check_ makes a function naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-03","code":"NAME-03","name":"A bare \"by\" name is data, never called","text":"name-03 a bare \"by\" name is data, never called. a bare _by_ name is always data, never a function: it is read with brackets, or with .get() when it is a map — never called. user_by_id& 91;id] ✓ right user_by_id.get(id) ✓ right, a map user_by_id(id) ✗ wrong user_by_id as a table of ids and users: read with brackets or .get(), never called docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-04","code":"NAME-04","name":"The first word's plural states what a key returns","text":"name-04 the first word's plural states what a key returns. plurality of the first word states lookup cardinality — what one key returns, not how big the container is. user_by_id& 91;id] // user users_by_role& 91;role] // user& 91;] children_by_parent_id& 91;parent_id] // node& 91;] user_by_id returns one user per key; users_by_role and children_by_parent_id return a list per key var_names.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-05","code":"NAME-05","name":"\"Sorted by\" is the same data, reordered","text":"name-05 \"sorted by\" is the same data, reordered. _sorted_by_ marks data of the same shape, reordered. the participle survives here because it carries what shape alone cannot. items_sorted_by_time // item[], still an array four items by time, and the same four reordered as items_sorted_by_time, still an array naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-06","code":"NAME-06","name":"_per_ marks a ratio or a rate","text":"name-06 _per_ marks a ratio or a rate. clicks_per_visit 1 240 clicks over 400 visits make clicks_per_visit, 3.1: a rate, not a list naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-07","code":"NAME-07","name":"Group by, sort by, index by produce them","text":"name-07 group by, sort by, index by produce them. the operations that produce those shapes are _group_by_ , _sort_by_ and _index_by_ . the criterion follows the verb. users_index_by_id(users) // user_by_id users_group_by_role(users) // users_by_role items_sort_by_time(items) // items_sorted_by_time users_index_by_id() makes user_by_id, users_group_by_role() makes users_by_role, items_sort_by_time() makes items_sorted_by_time docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-08","code":"NAME-08","name":"\"From\" means computed: result first, source last","text":"name-08 \"from\" means computed: result first, source last. use _from_ only when something is computed, parsed or constructed — result first, source last. user_from_token(token) date_from_timestamp(ts) tree_from_array(rows) a token is parsed into a user: user_from_token, the result first and the source last docs/readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-09","code":"NAME-09","name":"_from_ never means lookup","text":"name-09 _from_ never means lookup. a lookup disguised as construction is wrong. user_from_id(id) ✗ wrong user_by_id& 91;id] ✓ right ![an id looked up in the user_by_id table: user_by_id[id] is right, user_from_id(id) dresses the lookup as construction](../img/rules/from_is_not_lookup.svg) naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-10","code":"NAME-10","name":"\"To\" belongs in method position","text":"name-10 \"to\" belongs in method position. a standalone conversion prefers _from_ ; _to_ is for method position, where the source is the receiver. tree.to_json() tree.to_json() with tree as the receiver; standalone it is json_from_tree(tree), never tree_to_json(tree) naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-11","code":"NAME-11","name":"No \"to\" in data names","text":"name-11 no \"to\" in data names. _to_ is banned in data names — id_to_name is only a second spelling of name_by_id . id_to_name ✗ banned name_by_id ✓ right a map from ids to names is name_by_id; id_to_name is a second name for the same map naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-12","code":"NAME-12","name":"No \"indexed by\"; bare \"by\" says it","text":"name-12 no \"indexed by\"; bare \"by\" says it. _indexed_by_ does not exist. bare _by_ already names that shape. users_indexed_by_id ✗ does not exist user_by_id ✓ right users_index_by_id() makes user_by_id; users_indexed_by_id does not exist naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-13","code":"NAME-13","name":"\"Grouped by\" only for invariant plurals","text":"name-13 \"grouped by\" only for invariant plurals. _grouped_by_ is retired from data names except for invariant plurals — fish , data , series — which cannot state cardinality by plurality. prefer a countable noun when one exists. fish_by_tank // record<tank, fish fish_grouped_by_tank // record<tank, fish& 91;] rows_by_source ✓ over data_by_source fish_by_tank returns one fish, fish_grouped_by_tank a list; rows_by_source is preferred over data_by_source var_names.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-14","code":"NAME-14","name":"\"Of\" queries possession","text":"name-14 \"of\" queries possession. _of_ is a possession query. descendants_of(node) a node and its five descendants in a tree, asked for as descendants_of(node) naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-15","code":"NAME-15","name":"\"is\" names a boolean, a predicate or data","text":"name-15 \"is\" names a boolean, a predicate or data. is_ names a boolean: a predicate that returns one, or data that holds one. it is not a function marker; a check that must read as a call is check_ , a verb. is_ancestor(a, b) // a predicate, called is_open // a boolean, read check_status() // a check that reads as a call is_ancestor(a, b) is called and returns true, is_open is read as false, check_status() is a verb that runs naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-16","code":"NAME-16","name":"A conversion and its variable share a first word","text":"name-16 a conversion and its variable share a first word. a standalone conversion and the variable it fills share their first word. const json = json_from_tree(tree); const json = json_from_tree(tree): the variable and the conversion share the word json naming_markers.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-17","code":"NAME-17","name":"Names start with the domain","text":"name-17 names start with the domain. naming is domain-first. functions and files follow items_ , item_ or domain nouns; generic names are avoided. items_index_by_uid() ✓ right process() handle() util() ✗ avoid domain-first names such as items_sort_by_time() stand together; handle(), process() and util() say nothing formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"NAME-18","code":"NAME-18","name":"Verb-first families are the exceptions","text":"name-18 verb-first families are the exceptions. the verb-first families are the exceptions to domain-first naming: main — reserved for the entry function of an executable script — format_ , render_ , export_ , is_ , refresh_ , click_ , emit_ , check_ (mp-40), make_ (mp-53), and the steps of a script ( build_glossaries_html() ). a step is a function main() calls once per part of the work, in order; a verb-first name that is not a step is renamed when next touched. function main() { report_print(); } main, format_ , render_ , export_ , is_ , refresh_ , click_ , emit_ , check_ and make_ start with their verb; main runs the steps of a script formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR","code":"VAR","name":"Variables","text":"var variables a name states the shape of its data."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-01","code":"VAR-01","name":"A variable name states the shape of its data","text":"var-01 a variable name states the shape of its data. users // user& 91;] user_by_id // record<id, user users_by_role // record<role, user& 91;] items_sorted_by_time // item& 91;], reordered four names and the shapes they state: users a list, user_by_id one user per id, users_by_role a list per role, items_sorted_by_time the same list reordered var_names.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-02","code":"VAR-02","name":"Name the result out","text":"var-02 name the result out. a variable the function builds and returns unchanged is named out — return out; , exactly. a parameter, a loop variable or an outer value returned unchanged keeps its own name: return user; . function emails_from_users(users) { const out = & 91;]; for (const user of users) { if (user.email) { out.push(user.email); } } return out; } emails_from_users builds out, pushes into it and returns out; returning a parameter unchanged as out is wrong, return user; is right return_out.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-03","code":"VAR-03","name":"out never leaves the function","text":"var-03 out never leaves the function. the caller names the result by what it means. const emails = emails_from_users(users); const users_by_role = users_group_by_role(users); inside emails_from_users the result is out; the caller names it emails return_out.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-04","code":"VAR-04","name":"Return a literal directly","text":"var-04 return a literal directly. returning a literal or an expression directly is allowed; a guard return may return null , false , [] , {} . const out = {...}; return out; is rewritten as return {...}; const out = {a, b}; return out; ✗ rewrite this return {a, b}; ✓ as this const out = {a, b}; return out; rewritten as return {a, b};, and the guard returns null, false, () and {} formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-05","code":"VAR-05","name":"Errors use e in arrows, error in regular handlers","text":"var-05 errors use e in arrows, error in regular handlers. a caught error is error in a catch binding or regular function, and e in an arrow. this applies to promise rejection handlers and eventemitter error listeners. callback names stay e / error at every depth. dom error events follow var-16; only nested catch bindings use numbered names (var-17). promise.catch(e = report(e)); server.on('error', function (error) { throw error; }); catch (error) { console.log(error.message); } an error is e in an arrow, error in a regular function and in a catch binding var_names_error.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-06","code":"VAR-06","name":"Reserve e for error arrows; no err or ex","text":"var-06 reserve e for error arrows; no err or ex. e names an arrow error parameter. a regular error parameter or catch binding uses error , never e , err or ex ; only catch bindings get the nested numbers in var-17. catch (e) ✗ no catch (err) ✗ no catch (error) ✓ yes promise.catch(e = report(e)); ✓ yes catch (err), catch (ex) and catch (e) each rewritten as catch (error); e only in an error arrow var_names_error.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-07","code":"VAR-07","name":"Name stderr text by what it is","text":"var-07 name stderr text by what it is. accumulated stderr text is not an error variable. name it by what it is. stderr ✓ right err ✗ wrong a child process writes stderr text, collected as stderr, not as err var_names_error.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-08","code":"VAR-08","name":"Prefer explicit temporaries","text":"var-08 prefer explicit temporaries. use intermediate variables instead of nested expressions; favor clarity over terseness. a nested send(format_report(rows_filter(...))) rewritten as three named steps formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-09","code":"VAR-09","name":"Arrow values use v; arrow errors use e","text":"var-09 arrow values use v; arrow errors use e. a lone value or event parameter is v ; vv only when v is already taken where the arrow stands, vvv only when vv is. an arrow with no parameter, an error arrow or a parameter with another name takes nothing: () = items.filter(v = v.ok) , promise.catch(e = report(e, items.map(v = v.uid))) . a parameter the arrow does not use is not declared: promise.catch(() = items.map(v = v.uid)) , not e = … (lint-03); a regular function may. error arrows keep e at every depth (var-05). with several parameters, choose names for their roles; an error callback’s first parameter still follows the error naming rule. items.map(v = v.uid) items.some(v = v.sizes.some(vv = vv.width 100)) promise.catch(e = report(e)) nested arrows name their value v, then vv only when v is taken, then vvv; an error arrow keeps e formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-10","code":"VAR-10","name":"The for...of loop variable is the singular of the collection","text":"var-10 the for...of loop variable is the singular of the collection. for (const user of users) { } for (const child of node.children) { } users gives user and node.children gives child; for (const x of users) rewritten as for (const user of users) for_of.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-11","code":"VAR-11","name":"A cached length is \"end\"","text":"var-11 a cached length is \"end\". a cached array length in a single loop is named end . for (let i = 0, end = items.length; i < end; ++i) { } six items, the index i and the cached bound end = 6, in for (let i = 0, end = items.length; i < end; ++i) for_i_end_ii_jj_kk.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-12","code":"VAR-12","name":"Nested bounds double the index: ii, jj, kk","text":"var-12 nested bounds double the index: ii, jj, kk. in nested loops the cached bound is the doubled index name: i → ii, j → jj, k → kk. for (let i = 0, ii = foo.length; i < ii; ++i) { for (let j = 0, jj = bar.length; j < jj; ++j) { for (let k = 0, kk = baz.length; k < kk; ++k) { } } } three nested loops: i with ii, j with jj, k with kk, each bound its index doubled for_i_end_ii_jj_kk.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-13","code":"VAR-13","name":"A transformed or conditional result is not \"out\"","text":"var-13 a transformed or conditional result is not \"out\". only that variable is out . a value that is joined, stringified or otherwise transformed on its way out, or returned only on a condition ( ?: , if ), is not out ; it is named by what it is. out returned on a condition and out joined on its way out, each beside the same code with a name of its own; below, the one right shape: built, then return out; return_out.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-14","code":"VAR-14","name":"Start time is \"time0\"","text":"var-14 start time is \"time0\". the variable holding the start time of a measurement is always named time0 . the elapsed time is the current time minus time0 : a number, shown to a person only through format_duration (fn-02), never as ${date.now() - time0}ms at the call site. $time0 = microtime(true); echo format_duration(microtime(true) - $time0); const time0 = date.now(); console.log(format_duration(date.now() - time0)); time0, the work, date.now() - time0, then format_duration gives 2m 35s; a hand-rolled ms string rewritten as format_duration var_names_time0.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-15","code":"VAR-15","name":"Use time0 for measurement; paired names for other roles","text":"var-15 use time0 for measurement; paired names for other roles. do not name an elapsed-time start begin , start or t0 . outside that role, use start/finish or begin/end when the pair is actually used. const time0 = date.now(); // elapsed-time measurement const start = range.start; const finish = range.finish; a measurement start named start rewritten as time0; a range keeps start and finish or begin and end var_names_time0.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-16","code":"VAR-16","name":"Events use v in arrows, event in regular functions","text":"var-16 events use v in arrows, event in regular functions. a dom event is a value, including an event whose type is error . a one-parameter arrow uses v ; a regular event handler uses event . reserve e for error values in arrows. el.addeventlistener('click', v = v.stoppropagation()); el.addeventlistener('error', v = report(v)); el.addeventlistener('error', function (event) { report(event); }); a click or error event is v in an arrow and event in a regular function var_names_event.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-17","code":"VAR-17","name":"Nested catch bindings use error2, error3","text":"var-17 nested catch bindings use error2, error3. a catch inside another catch names its binding error2 , the next level error3 . this applies only to catch bindings: callback parameters stay e in arrows and error in regular functions, even inside a catch. catch (error) { try { await save_backup(scene); } catch (error2) { } } a catch inside a catch binds error2, the next error3; callbacks stay e or error var_names_event.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-18","code":"VAR-18","name":"A generic name has one meaning","text":"var-18 a generic name has one meaning. i , j , k , end , ii , v , e , error , event , out , time0 , req , res and rej each have the meaning listed on one page, res one in each of its pairs, req/res and res/rej ; anywhere else the variable is named by what it holds. const out = & 91;...new set(tags)]; return (out.length === tags.length) ? tags : out; ✗ no const unique_tags = & 91;...new set(tags)]; return (unique_tags.length === tags.length) ? tags : unique_tags; ✓ yes the generic names and their meanings, res in both its pairs; out returned on a condition rewritten as unique_tags generic_names.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VAR-19","code":"VAR-19","name":"A configuration value at the top is UPPER_CASE","text":"var-19 a configuration value at the top is upper_case. a constant the module keeps as configuration, a global name for a value set in the code, is written in upper case at the module's top level. a const inside a function or a block is lower case. imports are not this rule. const press_ms = 400; // the module's top function press_start(event) { const started = date.now(); // inside } press_ms and blob_url at the module's top in upper case; started and target inside a function in lower case manually_approved_rules.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN","code":"FN","name":"Function contracts","text":"fn function contracts what a prefix promises the caller."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-01","code":"FN-01","name":"\"format\" strictly returns human-facing text","text":"fn-01 \"format\" strictly returns human-facing text. format_ returns a string to show to a person, including when the value comes from object state. format_bytes(1457664) // \"1.4 mb\" format_duration(155000) // \"2m 35s\" format_usd(1299) // \"$12.99\" format_bytes, format_duration and format_usd turn values into the strings a person reads format_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-05","code":"FN-05","name":"\"render\" constructs asset content","text":"fn-05 \"render\" constructs asset content. render_ constructs content such as css, html or svg from state. the result can be a string; its purpose is an asset rather than a human-facing formatted value. public function render_css(): string { return \"body { color: {$this- text_color}; }\"; } render_css and render_svg turn state into css and svg assets render_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-02","code":"FN-02","name":"Never hand-roll a display conversion","text":"fn-02 never hand-roll a display conversion. a display conversion is never hand-rolled at a call site — an inline one is a missed format_ . ${math.round(bytes / 1024)}kb ✗ missed call format_bytes(bytes) ✓ right three hand-rolled byte conversions giving three answers, each rewritten as format_bytes(b) giving 1.4 mb format_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-03","code":"FN-03","name":"format_* returns — it never prints","text":"fn-03 format_ returns — it never prints. output is the caller’s job. console.log(format_tree(tree)); format_tree returns the text and the caller prints it; a format_tree that prints rewritten as console.log(format_tree(tree)) format_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-04","code":"FN-04","name":"\"format\" is not serialization","text":"fn-04 \"format\" is not serialization. format_ is not for machine formats. serialization is conversion, not display. format_user(user) // \"alex k. (admin)\" — humans json_from_user(user) // machines format_user gives the text a person reads; json_from_user gives the json a machine reads format_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-06","code":"FN-06","name":"\"render\" is recomputed, never stored","text":"fn-06 \"render\" is recomputed, never stored. the prefix separates constructed content from stored attributes. render_ is recomputed per call — never cached, never stored. $this- text_color // stored attribute $this- render_css() // constructed content a stored attribute is read; render_css is made on each call; storing its result rewritten as calling it where needed render_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-07","code":"FN-07","name":"\"render\" may return null or throw","text":"fn-07 \"render\" may return null or throw. render_ may return null when not applicable, and may throw when the state makes the value meaningless. if (!$this- theme) { return null; } render_css returns css for a theme, null with no theme, and throws for a broken one render_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-08","code":"FN-08","name":"\"render\" is sync and pure; downloads are \"export\"","text":"fn-08 \"render\" is sync and pure; downloads are \"export\". render_ constructs content without dom updates, file writes or downloads, and always returns its result. a function that brings a part of the page up to date returns nothing and is refresh_ (fn-10), never _render . a downloadable artifact — zip, csv or xlsx — is export_ , which may be async and side-effectful. $theme- render_css() // construct css content refresh_theme_labels() // bring a part of the page up to date export_zip() // produce a downloadable artifact render_css returns css, refresh_theme_labels updates the page, export_zip makes a download; theme_labels_render rewritten as refresh_theme_labels render_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-09","code":"FN-09","name":"Format and render differ by output purpose","text":"fn-09 format and render differ by output purpose. format_ returns a string a person reads; render_ constructs asset content. both may return strings; reading object state does not turn display text into render output. format_bytes(1457664) // \"1.4 mb\", human-facing text $theme- render_css() // css asset content format_bytes returns text a person reads; render_css returns css a browser uses format_xxx.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-10","code":"FN-10","name":"A local copy of remote data has \"refresh\"","text":"fn-10 a local copy of remote data has \"refresh\". anything that holds a local copy of remote data has refresh() — it pulls the data and brings the local vars up to date. mental model: the browser refresh button. refresh: async function () { await promise.all(& 91; this.refresh_user(), this.refresh_photos(), ]); }, refresh pulls the remote data into the local copy, this.user and this.photos refresh.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-11","code":"FN-11","name":"Refresh is safe to call anytime","text":"fn-11 refresh is safe to call anytime. refresh() is idempotent — safe to call at any moment, any number of times. refresh called on load, after a save and twice in a row reaches the same up-to-date state refresh.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-12","code":"FN-12","name":"Refresh composes per-part refreshes","text":"fn-12 refresh composes per-part refreshes. a fine-grained refresh_() syncs one part of the state; refresh() composes them. refresh_user: async function () { this.user = await fetch_user(); }, refresh runs refresh_user and refresh_photos, each filling its own part refresh.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-13","code":"FN-13","name":"Init is the first refresh","text":"fn-13 init is the first refresh. mounted: async function () { await this.refresh(); this.ready = true; }, mounted awaits this.refresh(), then sets this.ready = true refresh.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-14","code":"FN-14","name":"A modal returns a commit flag","text":"fn-14 a modal returns a commit flag. an interaction — modal, popover — executes its own logic and returns a boolean commit flag. it expresses intent; it does not expose control flow. these are the best practices for vue-modal: modal.return(true) inside, await modal_x().promise() at the call site. // true → changes were committed // false → no-op (cancel, or no changes) if (await modal_upload().promise()) { await blocking(this.refresh()); } the caller awaits modal_upload; the modal does its own work and returns true, committed, or false, nothing to do interactions-should-return-only-boolean-flag.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-15","code":"FN-15","name":"Guards return early","text":"fn-15 guards return early. validate inputs immediately and return null or empty values explicitly. the work buried in nested ifs rewritten as guards that return null and () first formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FN-16","code":"FN-16","name":"One public value per library module","text":"fn-16 one public value per library module. a reusable module exports one function, class, configuration or data value. executable scripts export nothing; tool-shaped files follow the tool. format_bytes.js, theme.js and config.js export one value each, bin/build exports nothing; a helpers.js exporting three values split into three modules formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT","code":"FMT","name":"Formatting","text":"fmt formatting braces, breaks, blank lines, comments."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-01","code":"FMT-01","name":"Four spaces, no tabs","text":"fmt-01 four spaces, no tabs. indentation is exactly 4 spaces. no tabs. a tab before const user turned into four spaces, shown as dots formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-02","code":"FMT-02","name":"A top-level function declaration puts its opening brace on the next line","text":"fmt-02 a top-level function declaration puts its opening brace on the next line. function main() { } function main() { with the brace on its line, turned into the brace on the next line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-03","code":"FMT-03","name":"Nested function: brace on the same line","text":"fmt-03 nested function: brace on the same line. a function declared inside another function keeps its opening brace on the declaration line. function main() { function limit_print(limit) { } } limit_print nested in main: its brace moves from a line of its own to the declaration line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-04","code":"FMT-04","name":"Callbacks keep the brace on the line","text":"fmt-04 callbacks keep the brace on the line. function expressions, callbacks and promise executors keep the opening brace on the declaration line. server.on('error', function (error) { // ... }); return new promise(function (resolve, reject) { // ... }); a callback and a promise executor with the brace below, turned into the brace on the declaration line formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-05","code":"FMT-05","name":"Class brace below; method brace inline","text":"fmt-05 class brace below; method brace inline. a class declaration puts its brace on the next line; its methods keep the brace on the declaration line. class logger { constructor(parent_group_uid) { } write(format, ...values) { } } class logger with its brace inline and a method brace below, turned into the class brace below and the method brace inline formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-07","code":"FMT-07","name":"Always use braces","text":"fmt-07 always use braces. always use braces for if , else , for , while . no single-line bodies. if (!user) return null; ✗ no if (!user) { ✓ yes return null; } if (!user) return null; on one line, turned into the same body in braces formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-08","code":"FMT-08","name":"else starts on a new line","text":"fmt-08 else starts on a new line. if (a) { } else if (b) { } else { } else glued to the closing brace, turned into else if and else each starting a line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-09","code":"FMT-09","name":"catch and finally start on a new line","text":"fmt-09 catch and finally start on a new line. try { } catch (error) { } finally { } catch and finally glued to the closing braces, turned into each starting a line formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-10","code":"FMT-10","name":"Align \"switch\" and \"case\"","text":"fmt-10 align \"switch\" and \"case\". switch and case are aligned at the same indentation level. switch (type) { case 'voice': break; case 'text': break; default: } case and default indented one level under switch, turned into aligned with switch formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-11","code":"FMT-11","name":"Keep short objects on one line; expand only when needed","text":"fmt-11 keep short objects on one line; expand only when needed. multiple fields alone do not require line breaks. when size or nested structure requires a multi-line object literal, use one field per line and a trailing comma. const next = {uid: item.uid, parent_uid: item.parent_uid, index}; a short object broken over lines turned into one line, and an expanded object given its trailing comma formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-12","code":"FMT-12","name":"Options-style methods are always function expressions","text":"fmt-12 options-style methods are always function expressions. even a return-only method without its own this or arguments keeps that form; the tiny-arrow rule applies to callbacks and value factories. const options = { refresh: async function () { }, px: function (value) { return value ? ${value}px : '0'; }, }; an options object with arrow methods, turned into the same methods written as function expressions formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-13","code":"FMT-13","name":"Keep an expression on one line when it fits on one line","text":"fmt-13 keep an expression on one line when it fits on one line. const total broken over two lines, turned into one line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-14","code":"FMT-14","name":"Never break after \"=\" before a call","text":"fmt-14 never break after \"=\" before a call. never split an assignment after = when its right-hand side is a single function call. const users_by_role = users_group_by_role(users); ✗ no const users_by_role = users_group_by_role(users); ✓ yes const user = with the call on the next line, turned into one line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-15","code":"FMT-15","name":"Break lines only to show structure","text":"fmt-15 break lines only to show structure. break a statement only when its size or nested structure makes the break necessary. line breaks must expose structure. a short condition broken for nothing turned into one line; a list of rows broken one row a line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-16","code":"FMT-16","name":"A blank line marks a phase change — nothing else","text":"fmt-16 a blank line marks a phase change — nothing else. add one only when the following statement begins an independent conceptual step. blank lines after every statement, turned into one blank line before the printing step formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-17","code":"FMT-17","name":"Keep setup next to its use","text":"fmt-17 keep setup next to its use. keep setup adjacent to the loop, condition or call that immediately uses it. tightly coupled setup and use are not separated merely because the statement type changes. const lines = () separated from its loop by a blank line, turned into right above it formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-18","code":"FMT-18","name":"Build objects from needed fields only","text":"fmt-18 build objects from needed fields only. construct an object from the required fields only. do not spread a full object when a subset is needed. {...item, index} ✗ bad {uid: item.uid, parent_uid: item.parent_uid, index} ✓ good an object built by spreading the whole item, turned into one that names uid and index formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-19","code":"FMT-19","name":"Comment intent, not mechanics","text":"fmt-19 comment intent, not mechanics. comments explain intent or policy only. no comments for obvious mechanics. one exception: the route comment of fmt-21 states the route. a comment repeating the call, turned into one giving the reason for the retry formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-20","code":"FMT-20","name":"Multi-line comments carry rules or guarantees","text":"fmt-20 multi-line comments carry rules or guarantees. a multi-line comment carries a rule, an invariant or a guarantee. a three-line comment retelling the steps, turned into a two-line limit the function keeps formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-21","code":"FMT-21","name":"Every route has a route comment","text":"fmt-21 every route has a route comment. every route function carries a route comment directly above it: method /path (params) . // express route handler // get /file/<name async function file_get(req, res) { } a route function with no comment, turned into one with its route comment, get /file/ endpoint_comment.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-22","code":"FMT-22","name":"Parentheses only when the route takes input","text":"fmt-22 parentheses only when the route takes input. parentheses appear only when the endpoint takes input — body, headers, query, comma-separated, in that order. // express route handler // post /upload (body, header:x-filename) function upload_post(req, res) { } get /health () losing its empty parentheses, and the upload route listing body before its header endpoint_comment.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-23","code":"FMT-23","name":"Headers prefixed; path params in the path","text":"fmt-23 headers prefixed; path params in the path. a header is prefixed with header: ; a path parameter is spelled in the path itself. header:x-filename /file/<name get /file (name, x-token) turned into get /file/ (header:x-token) endpoint_comment.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-24","code":"FMT-24","name":"An intent comment continues the same block on the next line","text":"fmt-24 an intent comment continues the same block on the next line. // post /upload (body, header:x-filename) // streams to data/ before the body is fully read. an intent comment split from the route comment by a blank line, turned into the next line of the same block endpoint_comment.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-25","code":"FMT-25","name":"Named functions outside class bodies are \"function\" declarations","text":"fmt-25 named functions outside class bodies are \"function\" declarations. every named function outside a class — top-level or nested — is a function name(...) declaration. class methods use their class syntax. function report_print() { } a function expression and an arrow held in const report_print, each turned into a function declaration formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-26","code":"FMT-26","name":"No spaces inside one-line braces","text":"fmt-26 no spaces inside one-line braces. a single-line object or array literal has no space inside its braces or brackets — a standalone literal, a nested one, or one row of a list. { value: 'today', label: 'today' } ✗ no {value: 'today', label: 'today'} ✓ yes & 91; 'remote', 'host', 'service' ] ✗ no & 91;'remote', 'host', 'service'] ✓ yes an object and an array with spaces inside their braces, turned into the same with none formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-27","code":"FMT-27","name":"Parenthesize compound operands in logic","text":"fmt-27 parenthesize compound operands in logic. in a logical expression ( && , , ?? ) and in the condition of a ternary, an operand that carries an operator of its own is wrapped in parentheses. a simple operand — identifier, member access, call, template literal, unary negation — stays bare, and a chain of one logical operator is one expression. a ternary given whole — a value, an argument, a return — stays bare too: it is wrapped only as an operand of another operator. a vue directive keeps its parentheses (vue-06), and only a directive: a condition moved out of a v-if into a computed or a method loses them. return is_dev & 124;& 124; i === end - 1; ✗ no return is_dev & 124;& 124; (i === end - 1); ✓ yes return (!is_ready && !is_gone); ✗ no return !is_ready && !is_gone; ✓ yes (state === 'done') ? 'ok' : 'wait' url: (is_api ? '/api/v1' : null), ✗ no url: is_api ? '/api/v1' : null, ✓ yes a comparison beside // and a ternary condition, each wrapped in parentheses, and a ternary or a chain given whole, bare formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-28","code":"FMT-28","name":"One variable per declaration","text":"fmt-28 one variable per declaration. each variable gets its own const or let statement; a declaration never lists several variables separated by commas. the header of an indexed loop is the exception. const osc = c.createoscillator(), gain = c.creategain(); ✗ no const osc = c.createoscillator(); const gain = c.creategain(); ✓ yes one const declaring osc and gain, turned into a const for each formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-29","code":"FMT-29","name":"One blank line between top-level functions","text":"fmt-29 one blank line between top-level functions. top-level functions stand one blank line apart: each function declared at module level is followed by one blank line before the next one. } function toggle_sound() ✗ no blank line } function toggle_sound() ✓ one blank line two top-level functions with no blank line between them, turned into one blank line apart formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-30","code":"FMT-30","name":"Only the last argument spans lines","text":"fmt-30 only the last argument spans lines. only the last argument of a call may span several lines; nothing follows a multi-line argument — no further argument after } , no chained call after }) . a function that would leave arguments dangling is declared inside the caller and passed by name; a chained call becomes a variable. page.evaluate(function (text) { // ... }, text); ✗ dangling page.evaluate(find_families, text); ✓ yes items.filter(function (item) { // ... }).map(v = v.uid); ✗ dangling a call with an argument after its multi-line function, and a call chained after one, each rewritten so nothing dangles formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-31","code":"FMT-31","name":"Build strings with template literals","text":"fmt-31 build strings with template literals. a string built from parts is a template literal; + never joins a string to a value. plain quotes stay for a one-line string with nothing to interpolate; a string that spans lines is a template literal (sql-01). 'weapons/turret-' + weapon.id ✗ no weapons/turret-${weapon.id} ✓ yes 'weapons/turret-' + weapon.id turned into a template literal formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FMT-32","code":"FMT-32","name":"An empty function is {} on one line","text":"fmt-32 an empty function is {} on one line. an empty function expression or arrow is a no-op, as ignore is: its body is {} on one line, never split over two. a function declaration and a method keep their braces on lines of their own; a body holding only a comment is not empty. params.update = params.update function () { }; ✗ no params.update = params.update function () {}; ✓ yes promise.catch(() = {}); ✓ yes an empty function expression split over two lines, turned into {} on one line formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW","code":"FLOW","name":"Control flow","text":"flow control flow imperative, explicit, no magic."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-01","code":"FLOW-01","name":"Prefer imperative control flow","text":"flow-01 prefer imperative control flow. a loop that keeps the uids of active items, beside its four steps in the order they run formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-02","code":"FLOW-02","name":"No index needed: use for...of","text":"flow-02 no index needed: use for...of. prefer for...of when the index is not needed. for (const item of items) { } an indexed loop that only fetches items(i), turned into for (const item of items) for_of.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-03","code":"FLOW-03","name":"Use an indexed loop when the index is needed","text":"flow-03 use an indexed loop when the index is needed. for (let i = 0, end = items.length; i < end; ++i) { } five items with their indexes: items(i) and items(i + 1) read together, so the loop counts for_of.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-04","code":"FLOW-04","name":"No do...while","text":"flow-04 no do...while. do { ... } while (...) does not exist. use while or for . do...while with its condition at the bottom, turned into while with the condition first formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-05","code":"FLOW-05","name":"Reduce and chains only on one line","text":"flow-05 reduce and chains only on one line. reduce , deep chaining and functional pipelines are allowed only if the entire expression fits on a single line. a one-line reduce kept; a four-line filter, map and reduce chain turned into a loop formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-06","code":"FLOW-06","name":"Arrows only for tiny callbacks","text":"flow-06 arrows only for tiny callbacks. do not use arrow functions for non-trivial logic. arrows are allowed only for tiny callbacks. const uids = items.map(v = v.uid); fresh.catch(ignore); an arrow holding a conditional computation, turned into a named function; tiny arrows kept formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-07","code":"FLOW-07","name":"A block-body arrow becomes a function","text":"flow-07 a block-body arrow becomes a function. an arrow with a block body does not exist — it becomes function (...) {} , with an optional _this inside. the empty () = {} , a no-op, is the one exception (fmt-32). request.on('end', async () = { ✗ does not exist }); request.on('end', async function () { ✓ this }); an async arrow with a block body, turned into async function; the empty () = {} is the exception formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-08","code":"FLOW-08","name":"A catch that ignores the error binds nothing","text":"flow-08 a catch that ignores the error binds nothing. try { } catch { } catch (error) with error never read, turned into catch {} formatting_blocks.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-09","code":"FLOW-09","name":"Plain data: Set, Map or {}","text":"flow-09 plain data: set, map or {}. use plain data structures — set , map or plain {} for lookups. no prototypes, no mutation via shared state. a module may own its state, a singleton over its own data, written only by its own functions; shared means across modules, and that is what is forbidden. let modal = null; // the module's own function modal_show() { modal = modal_make(); } set, map and {} for lookups; another module writing modal state, turned into a module writing only its own formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-10","code":"FLOW-10","name":"No implicit magic or hidden side effects","text":"flow-10 no implicit magic or hidden side effects. no implicit magic. no hidden side effects, no reliance on execution-order side effects. the one visible exception: side-effect imports, which sit at the very top of the file, in the order they run (file-11). import './sass/main.sass'; // the top of the file, in run order import './main-pre'; import vue from 'vue'; a side-effect import hidden mid-list, moved to the top block in run order formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-11","code":"FLOW-11","name":"A return-only callback is an arrow","text":"flow-11 a return-only callback is an arrow. options-style methods retain function expressions (fmt-12). a callback whose body is only return , and whose arrow fits on one line, is written as that arrow — the mirror of flow-07. function stays for a callback that does more, or that needs this or arguments . items.filter(function (item) { return item_text(item) === text; }); ✗ no items.filter(v = item_text(v) === text); ✓ yes a filter callback that only returns, turned from a function into an arrow formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FLOW-12","code":"FLOW-12","name":"Names are written whole","text":"flow-12 names are written whole. a constant, function, method, class or property is never glued together from strings at run time; a search or a rename must find it. a value that picks one of a few known names lists them with match (php) or switch (javascript). a name built at run time only for an open set that cannot be listed. constant(invoice::class.'::status_'.strtoupper($status)) ✗ no match ($status) { ✓ yes 'paid' = invoice::status_paid, 'void' = invoice::status_void, } a search for status_paid misses a name glued at run time and finds it in a match that lists it whole names_written_whole.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE","code":"FILE","name":"File and module structure","text":"file file and module structure one entry function, one fixed order."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-01","code":"FILE-01","name":"A library module exports one public value last","text":"file-01 a library module exports one public value last. the value may be a function, class, configuration or data. declare it above and export its name at file end; tool-shaped files follow their tool. module.exports = items_index_by_uid; a library file: requires, the value, helpers, and module.exports of that one value last formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-02","code":"FILE-02","name":"A script runs main and exports nothing","text":"file-02 a script runs main and exports nothing. an executable script uses main as its entry function and does not export. a script that exports, turned into one that only runs: shebang, requires, cli(main), main formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-03","code":"FILE-03","name":"Library or executable, never both","text":"file-03 library or executable, never both. do not combine a library export and an executable invocation in the same file. a file that both exports and runs, split into a library and an executable formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-04","code":"FILE-04","name":"An executable hands main to cli","text":"file-04 an executable hands main to cli. an executable hands its entry function to cli . cli owns the process contract: it keeps the event loop alive until main settles, reports a failure, and exits non-zero — exitcodeerror picks the code, any other error exits 1. a browser page script has no cli ; it calls main(); (mp-10). const cli = require('@vbarbarosh/node-helpers/src/cli'); cli(main); async function main() { } main handed to cli, which keeps the loop alive, reports a failure and exits non-zero cli_main.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-05","code":"FILE-05","name":"cli(main), never cli(main())","text":"file-05 cli(main) , never cli(main()) . cli runs the function through promise.try , so a synchronous throw is reported like a rejection; an already-invoked main() is a promise, and promise.try rejects it with “expecting a function” — the app dies at startup. cli(main); ✓ right cli(main()); ✗ dies at startup cli(main()) hands cli a promise and dies at startup; cli(main) lets cli call main and report a throw cli_main.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-06","code":"FILE-06","name":"No hand-rolled catch and exit","text":"file-06 no hand-rolled catch and exit. a hand-rolled main().catch(...) with process.exit(1) does not exist. main().catch(function (error) { console.error(error); process.exit(1); }); ✗ does not exist a hand-written main().catch with process.exit(1), replaced by cli(main) cli_main.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-07","code":"FILE-07","name":"Nothing precedes the shebang","text":"file-07 nothing precedes the shebang. an executable script may start with a shebang; nothing may precede it. !/usr/bin/env node a comment above the shebang, moved below it so ! is line 1 formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-08","code":"FILE-08","name":"Imports sorted in byte order","text":"file-08 imports sorted in byte order. the import block is sorted as plain text lines in byte order — what vim’s :sort over the block, or lc_all=c sort , produces. require and import alike. const $ = require('jquery'); const vue = require('vue'); const axios = require('axios'); const cli = require('@vbarbarosh/node-helpers/src/cli'); const fs = require('fs'); const {promisify} = require('util'); six requires in the order they were added, sorted in byte order as lc_all=c sort does formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-09","code":"FILE-09","name":"The whole line is the sort key","text":"file-09 the whole line is the sort key. the whole line is the sort key — const or import included, not the module path and not the variable name. requires sorted by name ignoring case, re-sorted by the whole line imports_sorted.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-10","code":"FILE-10","name":"Byte order, not locale order","text":"file-10 byte order, not locale order. byte order, not locale order: @ and $ come before letters, uppercase before lowercase, and { after z — destructured imports land last. const vue = require('vue'); ← v before a const axios = require('axios'); const {promisify} = require('util'); ← { after z byte order: $ and @, then a to z, a to z, then {; a locale-sorted list re-sorted that way imports_sorted.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-11","code":"FILE-11","name":"Imports form one unbroken list","text":"file-11 imports form one unbroken list. the import block is one contiguous list: no groups for builtins, packages and local files, and no blank lines or comments inside it — either one breaks the sort. two exceptions: a side-effect import keeps its run order in its own block at the top, and the named imports ( {foo, bar} ) may stand apart after the default ones — one blank line between blocks. import './sass/main.sass'; import './main-pre'; import vue from 'vue'; import axios from 'axios'; import {mapstate} from 'vuex'; import {promisify} from 'util'; imports split by comments into groups, turned into one list with side-effect and named blocks imports_sorted.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-12","code":"FILE-12","name":"Constants follow the requires","text":"file-12 constants follow the requires. file-level constants and variables follow the require statements. const report_limit = 31; a constant above the requires, moved below them formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-13","code":"FILE-13","name":"cli(main) right after initialization","text":"file-13 cli(main) right after initialization. cli(main); comes immediately after all global initialization, and no executable code other than that call may appear before the public entry function. code run between cli(main) and main, turned into globals followed by cli(main) formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-14","code":"FILE-14","name":"The public entry function is the first function in the file","text":"file-14 the public entry function is the first function in the file. in an executable, the first function declaration is function main() . a helper above main, moved below so main is the first function formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-15","code":"FILE-15","name":"Helpers follow the entry function","text":"file-15 helpers follow the entry function. helpers are defined after the public entry function, and before a library’s final export. a library file: the entry function, its helpers, then the export formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-16","code":"FILE-16","name":"Helpers must be named functions, not inline lambdas","text":"file-16 helpers must be named functions, not inline lambdas. a helper written as an arrow in a constant, turned into a named function formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-17","code":"FILE-17","name":"This repository is CommonJS","text":"file-17 this repository is commonjs. this repository's own code and node scripts are commonjs: const = require(''); , never import or export . elsewhere the rules cover both module systems. import and export default, turned into require and module.exports in this repository formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-18","code":"FILE-18","name":"Libraries follow a fixed order","text":"file-18 libraries follow a fixed order. library order is fixed. requires → constants → public entry → helpers → module.exports the five parts of a library in order: requires, constants, entry, helpers, exports formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-19","code":"FILE-19","name":"Executables follow a fixed order","text":"file-19 executables follow a fixed order. executable order is fixed. shebang → requires → globals → cli(main); → main() → helpers the six parts of a script in order: shebang, requires, globals, cli(main), main, helpers formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-20","code":"FILE-20","name":"One library export, last, and the file bears its name","text":"file-20 one library export, last, and the file bears its name. a reusable/library module exports one public value; the file is named after that value. declare it above and export its name last: module.exports = name; or export default name; . never combine an es-module declaration with its export or export an inline expression. executable scripts export nothing; tool-shaped files follow the tool. the tool decides the export's shape, never the file's name (mp-53). // helper.js function helper() { return null; } export default helper; helper.js exporting a combined declaration or an inline expression, turned into helper declared, then exported by name one_export_per_file.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-21","code":"FILE-21","name":"No named exports; one public value per library module","text":"file-21 no named exports; one public value per library module. several independent public functions are several modules, each named after its export. one exported class can contain methods. several names are never taken from a local module. export {engine_run, engine_transport}; ✗ no import {engine_run, engine_transport} from './engine'; ✗ no import engine_run from './engine_run'; ✓ yes import engine_transport from './engine_transport'; engine.js with two named exports, split into engine_run.js exporting one value one_export_per_file.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-23","code":"FILE-23","name":"Reusable scratch and test modules export one value too","text":"file-23 reusable scratch and test modules export one value too. research, scratch and test-support modules are not exempt. executable scripts export nothing; tool-shaped files follow the tool. a test fixtures module exporting two values, turned into users_fixture.js exporting one one_export_per_file.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-24","code":"FILE-24","name":"Libraries and tools dictate their own shape","text":"file-24 libraries and tools dictate their own shape. a named import from a library is the library’s shape, and a file whose shape is dictated by a tool follows the tool — module.exports.mochahooks in a mocha hooks file. import {mapstate} from 'vuex'; vuex's named import and mocha's hooks file keep their tools' shape; a named import from a local module becomes one value one_export_per_file.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-25","code":"FILE-25","name":"New code takes its place from its surroundings where no explicit rule applies","text":"file-25 new code takes its place from its surroundings where no explicit rule applies. read the code around the insertion point — what stands together, in what order — and continue it within that scope. explicit rules take priority (core-01); this does not require reorganizing existing code. format_time appended after render_page, moved beside the other format_ functions new_code_placement.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"FILE-26","code":"FILE-26","name":"A function family stays unbroken","text":"file-26 a function family stays unbroken. a run of functions of one family — render_ , format_ , click_ — stays unbroken. a new member joins the run; a function of another family goes outside it, never between two members. render_css() describe() ✗ splits the run render_html() render_css() render_html() describe() ✓ after the run describe() between render_css and render_html, moved after the render run new_code_placement.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ","code":"PROJ","name":"Project layout","text":"proj project layout a directory is a program; bin/ holds its verbs."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-01","code":"PROJ-01","name":"One project shape; bin/ holds the verbs","text":"proj-01 one project shape; bin/ holds the verbs. every project keeps one shape. a directory is a program, and bin/ holds its methods — one executable per verb, working the same in every language. bin/build bb bin/configure bin/release bin/run rr bin/self-check bin/test tt bin/watch ww the bin/ folder with build, configure, release, run, self-check, test and watch, and the same verbs in a node.js, a python and a php project layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-02","code":"PROJ-02","name":"After git pull, run bin/configure","text":"proj-02 after git pull, run bin/configure. bin/configure is the only command to run after git pull to make a checkout ready for development. git pull, then bin/configure, then a checkout ready to work in; a list of remembered steps is replaced by that one command layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-03","code":"PROJ-03","name":"Run, test, build and watch wrap npm","text":"proj-03 run, test, build and watch wrap npm. in a node.js project run , test , build and watch are usually thin wrappers over npm. the wrappers still earn their keep: rr , tt , bb , ww are shorter than any npm form, and the names hold for projects that are not node.js. alias rr='bin/run' alias tt='bin/test' alias bb='bin/build' alias ww='bin/watch' rr, tt, bb and ww call bin/run, bin/test, bin/build and bin/watch, which in a node.js project call npm start, npm test, npm run build and npm run watch layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-04","code":"PROJ-04","name":"build/ is never committed; dist/ is the release","text":"proj-04 build/ is never committed; dist/ is the release. bin/build puts what it builds into build/ . build/ is temporary: it and everything in it are never committed. taking something out of it into the repository is a decision of its own, as bin/release does with dist/ . dist/ holds the released build, committed by bin/release at release time only. there is one build: bin/release runs it and copies build/ into dist/ . build/ bin/build output, temporary, never committed dist/ released build, committed by bin/release bin/build writes build/, never committed; bin/release runs the build and copies build/ into dist/, committed at release time only layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-05","code":"PROJ-05","name":"Runtime state in data/, never committed","text":"proj-05 runtime state in data/, never committed. data/ holds runtime state. never committed. data/logs/2026-08-24.txt the running app writes data/logs/2026-08-24.txt; .gitignore lists data/, so git never sees it layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-06","code":"PROJ-06","name":".env belongs to whoever runs the app","text":"proj-06 .env belongs to whoever runs the app. .env is not the application's: docker loads it, or the dockerfile sets the variables. the rules set nothing for it. docker loads .env, or the dockerfile sets the variables, into the environment; the app only reads process.env layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-07","code":"PROJ-07","name":"config/ turns the environment into configuration","text":"proj-07 config/ turns the environment into configuration. config/ is the one place of the application's configuration. config/index.js reads the environment variables, may load files, and gives the code its configuration ready to use, through one path. // config/index.js module.exports = make_config(process.env); const config = require('../config'); process.env and files go into config/index.js, which gives the code one configuration object, read everywhere through require of config layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-08","code":"PROJ-08","name":"src/ has a folder per part","text":"proj-08 src/ has a folder per part. src/ holds one subdirectory per part of the program. src/agent/ src/http/ src/android/ src/parser/ src/desktop-electron/ src/ holding one folder per part: agent, http, android, parser and desktop-electron layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-09","code":"PROJ-09","name":"Shared helpers go to src/helpers/","text":"proj-09 shared helpers go to src/helpers/. small shared functions go to src/helpers/ , one function per file. src/helpers/array_group.js src/helpers/ with array_group.js, array_index_by.js and string_slug.js, each holding the one function it is named after layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-10","code":"PROJ-10","name":"A unit test sits next to the file it tests","text":"proj-10 a unit test sits next to the file it tests. src/helpers/array_group.js src/helpers/array_group.test.js array_group.test.js beside array_group.js in src/helpers/, instead of far away in tests/unit/ layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-11","code":"PROJ-11","name":"tests/ drives the whole program","text":"proj-11 tests/ drives the whole program. tests/ holds suites that drive the whole program, such as playwright. a playwright suite in tests/ drives a browser, which drives the whole running app layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-12","code":"PROJ-12","name":"Keep the README short","text":"proj-12 keep the readme short. readme.md stays short: what the project is and how to start it. a short readme with what the project is and how to start it; a long readme of every detail is replaced by it and links to docs layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-13","code":"PROJ-13","name":"Docs in docs/, notes in notes/, images in img/","text":"proj-13 docs in docs/, notes in notes/, images in img/. docs/ holds the official documentation, notes/ holds working notes — hints and findings made during development — and img/ holds static images, mostly for the readme. docs/ holds the official documentation, notes/ the working notes made during development, img/ the static images, mostly for the readme layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-14","code":"PROJ-14","name":"The project root carries the same standard files","text":"proj-14 the project root carries the same standard files. .env is not one of them: it is there only when the runner puts it there (proj-06). .env.example .gitignore dockerfile license readme.md package.json the project root with .env.example, .gitignore, dockerfile, license, readme.md and package.json layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-15","code":"PROJ-15","name":"A script starts from bin/templ","text":"proj-15 a script starts from bin/templ. a new bash script is a copy of bin/templ with what it does not need thrown away. its header, strict mode included, and the script / scriptdir / scriptname lines always stay; the colors, the exit message, the temporary directory with its exit trap and the four echo lines stay only if the script uses them. !/bin/bash http://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html the-set-builtin http://redsymbol.net/articles/unofficial-bash-strict-mode set -o nounset -o errexit -o pipefail script= realpath $0 scriptdir= dirname $script scriptname= basename $script bin/templ: the header and the script, scriptdir and scriptname lines always stay; the colors, the exit message, the temporary directory and the echo lines only if the script needs them templ"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-16","code":"PROJ-16","name":"bin/configure asks everything up front","text":"proj-16 bin/configure asks everything up front. bin/configure asks every question it may have — the sudo password, a choice, a key — at the very start, before any work. once the work has started there are no more questions; it never stops halfway, waiting for a password. questions for a password, a choice and a key come first and the work runs to the end; a run that stops halfway to ask for a password is the wrong way configure.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-17","code":"PROJ-17","name":"bin/configure takes sudo once, up front","text":"proj-17 bin/configure takes sudo once, up front. bin/configure first works out what the run will need, predicting the state after the install. when a step will need sudo — an electron chrome-sandbox that is missing, not root:4755 , or about to be replaced by the install — it takes sudo -v up front, and the later calls are sudo -n , which fail at once rather than prompt. sandbox=node_modules/electron/dist/chrome-sandbox state= stat -c %u:%a $sandbox 2 /dev/null wanted=… electron in package-lock.json installed=… electron in node_modules if & 91; \"$wanted\" != \"$installed\" ] & 91; \"$state\" != \"root:4755\" ]; then sudo -v fi sudo -n chown root:root $sandbox later: never a prompt bin/configure predicts the state after the install, takes sudo -v once if a step will need it, and later calls sudo -n, which never prompts configure.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-18","code":"PROJ-18","name":"Everything kept lives in data/","text":"proj-18 everything kept lives in data/. data/ is the project's permanent data: a restart, a redeploy or a migration does not delete it. everything the program writes and has to keep lives under data/ , and nowhere else. configuration is not there: it comes from config/ and the environment. uploads, the database and logs written by the program all live under data/, which survives restarts, redeploys and migrations; configuration comes from config/ and the environment data.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-19","code":"PROJ-19","name":"Docker images carry code, not data","text":"proj-19 docker images carry code, not data. a docker image carries the code, never the data. one mount of data/ — a named volume or a host directory — is all it takes to keep it; moving the project is moving its data/ and running it with the same environment. docker run -v app-data:/app/data app docker run -v /srv/app/data:/app/data app an image with the code and a data/ volume mounted into the container; docker run -v app-data:/app/data app data.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-20","code":"PROJ-20","name":"The README runs in a fixed order","text":"proj-20 the readme runs in a fixed order. a github readme.md runs in one order: badges, cover image, name, short description, website, quick start, documentation, license. badges and the further documentation pages may be absent, and the website link is left out when the website is the documentation; the rest are always there. the parts of a readme, top to bottom readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-21","code":"PROJ-21","name":"Badges at the top, a row per kind","text":"proj-21 badges at the top, a row per kind. badges are optional. when there are any, they sit at the very top, above the cover, a row per kind: the package and its numbers on one row, the ci workflows on the next. as many as the project has. the top of a readme: a row of package badges, a row of ci badges, then the cover image readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-22","code":"PROJ-22","name":"The cover image lives in img/","text":"proj-22 the cover image lives in img/. the cover image is a file in img/ , shown with and a dark twin when the colours need one. <picture <source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"img/cover-dark.png\" <img alt=\"rules\" src=\"img/cover.png\" </picture the readme shows the cover through : img/cover.png in light, img/cover-dark.png in dark readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-23","code":"PROJ-23","name":"The repository name is the one heading","text":"proj-23 the repository name is the one heading. the name is the one heading, right under the cover: the name of the repository. rules a readme with the cover, then rules as its one level-one heading, then sections readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-24","code":"PROJ-24","name":"One description sentence everywhere","text":"proj-24 one description sentence everywhere. the short description is one sentence on its own line, and the same sentence is the repository's about text and the description of package.json . one sentence, written once, appears as the readme description, the github about text and the description of package.json readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-25","code":"PROJ-25","name":"A link with the text Website follows the description","text":"proj-25 a link with the text website follows the description. its address is the repository's about → website and the homepage of package.json . it is left out when the website is the documentation. website a website link under the description, with the same address as the github about website and the homepage field of package.json readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-26","code":"PROJ-26","name":"A quick start in a few lines","text":"proj-26 a quick start in a few lines. a quick start shows how to install and run the project, in a few lines. npx vbarbarosh/rules src a quick start of one line, npx vbarbarosh/rules src, that installs and runs the project readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-27","code":"PROJ-27","name":"Documentation opens with the full docs link","text":"proj-27 documentation opens with the full docs link. a documentation section opens with the link to the full documentation. further pages follow as a short list, each with what it covers. documentation full documentation: & 42;& 42;& 91;docs/rules.html](docs/rules.html)& 42;& 42; & 42; & 91;formatting](formatting.md) — the javascript spec a documentation section: the full documentation link first, then a short list of further pages with what each covers readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-28","code":"PROJ-28","name":"License is the last section","text":"proj-28 license is the last section. license is the last section: the license named and linked to license . license & 91;mit](license) the readme sections in order with license last, linking to the license file readme.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-29","code":"PROJ-29","name":"Demos live in demos/","text":"proj-29 demos live in demos/. small demos — a short runnable file that shows how something is used — live in demos/ , never in examples/ . demos/items_by.js ✓ right examples/items_by.js ✗ wrong examples/items_by.js is wrong; the same demo lives in demos/items_by.js layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"PROJ-30","code":"PROJ-30","name":"The build ends with the self-check","text":"proj-30 the build ends with the self-check. the last step of bin/build runs bin/self-check : the application's own check that it is fit to work, one probe per thing it needs, the programs it calls and what the build was to produce; a lit lamp names what to fix (test-09). a build that fails it ships nothing. bin/build, the last step bin/self-check [ffmpeg] ffmpeg version 6.1.1 [pyftsubset] zopfli: 4120 < 5312 bytes build/front.d: missing ✗ ships nothing bin/build ends with bin/self-check, whose probes find ffmpeg and pyftsubset but a missing build/front.d, so the build ships nothing layout.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL","code":"REL","name":"Packaging and release","text":"rel packaging and release ship a prebuilt dist/."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL-01","code":"REL-01","name":"A project can ship a prebuilt dist/","text":"rel-01 a project can ship a prebuilt dist/. a project can distribute a prebuilt dist/ from the repository, the way vue, vuerouter, vuex, jquery, bootstrap, axios, cuid and jsondiffpatch do. dist/vue.js in the repository, served by unpkg, loaded by a page with a script tag, with no build on the user side packaging.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL-02","code":"REL-02","name":"Release order: clean, bump, build, commit, tag","text":"rel-02 release order: clean, bump, build, commit, tag. the release workflow is fixed: ensure there are no changes, increase the version, update dist/ , commit, tag. dist/ is updated by the one build, then a copy of build/ (mp-32). test -z \"$(git status --porcelain)\" npm version $1 --no-git-tag-version rm -rf build npm run build rm -rf dist cp -r build dist git add package.json package-lock.json dist git commit -m \"release: v$(...)\" git tag v$(...) a release in five steps: no changes, bump the version, build and copy build/ into dist/, commit, tag packaging.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL-03","code":"REL-03","name":"The script commits and tags, not the bump","text":"rel-03 the script commits and tags, not the bump. bump the version without committing or tagging, then own both steps in the script. npm version $1 --no-git-tag-version npm version without its git tag bumps the version only; the release script then commits and tags packaging.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL-04","code":"REL-04","name":"One command does the whole release","text":"rel-04 one command does the whole release. bin/release major minor patch performs the whole release. bin/release patch bin/release patch runs the whole release: clean, bump, build, commit, tag packaging.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL-05","code":"REL-05","name":"A release script runs under bash strict mode","text":"rel-05 a release script runs under bash strict mode. set -o nounset -o errexit -o pipefail set -o nounset stops on an unset variable, errexit on a failing command, pipefail on a failure inside a pipe packaging.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"REL-06","code":"REL-06","name":"A bad argument prints usage to stderr and exits 1","text":"rel-06 a bad argument prints usage to stderr and exits 1. echo usage: release 'major& 124;minor& 124;patch' &2 exit 1 bin/release banana prints usage: release major/minor/patch to stderr and exits 1 packaging.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"GIT","code":"GIT","name":"Commits","text":"git commits a lowercase title, scope first; details below; 72 characters a line."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"GIT-01","code":"GIT-01","name":"A commit title is a scope, a colon and a short description","text":"git-01 a commit title is a scope, a colon and a short description. the scope is one or two words: the part of the project the commit changes, its category. maps: placement rules for gates and portals, and map-check the title maps: placement rules for gates and portals, and map-check, as a scope, a colon and a description commits.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"GIT-02","code":"GIT-02","name":"Commit titles are lowercase","text":"git-02 commit titles are lowercase. the title is all lowercase, names included. readme: elites and arcade saves as the code has them ✓ yes readme: elites and arcade saves ✗ no readme: elites and arcade saves is wrong; readme: elites and arcade saves as the code has them is right commits.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"GIT-03","code":"GIT-03","name":"A commit title is 72 characters at most","text":"git-03 a commit title is 72 characters at most. the whole title, scope included, stays within 72 characters. short is the rule; 72 is the ceiling, not a target. maps: one name for the gate jump reach 38 docs: build-docs and a github pages workflow 44 72 at most title lengths as bars against a line at 72 characters: 38 and 44 fit, 89 runs past it; the long one is cut to fit commits.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"GIT-04","code":"GIT-04","name":"A short body, only when needed","text":"git-04 a short body, only when needed. details go in a body after one blank line, only when they are really needed. a body is short, in ordinary sentences. docs: link the placement rules, gates and portals as they stand docs/readme.md lists placement.md; objects.md describes gates and portals as the placement rules now put them. a commit with a title, a blank line and a two-line body, beside a commit that needs no body commits.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"GIT-05","code":"GIT-05","name":"A body wraps at 72 characters","text":"git-05 a body wraps at 72 characters. every line of the body stays within 72 characters, as the title does, and breaks between words. a path, a url or a command longer than that keeps a line of its own, whole. docs: the agents page has a picture for each guide consequences first: the task, its four what-if questions and what each shapes. one example, all cases: only the shown dropdown fixed against every case found. a commit body wrapped at 72 characters, each line ending before a red line at column 72 commits.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG","code":"LOG","name":"Logs","text":"log logs one line, one event, four fields."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-01","code":"LOG-01","name":"One line, one event, four fields","text":"log-01 one line, one event, four fields. a log is one stream of lines, as if one infinite file; its files may be split by day (proj-05). one physical line records one event, in four fields and nothing else. [time][group_uid][sender] details a log line as its four fields, time, group_uid, sender and details, each with what it holds, above three such lines of one log logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-02","code":"LOG-02","name":"Measurements go in details","text":"log-02 measurements go in details. elapsed time, status and every other measurement belong in details , not in a field of their own. [cd8e5vqp][db_query_end_error] 30.001s etimedout the fields of a line with the measurements in details; a fifth bracketed field for 30.001s beside the same line with it in details logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-03","code":"LOG-03","name":"time is ISO-8601 UTC with milliseconds","text":"log-03 time is iso-8601 utc with milliseconds. 2026-08-17t16:09:20.185z an iso-8601 utc time split into date, t, time, milliseconds and z; a local time and an epoch each beside the iso form logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-04","code":"LOG-04","name":"Omit time when the runtime stamps it","text":"log-04 omit time when the runtime stamps it. include time when writing directly to a file; omit it when the runtime already timestamps the line, as docker does. & 91;2026-08-17t16:09:20.185z]& 91;cd8e5vqp]& 91;db_query_begin] name=select_users & 91;cd8e5vqp]& 91;db_query_begin] name=select_users a line written to a file carries its time; under docker, docker's timestamp stands before the app's line, and a doubled time beside the line written once logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-05","code":"LOG-05","name":"Every event carries its group uid","text":"log-05 every event carries its group uid. a group is one unit of work — a request, a job, a cron run. every event carries its group’s uid, an opaque string, usually a cuid. four lines of one request, each starting with its group uid cd8e5vqp; a line without a uid beside the same line with it logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-06","code":"LOG-06","name":"A group opens with group_spawn","text":"log-06 a group opens with group_spawn. the first event of a group is group_spawn , emitted synchronously before the uid is passed to other work or another event is emitted for the group. a root group omits parent= . & 91;new_group_uid]& 91;group_spawn] parent=old_group_uid & 91;c1a0root]& 91;group_spawn] a child group and a root group, each opening with group_spawn; a spawn after the first work beside the spawn first logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-07","code":"LOG-07","name":"The caller's uid becomes the parent","text":"log-07 the caller's uid becomes the parent. messages and requests carry the caller’s uid; the receiver uses it as the parent of its new group. a web group sends a job, the message carries its uid, and the worker's group_spawn names it as parent; a spawn without parent beside one with parent=aaa logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-08","code":"LOG-08","name":"A sender names one call site","text":"log-08 a sender names one call site. sender identifies one logging call site, and the name is unique in the whole codebase — a grep for it lands on one place. think of it as a const in a single namespace. a grep for a sender lands on one line of code; one sender in two places beside a name for each place logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-09","code":"LOG-09","name":"sender is snake_case","text":"log-09 sender is snake_case. ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_] $ the snake_case pattern of a sender; camelcase and hyphenated senders each beside db_query_begin logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-10","code":"LOG-10","name":"Every begin is closed by its end","text":"log-10 every begin is closed by its end. a _begin event is closed by the same stem with _end_ok or _end_error , in the same group. an end never appears without its begin in that group. & 91;c9k2vf3p]& 91;group_spawn] & 91;c9k2vf3p]& 91;mp4gif_worker_begin] & 91;c9k2vf3p]& 91;mp4gif_worker_end_ok] 4.512s & 91;c7t0xb1m]& 91;group_spawn] & 91;c7t0xb1m]& 91;mp4gif_worker_begin] & 91;c7t0xb1m]& 91;mp4gif_worker_end_error] 0.884s ffmpeg exit=1 a begin closed by end_ok and a begin closed by end_error; an end with no begin beside the begin and its end logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-11","code":"LOG-11","name":"A plain end when the outcome speaks","text":"log-11 a plain end when the outcome speaks. use _end when the logged outcome — an http status, say — already says what happened and classifying it as ok or error would mislead. an http request closed by a plain end with its 404; an end_error for the 404 beside the plain end logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-12","code":"LOG-12","name":"Spawn a group only when it pays","text":"log-12 spawn a group only when it pays. a group costs at least three lines. spawn one only when the work earns them: seconds of work, or many logging call sites. do not spawn a group for one short call. & 91;bbb]& 91;group_spawn] parent=aaa & 91;bbb]& 91;process_begin] & 91;bbb]& 91;process_end_ok] 0.000s ✗ noise three lines of a group spawned for one short call beside the two lines on the caller's group; a group earns its lines with seconds of work or many call sites logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-13","code":"LOG-13","name":"Log short work on the caller’s group, not a new one","text":"log-13 log short work on the caller’s group, not a new one. when nothing is reported between the two ends, record one event. & 91;aaa]& 91;process_begin] & 91;aaa]& 91;process_end_ok] 0.000s & 91;aaa]& 91;process_handled] 0.000s ✓ even better a group spawned for nothing beside the begin and end on the caller's group, and one process_handled line, even better logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-14","code":"LOG-14","name":"No lone end in a new group","text":"log-14 no lone end in a new group. two lines are the same noise when a new group holds a lone end. the matching begin was emitted on the parent — keep the pair there. & 91;bbb]& 91;group_spawn] parent=aaa & 91;bbb]& 91;process_end_ok] 0.000s ✗ noise an end that strayed into a new group beside the begin and end together on the parent logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-15","code":"LOG-15","name":"Details are terse, on one line","text":"log-15 details are terse, on one line. details are human-readable and terse, and never contain a physical newline. a details with a newline, its second line without fields, beside the same event on one terse line logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-16","code":"LOG-16","name":"No secrets or payloads in details","text":"log-16 no secrets or payloads in details. details never contain secrets, tokens, signed urls, url query values, bodies or headers. prefer a reference to the payload over the payload. a token, a signed url and a body each beside a reference that replaces it: a user uid, a file path, a size logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-17","code":"LOG-17","name":"Simple words bare, the rest JSON-quoted","text":"log-17 simple words bare, the rest json-quoted. a printable ascii word without spaces, double quotes, backslashes or square brackets is written bare — exactly the bytes that were sent, so it cannot span words, break the line, or mint a fake tag. every other string is json-quoted. name=select_users reason=\"db timeout\" value=\"\" a bare word and two json-quoted values with why; an unquoted value with a space and a bracketed value each beside its quoted form logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-18","code":"LOG-18","name":"Structures as compact JSON","text":"log-18 structures as compact json. arrays, objects, booleans and null use compact json. retry=true limits={\"soft\":10,\"hard\":25} retry and limits as compact json; spaced json and a yes for a boolean each beside the compact form logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-19","code":"LOG-19","name":"An unordered list is sorted and prefixed with its count","text":"log-19 an unordered list is sorted and prefixed with its count. ordered values are not sorted. 3:& 91;180,783,846] 2:& 91;\"bar\",\"foo\"] 0:& 91;] three lists turned into their sorted, counted form; a list in order of arrival beside 3:(180,783,846) logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-20","code":"LOG-20","name":"Truncate on UTF-8, count what is cut","text":"log-20 truncate on utf-8, count what is cut. truncation preserves valid utf-8 and ends with the number of omitted bytes. ...+4821 a long value cut and 4 821 bytes counted; a cut inside a character beside a whole one ending ...+4821 logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-21","code":"LOG-21","name":"Try to keep one log line under 2k UTF-8 bytes","text":"log-21 try to keep one log line under 2k utf-8 bytes. a log line describes an event; it is not payload storage. a 120-byte event under the 2 kb mark and a 48 000-byte payload far past it; the payload beside a counted, truncated line logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-22","code":"LOG-22","name":"group_exit is optional; nothing follows it","text":"log-22 group_exit is optional; nothing follows it. group_exit is optional and mainly serves to detect leaks. after it is emitted, no producer emits another event for that group; a parent may emit it for work it abandons. consumers must accept groups without it — its absence makes no claim. [group_uid][group_exit] a group closed by group_exit and a note that it is optional; an event after the exit beside the exit last logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LOG-23","code":"LOG-23","name":"After means emission order, not timestamps","text":"log-23 after means emission order, not timestamps. “after” group_exit means producer emission order, not timestamp or collector ingestion order. buffering may deliver an older event later; that alone is not an error. without a causal-order signal — preserved per-group order, a shared sequence — cross-process detection is advisory. two events emitted in one order and received in the other; judging by arrival beside judging by emission logs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS","code":"CSS","name":"Classes and styles","text":"css classes and styles a fixed class order; every class has a rule."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-01","code":"CSS-01","name":"Utilities first, app classes next, local classes last","text":"css-01 utilities first, app classes next, local classes last. classes inside one class=\"…\" attribute keep a fixed order. plain utilities lead; an app-prefixed class ( vb- , np- — one prefix per app, app- in these examples) sorts after all of them, base class included; a file-local - class is always last. layout spacing sizing decoration typography app-prefix local <div class=\"flex-row-c -root\" / <div class=\"fluid p30 xpt app-scrollbars-light -grid\" / <div class=\"db w80 h80 br4 fit-cover cur-pointer app-background -preset-img\" / <div class=\"flex-row-cl gap10 app-font-b14 -title-trigger\" / the class order as chips, from layout to local, and an example class list labelled by family; local first beside utilities, app, local css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-02","code":"CSS-02","name":"Item class leads; gap closes the layout","text":"css-02 item class leads; gap closes the layout. within the utilities: an item class ( fluid , grow , shrink , flex-fluid , flex-grow , flex-nogrow , flex-shrink , flex-noshrink , flex-static ) or a display or position utility ( db , abs ) leads where the element carries one; a gap closes the layout group — every flex , grid or split class first, then the gap; grid and flex never share an element; ph precedes pv , and spacing comes before typography. fluid flex-row gap10 oa flex-row flex-align-center gap10 mt10 flex-col-c gap15 grid-foo gap5 fluid flex-row gap10 oa and flex-row flex-align-center gap10 mt10 as labelled chips; gap first and flex with grid each beside its right order css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-03","code":"CSS-03","name":"No class without a rule","text":"css-03 no class without a rule. every class either has a rule in the file’s own style block or is an established project utility. an element that needs no styling stays classless — a label-only class rots and sends the next reader looking for a rule nobody wrote. <!-- the wrapper is not styled, so it carries no class -- <div <div class=\" -funnel-bars\" ...</div </div a wrapper div without a class around a styled -funnel-bars and its rule; a -wrapper with no rule beside the classless wrapper css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-04","code":"CSS-04","name":"Gap for flex and grid, mg and mi for blocks","text":"css-04 gap for flex and grid, mg and mi for blocks. gap spaces the children of a grid or flex container; mg and mi are the block-container forms. the two do not mix. a flex row spaced by gap10 and a block spaced by mg10; mg10 on a flex row beside gap10 css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-05","code":"CSS-05","name":"Fluid in a split, flex-fluid in a flex","text":"css-05 fluid in a split, flex-fluid in a flex. fluid marks the filling child of an hsplit / vsplit (whose children default to flex: none ); flex-fluid marks the growing child of a plain flex-row / flex-col . same css — the choice states which container the element sits in. a fluid child filling an hsplit and a flex-fluid child growing in a flex-row; flex-fluid in a split beside fluid css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-06","code":"CSS-06","name":"Broad before narrow","text":"css-06 broad before narrow. an x class usually comes before the setter it clears the way for — the broad class leads and the narrow one corrects it, whichever of the two is the reset. <div class=\"xm ml5\" / <!-- margin: 0; margin-left: 5px; -- <div class=\"m5 xml\" / <!-- margin: 5px; margin-left: 0; -- xm ml5 and m5 xml with the margins each produces; the reset after the setter beside the reset first css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-07","code":"CSS-07","name":"Rows and columns are a table","text":"css-07 rows and columns are a table. rows and columns are a , not a stack of flex rows — the columns line up only until one cell is wider. flex rows stay for content that is genuinely not tabular. a stack of flex rows whose columns drift beside a table whose columns line up, and their code css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-08","code":"CSS-08","name":"Imports and includes come first","text":"css-08 imports and includes come first. @import comes first in a block; @include comes first in a rule, ahead of the property declarations — the mixin brings the base, the block overrides it. . -card @include app-transition(box-shadow, border-color) box-sizing: border-box a rule with the include after a declaration beside the same rule with the include first css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-09","code":"CSS-09","name":"Transitions come from the mixin","text":"css-09 transitions come from the mixin. a transition: property is not written by hand — @include app-transition(props…) fills it, expanding each property into at the shared default speed. app-transition-fast and app-transition-debug are the same mixin at the shared fast and debug speeds. list the exact properties that change, never all , and put the include on the base rule rather than the state variant, so the animation covers both directions. the mixin is sass; plain .css files are not checked for it. @include app-transition(box-shadow, border-color) the transition mixin expanding into each property at the shared speed; transition: all by hand beside the mixin with exact properties css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-10","code":"CSS-10","name":"Five families: layout, spacing, sizing, decoration, typography","text":"css-10 five families: layout, spacing, sizing, decoration, typography. what each family holds: layout is the flex/grid/split container and its flex- modifiers; spacing is p / m ; sizing is w / h / max-w / min-h ; decoration is br , fit-cover , cur-pointer and the other shape and surface utilities; typography is fs fw lh , then alignment and nowrap . the five families, layout, spacing, sizing, decoration and typography, with examples; a mixed-up class list beside the family order css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-11","code":"CSS-11","name":"A header aligns like its column","text":"css-11 a header aligns like its column. a table header is aligned the way its column's data is: centered data, centered header; right-aligned data, right-aligned header. a table with a centered header over right-aligned numbers beside one whose header aligns as its column css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-12","code":"CSS-12","name":"One selector per line","text":"css-12 one selector per line. a selector list puts each selector on its own line, the comma closing the line; the brace follows the last one. .docs th, .docs td { ✗ no .docs th, .docs td { ✓ yes two selectors on one line beside one selector a line, the comma closing each css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"CSS-13","code":"CSS-13","name":"One blank line between rules","text":"css-13 one blank line between rules. rules stand one blank line apart: each closing brace is followed by one empty line before the next selector — never two, never none. .hidden { display: none !important; } .intro { ✗ no blank line .hidden { display: none !important; } .intro { ✓ one blank line two rules with no blank line between them beside the same rules one blank line apart css_classes.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL","code":"SQL","name":"SQL queries","text":"sql sql queries mysql: upper-case keywords; the query laid out like code."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL-01","code":"SQL-01","name":"A long query opens and closes on its own line","text":"sql-01 a long query opens and closes on its own line. a query that spans lines opens its template literal with a line break and closes it on a line of its own; the query is indented one level deeper than the call that runs it. await knex.raw( update user_identities ... ); a query glued to its knex.raw call beside the query opening and closing on its own lines, indented one level sql.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL-02","code":"SQL-02","name":"Keywords upper case, names lower case","text":"sql-02 keywords upper case, names lower case. every keyword, type and function is upper case; tables, columns, indexes and aliases stay lower case, spelled as the schema spells them. select min(id) as id from user_identities ✓ yes select min(id) as id from user_identities ✗ no a query's tokens as keywords and names; an all-lower-case query beside keywords upper case and names lower case sql.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL-03","code":"SQL-03","name":"Each clause on its own line","text":"sql-03 each clause on its own line. the dialect is mysql. at the top level of a statement, each clause starts a line of its own — select , from , every join , update , set , create unique index , on , where , alter table , drop index — and what the clause takes goes on the next line, indented four spaces. a keyword of several words stays on one line. create unique index user_identities_primary_user_id_unique on user_identities (primary_user_id) drop index user_identities_primary_user_id_unique on user_identities a create unique index whose clauses run on beside each clause on its line with its operand indented under it sql.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL-04","code":"SQL-04","name":"A subquery body is indented between its parentheses","text":"sql-04 a subquery body is indented between its parentheses. a subquery opens with ( at the end of the line that uses it; its body is indented one level, and ) closes it on a line of its own at that line's indent, followed by the alias. where id in ( select id from ( select min(id) as id ... ) as oldest ) a flat nested subquery beside the same subquery indented a level per pair of parentheses sql.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL-05","code":"SQL-05","name":"Short expressions may stay on one line","text":"sql-05 short expressions may stay on one line. a short expression can stay on one line: a where that opens a subquery, even at the top level, and the clauses inside a subquery. where id in ( select id from ( select min(id) as id from user_identities a where split to the word beside short expressions kept on one line inside the subquery sql.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"SQL-06","code":"SQL-06","name":"One ALTER TABLE action per clause","text":"sql-06 one alter table action per clause. each action of an alter table is a clause of its own, its operand indented under it; the comma between two actions ends the first operand's line. a column definition stays whole on its line. alter table user_identities drop index user_identities_primary_user_id_unique, drop column primary_user_id an alter table with its actions run together beside one action per clause, each operand indented sql.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE","code":"VUE","name":"Vue 2","text":"vue vue 2 components that behave like the platform."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-01","code":"VUE-01","name":"Every component is provided with three global methods through a mixin","text":"vue-01 every component is provided with three global methods through a mixin. px(value) // format a pixel value uid(& 91;name]) // ids unique across components emit_input(value) // return a value from an input one globals mixin hands px, uid and emit_input to every component vue-globals.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-02","code":"VUE-02","name":"Ids come from uid","text":"vue-02 ids come from uid. generated ids are bound to the current element and are what id and label[for] use. the name uid was chosen so it does not conflict with the id attribute every element may carry. uid: function (name) { return name ? c${this.& 95;uid}& 95;${name} : c${this.& 95;uid} ; }, the component's _uid 42 and uid('email') make the id c42_email, used by both the label and the input vue-globals.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-03","code":"VUE-03","name":"Spell out v-on and v-bind","text":"vue-03 spell out v-on and v-bind . the shorthands @ and : are not used. the shorthands :src and @click beside the same element written with v-bind:src and v-on:click vue-formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-04","code":"VUE-04","name":"Attributes keep one order","text":"vue-04 attributes keep one order. ref v-if v-for v-on v-bind:ref v-bind [...] class type placeholder title the ten attributes in their order, ref first and title last, and an input element written in that order vue-formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-05","code":"VUE-05","name":"Lift v-if off a v-for","text":"vue-05 lift v-if off a v-for. avoid v-if and v-for on the same component — lift the condition to a . <template v-if=\"items\" <div v-for=\"item in items\" v-bind:key=\"item.uid\" {{ item.title }} </div </template a div carrying both v-for and v-if beside the condition lifted to a template around the loop vue-formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-06","code":"VUE-06","name":"Compound directive values in parentheses","text":"vue-06 compound directive values in parentheses. a simple directive value stays bare; a compound one is wrapped in parentheses. every directive — v-if , v-else-if , v-show , v-on , v-bind . simple is a single identifier, member access, method call, template literal or unary negation; compound is anything carrying a binary operator or a ternary. only in a template: inside an attribute's quotes a compound value is easy to miss, and the parentheses show it. the same value moved into script, a computed or a method, loses them and follows fmt-27. <template v-if=\"is_ready\" <template v-if=\"(is_loading_fonts & 124;& 124; is_loading_images)\" <div v-bind:class=\"(compact ? 'narrow' : 'wide')\" / an operator and a ternary left bare beside the same values in parentheses, and a simple value wrapped beside it bare vue-formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-07","code":"VUE-07","name":"An input works like a native input","text":"vue-07 an input works like a native input. anything which takes data from a user is called an input and must work as a normal input element. 1. takes data from the value property 2. emits an input event 3. respects the disabled state 4. respects the readonly attribute 5. reacts to clicks on the associated label 6. reacts to mouse hover from that label 7. injects and uses app_form_input_id 8. takes focus with the tab key app-input-email takes value, disabled and readonly, emits input, and answers a click on its label and the tab key like an input vue-input.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-08","code":"VUE-08","name":"Avoid generic inputs","text":"vue-08 avoid generic inputs. avoid generic inputs — those with a lot of properties like min , max , step . one app-input-number with min, max, step and more beside app-input-percent, app-input-px and app-input-year vue-input.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-09","code":"VUE-09","name":"Forms submit by button and Enter","text":"vue-09 forms submit by button and enter. compose forms from app-form-div and app-label , inside a whose submit the submit button and the enter key both reach. <form v-on:submit.prevent=\"submit\" class=\"mg10\" <app-form-div class=\"flex-row gap10\" <app-label email:</app-label <app-input-email v-model=\"user.email\" / </app-form-div </form a form whose field (enter) and submit button both reach its one submit handler vue-form.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-10","code":"VUE-10","name":"Cancel and submit close the form","text":"vue-10 cancel and submit close the form. a form’s buttons close the form: cancel returns false through vue-modal’s modal.return , submit is a type=\"submit\" button. <app-button-orange v-on:click=\"modal.return(false)\" cancel </app-button-orange <app-button-green type=\"submit\" submit </app-button-green a rename file form: cancel returns false through modal.return and closes; submit is a type submit button that saves and closes vue-form.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-11","code":"VUE-11","name":"One button, one class","text":"vue-11 one button, one class. a button assembled from btn btn-green btn-lg beside one class, button-green vue-button.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-12","code":"VUE-12","name":"A slider thumb moves from 0 to 100%","text":"vue-12 a slider thumb moves from 0 to 100%. a slider is usually a container and a thumb. prefer positioning the thumb with top or left from 0 to 100% — 0 means empty, 100% means full. left: 0 // empty left: 100% // full three sliders with the thumb at left 0, 50% and 100%: empty, half and full vue-slider.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-13","code":"VUE-13","name":"One SVG icon per file","text":"vue-13 one svg icon per file. each svg icon goes into its own svg-icon- .vue file, containing only a template element with an svg inside. <template <svg viewbox=\"0 0 100 100\" xmlns=\"http& 58;//www& 46;w3.org/2000/svg\" <path fill=\"currentcolor\" d=\"...\" / </svg </template a folder of svg-icon- .vue files and the one file content: a template with an svg whose path fills currentcolor vue-svg-icon.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-14","code":"VUE-14","name":"Icons use currentColor","text":"vue-14 icons use currentcolor. svg icons respect the currentcolor css property. the same archive icon in a plain, an accent and a danger button, taking each button text colour vue-svg-icon.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-15","code":"VUE-15","name":"Component options keep one order","text":"vue-15 component options keep one order. props inject provide mixins data computed watch methods mounted created beforedestroy the eleven component options in their order, from props to beforedestroy vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-16","code":"VUE-16","name":"Name a handler by event and button text","text":"vue-16 name a handler by event and button text. an event handler is named after the ui part it is bound to — the event, then the button text. <button v-on:click=\"click_approve\" approve </button the approve button handled by click_approve; handle_click beside it, saying nothing of the button vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-17","code":"VUE-17","name":"A handler on an icon is named after the icon","text":"vue-17 a handler on an icon is named after the icon. <button v-on:click=\"click_icon_archive\" <svg-icon-archive / </button an archive icon button handled by click_icon_archive; click_button beside it, naming no button vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-18","code":"VUE-18","name":"Rename the handler with its label","text":"vue-18 rename the handler with its label. after the text or icon is changed, rename the corresponding event handler. approve → accept // click_approve → click_accept approve with click_approve; after the label becomes accept, click_approve left stale beside click_accept vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-19","code":"VUE-19","name":"Empty elements close themselves","text":"vue-19 empty elements close themselves. an element without a body closes itself — a component, an html element and a void element alike. this holds in .vue files and string templates; an in-dom template is parsed by the browser first. <th </th ✗ no <th / ✓ yes <app-input-email v-model=\"user.email\" / an empty th, an empty component and a bare br left open, each beside its self-closing form vue-formatting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-20","code":"VUE-20","name":"A piece shown in two places is a component","text":"vue-20 a piece shown in two places is a component. whatever the screen shows in more than one place, a badge most often, is a vue component of its own, used in each place. never the same markup and classes copied: a change then reaches some copies and misses the others. <span class=\"flex-row-cl gap5 br999 -reply\" ✗ no in progress {{ detail }} </span <badge-in-progress v-bind:detail=\"detail\" / ✓ yes one badge-in-progress component used in the list and in the detail, beside markup copied into each place vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-21","code":"VUE-21","name":"Named kind first","text":"vue-21 named kind first. a component is named - , as icons are svg-icon- . sorted by name, every component of a kind sits together. badge-filing badge-in-progress badge-no-reply components named what-kind scatter when sorted; named kind-what, each kind sorts together vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-22","code":"VUE-22","name":"What changes is a prop","text":"vue-22 what changes is a prop. what differs from place to place goes in as a prop: a count, a time, a plan's progress. the component holds the rest. <badge-new v-bind:count=\"3\" / // 3 new <badge-in-progress detail=\"for 4 h\" / badge-new with count 3 and 12 and badge-in-progress with a detail, each rendering its own badge vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-23","code":"VUE-23","name":"A variant is a suffix","text":"vue-23 a variant is a suffix. a smaller or otherwise different form is a component of its own, named with a suffix; never a second copy written by hand where it is needed. badge-in-progress → badge-in-progress-sm badge-in-progress and its smaller badge-in-progress-sm; a badge shrunk by a class at one place beside the variant vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"VUE-24","code":"VUE-24","name":"A kind shares one look through one mixin","text":"vue-24 a kind shares one look through one mixin. what every component of a kind shares, its shape, size and type, comes from one sass mixin; each component sets only its own colours. . -root @include app-badge background: var(--accent-soft) color: var(--accent) the app-badge mixin gives shape, size and type to badge-new, badge-done and badge-waiting, each with its own colours vue-components.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI","code":"UI","name":"UI appearance","text":"ui ui appearance how a screen looks."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-01","code":"UI-01","name":"Every screen has a light/dark switch","text":"ui-01 every screen has a light/dark switch. an app screen, a report, a standalone page: each shows a switch the reader can click. following the os setting alone does not count. one exception: a page made only to be photographed for a rule's picture keeps no switch. an app screen, a report and a standalone page, each with a theme switch; following the os setting alone beside a switch on every screen theme_switch.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-02","code":"UI-02","name":"Two states, one shared icon button","text":"ui-02 two states, one shared icon button. show the current theme: sun for light, crescent for dark. use img/theme-sun.svg and img/theme-moon.svg ; size and border may vary. click toggles directly, with no menu, system or auto option. sun: light → crescent: dark → sun: light one button cycling sun, crescent, sun with each click; a light, dark, system menu beside it theme_switch.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-03","code":"UI-03","name":"The dark theme redefines the light colors","text":"ui-03 the dark theme redefines the light colors. the light colors live on :root ; the dark theme sets the same variables under :root[data-theme=\"dark\"] . :root { --color-bg: ffffff; --color-text: 1b1f23; } :root& 91;data-theme=\"dark\"] { --color-bg: 15181c; --color-text: e6e8eb; } :root sets --color-bg and --color-text; :root(data-theme=\"dark\") sets the same names to other values theme_switch.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-04","code":"UI-04","name":"The theme is set before the page shows","text":"ui-04 the theme is set before the page shows. the page reads the saved choice from localstorage (the os setting once, when nothing is saved), puts it on as data-theme , and saves each new choice; storage access sits in try / catch . document.documentelement.dataset.theme = theme; the page reads localstorage and sets data-theme on html before the first paint; painting light then switching flashes theme_switch.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-05","code":"UI-05","name":"The switch stays in reach on a long page","text":"ui-05 the switch stays in reach on a long page. when the header scrolls away, a copy of the switch appears at the right end of the sticky bar. at the top the switch is in the header; scrolled, a copy rides at the right end of the sticky bar theme_switch.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-06","code":"UI-06","name":"Every project page opens with the same header","text":"ui-06 every project page opens with the same header. the project on the left; github and the theme switch on the right; search and section links between them, the search lined up with the content column. the parts of a page header, left to right page_header.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-07","code":"UI-07","name":"Left: icon, name, version","text":"ui-07 left: icon, name, version. the project's icon when it has one, its name, and its released version. & 91;icon] authwall docs v1.16.0 a header with the icon, the name authwall docs and the released version v1.16.0; an unreleased version beside it page_header.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-08","code":"UI-08","name":"Right: the links, then the theme switch, always last","text":"ui-08 right: the links, then the theme switch, always last. the github icon links to the repository; other links, such as twitter or facebook, sit beside it. however many links a project has, the switch stays at the right edge, the same on every page. the icons look alike: one size, no frame on any of them. & 91;github] & 91;twitter] & 91;current theme icon] headers with two links and with one, the theme switch last at the right edge in both page_header.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-21","code":"UI-21","name":"A table keeps its columns while they fit","text":"ui-21 a table keeps its columns while they fit. with room on the screen every column stays on top, and the table is read at a glance, without a click. only a table that turns into a grid — cells wrapping over several lines, words or paths broken mid-word, columns cut off — folds columns into its rows' details, and only as many as it must, at that width: the same table keeps more columns on a wide screen than on a phone. on a wide screen a table folded down to name and state beside the same table with every column on top; at phone width a table broken mid-word into a grid beside the same rows with name and state, one opened to show what folded table_details.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-22","code":"UI-22","name":"What a row is and its state never fold","text":"ui-22 what a row is and its state never fold. the columns that tell what a row is and what state it is in always stay on top. the others fold one at a time, the least needed first, until the rest fit on one line; a column the reader compares across rows folds last. wide: logs ~/logs.sock vps1 ● ready phone: ▸ logs ● ready table_details.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UI-23","code":"UI-23","name":"A click opens what folded, below the row","text":"ui-23 a click opens what folded, below the row. a click on the row opens the folded columns right under it, as wide as the table: one line each, its label and its value; a long value wraps there whole. only what folded is there, never a repeat of what the row shows; a table with nothing folded has no click and no chevron. another click closes them; the row clicked stays where it was (uim-01). ▾ charts-on-the-other-side ● ready listens on laptop · ~/charts.sock carried to vps1 · 127.0.0.1:41941 last failure econnreset, 10:42 table_details.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM","code":"UIM","name":"UI mechanics","text":"uim ui mechanics how a screen works."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-01","code":"UIM-01","name":"Nothing jumps","text":"uim-01 nothing jumps. in a list or a grid, the item the reader works with, the one selected or being looked at, stays where it is on the screen while the app adds, removes or grows items above, below or beside it — a new message, a summary that finishes loading; the scroll position adjusts instead, as far as the list's ends allow. a control that docks or hides as the page scrolls is outside this rule. a summary grows above; on the left the message being read drops, on the right it stays ▶ try it scroll_anchoring.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-02","code":"UIM-02","name":"Keep the place yourself; the browser won't","text":"uim-02 keep the place yourself; the browser won't. before such a change, note the element in the middle of the view and where it is on the screen; after the change, scroll by however far it moved. the browser keeps the place only when the change is entirely above the view: not at the very top, not when the growing block is itself on screen, not after a re-render. const keep = scroll_keep(scroller); await keep(async function () { summary.text = text; await vm.$nexttick(); }); a block above grows: alone the browser lets the line being read move 120 px; scroll_keep holds it at 0 px ▶ try it scroll_anchoring.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-03","code":"UIM-03","name":"Test it","text":"uim-03 test it. for each kind of change, a test notes where the middle element is, makes the change, and checks it moved 0 px — at the top of the view and in the middle. one more check runs without the fix and must see the jump. assert.equal(shift, 0); the test notes the middle element, makes the change, measures and asserts 0 px; without the fix it must see the jump scroll_anchoring.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-04","code":"UIM-04","name":"A file input also takes a drop","text":"uim-04 a file input also takes a drop. anything that lets the user pick files also takes files dragged onto it; anything that lets them pick a whole folder also takes a folder dragged onto it. a file field and a folder field: each picked with its button, or dragged onto it file_drop.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-05","code":"UIM-05","name":"The drop takes what the dialog takes","text":"uim-05 the drop takes what the dialog takes. the same file types, one or many; a whole folder when the dialog picks folders, read file by file. a dropped file goes down the same path as a picked one. file_drop_bind(element, input, take); // the input says what the dialog takes for (const entry of entries) { if (entry.isfile input.webkitdirectory) { await entry_files_read(entry, files); } } take(files_as_dialog_takes(files.length ? files : & 91;...event.datatransfer.files], input)); a picked and a dropped file both go through files_as_dialog_takes to take(files) file_drop.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-06","code":"UIM-06","name":"Show the drop target; never lose the page","text":"uim-06 show the drop target; never lose the page. the element lights up while files are dragged over it, and a file dropped beside it does not open in the tab. window.addeventlistener('dragover', v = v.preventdefault()); window.addeventlistener('drop', v = v.preventdefault()); a drop zone lit while dragging and a file dropped beside it ignored, beside a tab that opens the file file_drop.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-07","code":"UIM-07","name":"Trust the user: no \"Are you sure?\"","text":"uim-07 trust the user: no \"are you sure?\". delete, rename, move, cancel: it happens at once, and the user keeps working. not a dialog on every delete, but done at once with undo no_confirmations.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-08","code":"UIM-08","name":"Undo instead","text":"uim-08 undo instead. a deleted thing goes to a trash; a rename, an edit or a move can be undone. the way back is right there after the action. deleted · undo a delete confirmation and a rename with no way back, beside deleted · undo and renamed · undo no_confirmations.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-09","code":"UIM-09","name":"Confirm only what cannot be undone and costs a lot","text":"uim-09 confirm only what cannot be undone and costs a lot. closing an account, erasing data for good. even then, not an ok/cancel box but a deliberate step: typing the name, or a link sent by email. an ok/cancel box for closing an account beside typing the account name to close it no_confirmations.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-10","code":"UIM-10","name":"Use optimistic updates; the user keeps working","text":"uim-10 use optimistic updates; the user keeps working. keep the backend snapshot immutable and local pending changes separate. show the local change and send its update immediately. after acknowledgement, reread the backend, replace the snapshot, and clear only the pending change confirmed by that result; preserve later actions and reject stale refreshes. show the change where the user made it, at once, marked as waiting, whatever route it takes inside. keep failures visible as unsynchronized and retry safely; if only the reread failed, retry that step. read → local overlay → update → acknowledgement → refresh → replace snapshot → clear confirmed overlay send is pressed: on the left the comment is nowhere for 2 seconds, then it shows; on the right it shows at once marked sending, and the mark goes when the server has it optimistic_updates.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-11","code":"UIM-11","name":"Typing focuses the main search, first character included","text":"uim-11 typing focuses the main search, first character included. on a page with one primary search/filter, typing outside editable fields focuses it immediately and enters the first character too. local inputs retain their typing; respect shortcuts and native keyboard behavior. click background → type sm → main filter contains sm typing s then m outside a field puts sm in the main filter; a local field keeps its own typing main_search.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-12","code":"UIM-12","name":"Select the first actionable item by default","text":"uim-12 select the first actionable item by default. in a list of action targets, the first visible actionable item in displayed order is selected by default; enter acts on it. an explicit user selection wins. a new filtered list defaults to its first actionable item; an empty list has no action. sqlite, linkedin → enter opens sqlite sqlite, first in the list, is selected, so enter opens it; nothing selected beside it default_list_item.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-13","code":"UIM-13","name":"Show only what works","text":"uim-13 show only what works. a control the app already knows will fail is not drawn as if it would work: an expired option, the button of an ended chat, an action the user has no right to. nobody presses a button to be told \"sorry, this has expired\". a support chat: on the left end chat answers sorry, this option has timed out; on the right the expired options are gone and the reason is shown dead_controls.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-14","code":"UIM-14","name":"Ask the action first: a dry run","text":"uim-14 ask the action first: a dry run. every action can answer \"would it work now?\" without doing anything. the screen asks before drawing the control, and again whenever the answer can change; a button that expires on screen changes at that moment, not on the next click. can_end_chat(chat) → false → no end chat button can_end_chat(chat) is asked before drawing: true shows end chat, false draws none; a button that says sorry after the click beside it dead_controls.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"UIM-15","code":"UIM-15","name":"No means hidden, or disabled with the reason on it","text":"uim-15 no means hidden, or disabled with the reason on it. hide what nobody would look for there; disable what the user expects in its place, with the reason written on it or beside it. never a control whose only answer comes after the click. expired yesterday at 10:51 a control hidden where nobody looks for it, or disabled with expired yesterday at 10:51 beside it; a sorry after the click beside that dead_controls.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC","code":"DOC","name":"Writing the rules","text":"doc writing the rules how a document in this repo is built."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-01","code":"DOC-01","name":"A document has two halves","text":"doc-01 a document has two halves. the author writes the top half, compressed around what he already knows; the ai writes the bottom half, restating it for a reader who lacks that context. a document as two stacked halves: the author's compressed notes on top, the ai's restated explanation below, each written by one of them writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-02","code":"DOC-02","name":"Author sources govern the explanation","text":"doc-02 author sources govern the explanation. read the top half and applicable manually approved rules. an approved entry may explicitly clarify older wording within its scope; if the sources disagree without such a decision, ask before regenerating. the author's half and the approved rules feed the ai, which writes the explanation; when they disagree with no approved entry, the ai asks first writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-03","code":"DOC-03","name":"No AI patches the top half","text":"doc-03 no ai patches the top half. the one exception is an explicit request from the author. an ai rewording of the author's line, wrong, beside the line kept word for word; the one exception is the author asking for it writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-04","code":"DOC-04","name":"Regenerate the bottom half from both sources, never edit it","text":"doc-04 regenerate the bottom half from both sources, never edit it. after the top half or an applicable manually approved rule changes, regenerate the explanation from both. preserve approved decisions and their scope. a change on top leads to the whole bottom half written again from both sources; patching one line of the bottom is wrong writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-05","code":"DOC-05","name":"Usually a heading starts the bottom half","text":"doc-05 usually a heading starts the bottom half. most of the time the bottom half starts with a heading, and that heading is the boundary. a --- line or a closing fence before it is decoration. there is no strict marker. --- ✨ ai-generated content below ✨ --- the author's notes, a decorative --- line, then the heading that is the real boundary of the ai's half writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-06","code":"DOC-06","name":"One audit, one file; its register is the issue list","text":"doc-06 one audit, one file; its register is the issue list. the report of a full audit is one file, notes/audit-.md . the projects have no issue tracker: the finding register of the newest audit note is the issue list, and later audits refer to findings by their ids. notes/audit-2026-09-02.md the notes/ folder of audit files, the newest one's finding register serving as the issue list of ids audit_note.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-07","code":"DOC-07","name":"Audit notes keep one section order","text":"doc-07 audit notes keep one section order. an audit note keeps one order: title, repository line, scope, verdict with a health-at-a-glance table, finding register, findings, what is good, recommended order of work, status of prior findings, file inventory. the ten sections of an audit note in their fixed order, from the title to the file inventory audit_note.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-08","code":"DOC-08","name":"Scope says what ran and what did not","text":"doc-08 scope says what ran and what did not. the scope lists every check actually run, and what was not exercised. a check which was not run is said so; it is never implied. the checks that ran listed beside the one that did not, with the reason it was not run audit_note.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-09","code":"DOC-09","name":"Findings have permanent ids and evidence","text":"doc-09 findings have permanent ids and evidence. a finding id is -nn , never reused and never renumbered. each finding is one section citing file:line and the evidence — the quoted lines, the command which was run and what it printed. a finding in the author's own text is reported only; it is not patched. demo-01 classes banned in one file, shown in another (high) a finding with its id, file and line, quoted code and the command with its output; reusing an old id is wrong, the next one is new audit_note.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-10","code":"DOC-10","name":"Audit notes have no top half and stay untracked","text":"doc-10 audit notes have no top half and stay untracked. an audit note is written by the ai as a whole, so it has no top half and no separator. it is left untracked; it is committed only on request. an audit note written whole by the ai, shown untracked by git status, committed only on request audit_note.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-11","code":"DOC-11","name":"Keep manually approved rules outside regenerated text","text":"doc-11 keep manually approved rules outside regenerated text. record only explicit author decisions in manually_approved_rules.md, with scope, approval date and evidence. read applicable entries when rewriting explanations. maintain the approval file; never regenerate it or treat an ai suggestion as approval. author source + manually approved rules → regenerated explanation the author's source and the approved rules feed the explanation; an ai suggestion filed as an approval is wrong, an entry with the author's message is right manually_approved_rules.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-12","code":"DOC-12","name":"A document without an author section is open to requested edits","text":"doc-12 a document without an author section is open to requested edits. a document with no author notes above a heading is an ordinary file: the ai edits it for the task asked. where the author's section exists, it stays on top and is never overwritten. when its end is unclear, ask first. author notes → never overwritten heading explanation → regenerated no author notes → edited on request a file with author notes, kept on top while the explanation below is regenerated, beside a file without them, edited on request writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-13","code":"DOC-13","name":"A glossary keeps the author's terms; the AI explains below them","text":"doc-13 a glossary keeps the author's terms; the ai explains below them. everything before the first section (title, note, groups, term list) is the author's and is never rewritten. each section explains one term and is regenerated from the author's line. testing (test) yours - flaky test – … yours intermittent failures ai a glossary file: the author's title and term list on top, never rewritten, and below one ai section per term writing.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"DOC-14","code":"DOC-14","name":"A GIF shows its time and its progress","text":"doc-14 a gif shows its time and its progress. every gif in the docs is recorded at 30 frames a second, counts its time from 0 at its first frame in a corner, and has a thin bar along its top that fills as it plays; both start again when it loops. a gif frame with nothing to tell how far in it is, beside one with a bar two thirds along its top and 3.07 s in its corner gifs.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT","code":"LINT","name":"Stated by the linter","text":"lint stated by the linter rules that only linting.md and the preset spell out."},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-01","code":"LINT-01","name":"Spaces around operators, except *, / and ","text":"lint-01 spaces around operators, except , / and . , / and are tight. every other binary, logical and assignment operator has a space on each side. const x = a& 42;b + c/d - e& 42;& 42;2; const y = a % 2; spaced multiplication and tight addition, wrong, beside tight / and spaced + - %, right linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-02","code":"LINT-02","name":"Space before an anonymous function's parenthesis","text":"lint-02 space before an anonymous function's parenthesis. an anonymous function has a space before its parenthesis; a named one has none. server.on('error', function (error) { }); function main() { } function(error) without a space and function main () with one, wrong, beside function (error) and function main(), right linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-03","code":"LINT-03","name":"Tiny arrows fit one line","text":"lint-03 tiny arrows fit one line. a tiny arrow is a single-line expression callback or value factory, passed as a call argument or as an object property value; an empty () = {} is one too (fmt-32). options-style methods retain function expressions (fmt-12); literal methods: {...} tables reject arrows. it fits one line; the number of parameters does not matter. a lone value/event parameter is v , vv only when v is already taken where it stands (var-09); an error parameter stays e at every depth (var-05), including .catch , .then rejection callbacks and eventemitter error listeners. it is taken whole — v = v.uid , not ({uid}) = uid — and a last parameter it does not use is not declared: () = run() , not v = run() . a named helper is a function declaration. four wrong arrows beside their right forms: a destructured parameter, err for an error, a named helper as an arrow, an arrow in methods linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-04","code":"LINT-04","name":"Use for...of, not forEach","text":"lint-04 use for...of, not foreach. foreach does not exist. use for...of . items.foreach(function (item) { ✗ no }); for (const item of items) { ✓ yes } items.foreach(...), wrong, beside for (const item of items), right linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-06","code":"LINT-06","name":"Imports first: side effects, defaults, named","text":"lint-06 imports first: side effects, defaults, named. imports are the first statements of a file, one statement per line, in up to three blocks: side-effect imports — import './x' , a bare require('x') — first, in the order they run; then the sorted default imports; then, if set apart, the sorted named imports ( {foo, bar} ). one blank line may separate the blocks. the three import blocks in order: side effects as they run, sorted defaults, sorted named imports linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-07","code":"LINT-07","name":"The project picks its module format","text":"lint-07 the project picks its module format. module format is the consuming project’s choice: javascript defaults to es modules, .cjs is commonjs, and the sorting rule supports both import forms. commonjs-only is this repository’s own rule. es modules for .js, commonjs for .cjs, and commonjs only in this repository linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-08","code":"LINT-08","name":"A layout and its gap share a class value","text":"lint-08 a layout and its gap share a class value. gap closes the layout group: the flex/grid/split container, then its flex- modifiers, then the gap. put a layout and its gap in the same class value. a layout and its gap in separate class values, wrong, beside them in one value, right linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-09","code":"LINT-09","name":"Font size and weight before line height","text":"lint-09 font size and weight before line height. within typography, fs and fw come before lh . several local - classes together form the final group. line height before font size and a local class before a utility, wrong, beside fs, fw, lh and local classes last, right linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-10","code":"LINT-10","name":"Write #- only where the loader replaces it","text":"lint-10 write - only where the loader replaces it. - is written only in the forms the hashtag loader replaces: a double-quoted class , card_class or attribute ending in -class / :class ; . - in a style block; and the exact single-quoted '. - form in javascript. four - forms the loader skips beside the forms it replaces: double-quoted class attributes, '. - in javascript, . - in a style block linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-11","code":"LINT-11","name":"Every #- class has a real style","text":"lint-11 every - class has a real style. each statically known - class has a nonempty style selector in the same component. declarations, includes and extends count as styles; an empty rule, a variable-only rule, or a mention inside :not(...) does not. an empty - rule and a - class only inside :not(), wrong, beside rules with declarations, right linting.md"},
{"page":"rules.html","id":"LINT-12","code":"LINT-12","name":"A Vue script indents four spaces","text":"lint-12 a vue script indents four spaces. a vue is indented four spaces with one base indent. a vue script with no base indent and two spaces, wrong, beside one base indent and four spaces, right linting.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM","code":"ANIM","name":"Animations","text":"anim animations how a screen moves."},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-01","code":"ANIM-01","name":"Sections slide the way the list goes","text":"anim-01 sections slide the way the list goes. picking another section in the sidebar slides the content vertically, like switching workspaces: down the list, the old page leaves upward and the new one rises from below; up the list, the reverse. sections 1 to 2, 2 to 10 and 10 to 9: down the list the page goes up, up the list it goes down ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-02","code":"ANIM-02","name":"A far jump is one slide","text":"anim-02 a far jump is one slide. from section 2 to 10 the content does not pass through 3 to 9: the two pages slide, and only the highlight crosses the items between them. from 2 to 10 only the two pages slide; the highlight crosses the items between ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-03","code":"ANIM-03","name":"The highlight and the content move together","text":"anim-03 the highlight and the content move together. the selection in the sidebar slides from the old item to the new one at the same time as the content, with the same duration and easing. the highlight slides from item to item while the pages slide, both ending together ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-04","code":"ANIM-04","name":"Depth is left and right","text":"anim-04 depth is left and right. opening a row moves the list out to the left as its page comes in from the right; back does the reverse. sections still slide up and down, from a list or from a row's page. a row opens from the right and back returns from the left; a section picked from a row's page slides vertically ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-05","code":"ANIM-05","name":"On a row's page, its own section is Back","text":"anim-05 on a row's page, its own section is back. clicking the section the open row belongs to steps back out to its list, the way back does. on a calendar row's page, clicking calendar in the sidebar slides back to the calendar list ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-06","code":"ANIM-06","name":"A list shows each change as movement","text":"anim-06 a list shows each change as movement. an added row opens a gap and fades into it; a removed row fades out and the gap closes; a moved row travels to its new place while the others make room. rows added at the start and in the middle open a gap, a removed row's gap closes, a moved row travels over the others ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-07","code":"ANIM-07","name":"A list change never moves the working row","text":"anim-07 a list change never moves the working row. rows added or removed above the selected row leave it where it is on the screen, from the first frame to the last; the scroll position takes up the difference (uim-01). near the list's end, where the place cannot hold, the view settles in the same frames as the move and is still when it ends, never after. rows come in above and the selected row stays on the dashed line; with the rule off it drops below ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-08","code":"ANIM-08","name":"A click mid-way changes course","text":"anim-08 a click mid-way changes course. a click during an animation neither waits for it nor jumps back: everything moves on from where it stands on the screen at that moment. a slide reversed half way, back pressed half way into a row, a section picked half way into another ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-09","code":"ANIM-09","name":"A header opens its message and folds it back","text":"anim-09 a header opens its message and folds it back. clicking a folded one-line header opens the message in full; clicking the open header folds it back to its line. the card's height goes from the one to the other and the messages below slide with its edge. three cards: a folded header opens its message and folds it back, the messages below slide ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-10","code":"ANIM-10","name":"The parts morph, nothing pops","text":"anim-10 the parts morph, nothing pops. the parts the two layouts share morph into their places in the other one: the avatar shrinks into the line, the time moves beside it, the text glides up at its smaller size, and several lines join into the one line, each to its own place in reading order; a pill keeps its box and words and only its extra words narrow away, covered by its edge, never faded. opening is the same, backwards. only what has no place there, the name, the picture, a player, the rest of the text, fades, over the whole movement: never whole on one frame and gone the next. a voice message: its three status lines join into the one line, the pill narrows, the time moves; slowly at 2 s ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-11","code":"ANIM-11","name":"The header clicked never moves","text":"anim-11 the header clicked never moves. it stands still from the first frame to the last, opening or folding, mid-way too; only what is below it moves (uim-01). the clicked header stays on the dashed line while its message morphs open and folded ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-12","code":"ANIM-12","name":"A moving part lands exactly","text":"anim-12 a moving part lands exactly. each part is measured where it will be, laid out unseen first, so its last frame is the still state to the pixel: no hop at the end. left, a time box lands and then corrects itself; right, it is measured where it will be and lands exactly ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-13","code":"ANIM-13","name":"New things arrive whole","text":"anim-13 new things arrive whole. a new row or message arrives in its first frame as it will stay, with every tag and badge on it and at its final height; nothing fills in or grows after it lands. left, a new message jumps in and its answered tag fills in after; right, it arrives with the tag at its final height ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-14","code":"ANIM-14","name":"Growing goes through the heights between","text":"anim-14 growing goes through the heights between. a section that opens grows from closed to full through every height between, its content clipped inside, never shown whole for a frame first; closing is the same, backwards. left, a sending section is whole for a frame, then grows; right, it grows through every height ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-15","code":"ANIM-15","name":"The chosen highlight glides in its final colours","text":"anim-15 the chosen highlight glides in its final colours. the selection highlight moves from item to item in the colours it ends in, from the first frame to the last; no grey or faded colour on the way. left, the highlight turns grey on the way to projects; right, it keeps its own colours all the way ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"animations.html","id":"ANIM-16","code":"ANIM-16","name":"Nothing appears or disappears suddenly","text":"anim-16 nothing appears or disappears suddenly. no element appears or disappears suddenly; every element appears and disappears smoothly. best, it morphs (motion-01): what comes from something on the screen or goes into it changes its shape, size and place through every frame between, a menu growing out of its button and back into it, a chip opening into its field. only what has nothing to come from or go into, a toast, a tooltip, fades in as it comes and out as it goes, at the shared speed; never whole on one frame and gone on the next. left, a menu and a toast pop in and out on one frame; right, the menu grows out of its button and back, the toast fades in and out ▶ try it animations.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"AGENT","code":"AGENT","name":"Agents","text":"agent agents vocabulary to use when developing agents (coding agents, design agents, embedded agents, etc.)."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART","code":"PART","name":"Parts","text":"part parts what an agent is made of."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-01","code":"PART-01","name":"agent","text":"part-01 agent harness + model + tools + policy. never the model alone. agent: harness, model, tools and policy in one box, the workspace beside it agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-02","code":"PART-02","name":"model","text":"part-02 model the llm, identified by a string ( claude-opus-5 ). stateless: one context in, one message out. model: a context in, one message out, nothing remembered between calls agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-03","code":"PART-03","name":"harness","text":"part-03 harness the code that runs the loop: builds context, calls the model, executes tools, appends results, stops. harness: the loop of building the context, calling the model, running the tools, appending, stopping agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-04","code":"PART-04","name":"tool","text":"part-04 tool a named operation the model may request. the model sees its definition; the harness owns its implementation. tool: the definition the model sees, the implementation the harness owns agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-05","code":"PART-05","name":"policy","text":"part-05 policy text in the system message that constrains behavior. read, not enforced. enforcement is permission, gate and sandbox. policy: text the model reads; what must not happen is stopped by permission, gate and sandbox agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-06","code":"PART-06","name":"skill","text":"part-06 skill instructions loaded into context on demand, not resident in every call. skill: instructions on a shelf, loaded into the context only when asked for agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PART-07","code":"PART-07","name":"workspace","text":"part-07 workspace what tools act on: a repo, a canvas, an app database. workspace: a repository, a canvas or an app database, what the tools act on agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"ROLE","code":"ROLE","name":"Roles","text":"role roles who stands outside the loop."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"ROLE-01","code":"ROLE-01","name":"user","text":"role-01 user who starts a turn: a person or a calling application. user: a person or an application starts a turn agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"ROLE-02","code":"ROLE-02","name":"operator","text":"role-02 operator who configures and deploys the agent. operator: writes the policy, picks the model and tools, sets limits, deploys agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TIME","code":"TIME","name":"Time","text":"time time from one call to a whole session."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TIME-01","code":"TIME-01","name":"call","text":"time-01 call one request to the model. call: one request to the model, inside a round, a turn, a session, a run agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TIME-02","code":"TIME-02","name":"round","text":"time-02 round one call plus execution of the tool_calls it returned. zero tool_calls for a final. round: one call plus the tool_calls it returned agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TIME-03","code":"TIME-03","name":"turn","text":"time-03 turn one user message (or one scheduled task) through to the final. one or many rounds. one user message, or one scheduled task, through to its last final: one round or many. the checks after a final belong to the same turn: when the tests fail, the harness sends the failure back and the fixing goes on in the same turn, so max_rounds and the budget cover it too (mp-60). turn: one user message through to the final, one or many rounds agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TIME-04","code":"TIME-04","name":"session","text":"time-04 session ordered turns sharing one transcript. session: ordered turns sharing one transcript agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TIME-05","code":"TIME-05","name":"run","text":"time-05 run one launch of the harness process (cli invocation, cron job). run: one launch of the harness, holding the session agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG","code":"MSG","name":"Messages","text":"msg messages what the transcript holds and a call sends."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-01","code":"MSG-01","name":"message","text":"msg-01 message one unit of the transcript. has a role: system, user, assistant, tool. message: one unit of the transcript with its role agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-02","code":"MSG-02","name":"transcript","text":"msg-02 transcript every message of the session so far, append-only. includes tool_calls and tool_results. transcript: every message so far, append-only agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-03","code":"MSG-03","name":"context","text":"msg-03 context what one call sends: system, tool_definitions, the transcript or its compacted form, grounding. what surrounds a thing and gives it its meaning: the sentence around a word, the conversation around a message, the project around a file. the same words mean different things in different contexts, so the context is read first and the thing after. for an ai model, the context is the context window: everything it reads before it answers, counted in tokens. one call sends it whole: the system message, the tool definitions, the transcript or its compacted form, the grounding. the model has nothing else: what is not in the context does not exist for it, and what falls out of it (compacted away, or left behind when a session ends) is gone unless it was written down somewhere it will be read again, a note, a file, a database. context: system message, tool definitions, the transcript or its summary, grounding, sent whole to the model agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-04","code":"MSG-04","name":"sidecar","text":"msg-04 sidecar a second payload carried beside a message, never merged into it: the untruncated tool output, file handles, timings. the request goes over the wire; the sidecar stays with the harness. sidecar: the small message over the wire, the large payload kept by the harness agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-05","code":"MSG-05","name":"memory","text":"msg-05 memory what outlives one context: the transcript, plus external stores (notes, a database). memory: the transcript, notes, files and databases that outlive one context agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-06","code":"MSG-06","name":"system","text":"msg-06 system the message that holds the policy. tool_definitions travel beside it, not in it. system: the message holding the policy, the tool definitions beside it agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-07","code":"MSG-07","name":"tool_call","text":"msg-07 tool_call model → harness: tool name, arguments, id. tool call: name, arguments and id, from the model to the harness agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-08","code":"MSG-08","name":"tool_result","text":"msg-08 tool_result harness → model: output or error of one tool_call, paired by id. data, never instructions. tool result: output or error back to the model, paired by id, data not instructions agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-09","code":"MSG-09","name":"final","text":"msg-09 final an assistant message with no tool_call. ends the turn. says nothing about whether the task succeeded. an assistant message with no tool_call. it ends the turn once the checks after it pass; when a check fails, the failure goes back and another final comes later in the same turn. a final says nothing about whether the task succeeded. final is our name, not an api's (mp-60). final: an assistant message with no tool_call ends the turn; its claim still needs a check agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MSG-10","code":"MSG-10","name":"stop_reason","text":"msg-10 stop_reason why a call ended: final, tool_call, max_tokens, refusal. the names are the api's. why a call ended: final, tool_call, max_tokens, refusal. these are our own names; each api spells them its way. anthropic's stop_reason says end_turn for a final, tool_use for a tool_call, max_tokens and refusal ; openai's finish_reason says stop , tool_calls , length and content_filter (mp-60). stop reason: final, tool_call, max_tokens or refusal agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL","code":"TOOL","name":"Tools","text":"tool tools what the model may request, and what contains it."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-01","code":"TOOL-01","name":"tool_definition","text":"tool-01 tool_definition name + description + input_schema. what the model sees. the description is what makes the model pick it. tool definition: name, description and input schema agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-02","code":"TOOL-02","name":"input_schema","text":"tool-02 input_schema the shape of a tool's arguments, as json schema. input schema: arguments that fit run, arguments that do not are refused agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-03","code":"TOOL-03","name":"readonly","text":"tool-03 readonly observes only: view, grep, query. declared at registration; a tool is classed by the worst its arguments allow. readonly: the three classes of tools, readonly highlighted agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-04","code":"TOOL-04","name":"mutating","text":"tool-04 mutating changes the workspace: edit, insert, shell. mutating: the three classes of tools, mutating highlighted agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-05","code":"TOOL-05","name":"destructive","text":"tool-05 destructive mutating and hard to undo: rm, drop, deploy. destructive: the three classes of tools, destructive highlighted agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-06","code":"TOOL-06","name":"permission","text":"tool-06 permission what the harness lets a tool touch: paths, hosts, commands. enforced before the tool runs. permission: a tool_call outside the allowed paths is refused before it runs agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-07","code":"TOOL-07","name":"gate","text":"tool-07 gate user approval required before a tool runs. default for destructive. gate: the harness asks the user before a destructive tool runs agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-08","code":"TOOL-08","name":"sandbox","text":"tool-08 sandbox the boundary that contains a tool's effects: files, network, processes. sandbox: files, network and processes inside the boundary, the machine outside it agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TOOL-09","code":"TOOL-09","name":"grounding","text":"tool-09 grounding workspace state the harness injects into context each turn. grounding: the state of the workspace, injected into the context each turn agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"LIM","code":"LIM","name":"Limits","text":"lim limits caps, and what is cut to fit them."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"LIM-01","code":"LIM-01","name":"max_rounds","text":"lim-01 max_rounds hard cap on rounds per turn. max rounds: the seventh round never runs agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"LIM-02","code":"LIM-02","name":"budget","text":"lim-02 budget token, time or cost cap, scoped to a turn, run or session. budget: token, time and cost bars, scoped to a turn, run or session agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"LIM-03","code":"LIM-03","name":"truncate","text":"lim-03 truncate cut one tool_result to fit. the full output stays in the sidecar. truncate: one result cut to fit, the whole of it in the sidecar agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"LIM-04","code":"LIM-04","name":"compact","text":"lim-04 compact replace the older part of the transcript in context with a summary. the replaced messages stay in the transcript and in the sidecar. compact: older messages replaced by a summary in the context, kept in the transcript agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"FAIL","code":"FAIL","name":"Failure","text":"fail failure how a turn goes wrong."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"FAIL-01","code":"FAIL-01","name":"stall","text":"fail-01 stall rounds continue without progress. reading counts as progress; repeating the same call does not. stall: the same call five times, nothing moves agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"FAIL-02","code":"FAIL-02","name":"runaway","text":"fail-02 runaway a turn stopped by max_rounds or budget. runaway: a turn stopped by the cap, not by a final agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"RECOV","code":"RECOV","name":"Recovery","text":"recov recovery what follows a final."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"RECOV-01","code":"RECOV-01","name":"check","text":"recov-01 check harness runs tests or lint after final and re-prompts on failure. extends the turn. the harness runs the tests or the linter after a final and, on a failure, sends it back to the model. it extends the turn: the turn ends at the last final, once the checks pass (mp-60). check: tests run after the final; a failure goes back as another round agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"RECOV-02","code":"RECOV-02","name":"review","text":"recov-02 review one extra call asking a model to inspect the result against the task. same model, another agent, or a human. review: one extra call reads the task and the result and gives a verdict agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"RECOV-03","code":"RECOV-03","name":"handoff","text":"recov-03 handoff agent stops and passes the task and its context to the user or to another agent. handoff: the task and its context passed whole to the user or another agent agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"KIND","code":"KIND","name":"Kinds","text":"kind kinds an agent is named by its workspace."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"KIND-01","code":"KIND-01","name":"coding agent","text":"kind-01 coding agent workspace = repo; tools = view, grep, edit, shell. coding agent: workspace a repository, tools view, grep, edit, shell agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"KIND-02","code":"KIND-02","name":"design agent","text":"kind-02 design agent workspace = canvas or artifact; tools = inspect, render, place, style. design agent: workspace a canvas, tools inspect, render, place, style agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"KIND-03","code":"KIND-03","name":"embedded agent","text":"kind-03 embedded agent workspace = app data via the app's api; scope enforced by the app's access controls. embedded agent: workspace app data through its api agents.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST","code":"TEST","name":"Testing","text":"test testing words for failures, and for the tests that find them."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-01","code":"TEST-01","name":"Happy Path","text":"test-01 happy path the ideal scenario without errors happy path: the green road of steps, the grey error branches never taken testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-02","code":"TEST-02","name":"Smoke Tests","text":"test-02 smoke tests a small set of basic tests that verify whether the most critical parts of a system work at all. they answer one question: does the application run without immediately breaking? if smoke tests fail, deeper testing is usually pointless. (why \"smoke\"? from hardware: power it on – if smoke comes out, something is seriously broken.) smoke tests: power on, does it run at all; the deep tests wait behind testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-03","code":"TEST-03","name":"Edge Case","text":"test-03 edge case a situation at an extreme of the input – an empty list, the first or the last item, the maximum size – where code written for the usual case breaks edge case: the usual range in green, the empty, first, last and maximum in red testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-04","code":"TEST-04","name":"Transient Error","text":"test-04 transient error temporary issues that resolve quickly transient error: two failed tries, a wait, the third answered testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-05","code":"TEST-05","name":"Intermittent Failure","text":"test-05 intermittent failure a failure that happens sometimes, unpredictably a failure of the application that happens sometimes, unpredictably, and usually cannot be reproduced consistently. request 1 works, request 2 fails, request 3 works again — even though nothing was changed. a test that fails this way is a flaky test. common causes: timing issues and race conditions, network instability, concurrency issues (threads or processes interfering), uninitialized values, hardware flakiness (bad ram, overheating), resource limits hit sometimes (a connection pool exhausted). painful because it does not fail every time, logs often do not show a clear cause, and \"works on my machine\" happens a lot. intermittent failure: six requests to the same code, two of them failing, nothing changed testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-06","code":"TEST-06","name":"Error Flood","text":"test-06 error flood scenario with 75% errors (?) provisional: not defined yet. the author's note reads \"a scenario with 75% errors\", a working name kept until the author settles what it means (mp-62). error flood: forty requests, thirty of them failed testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-07","code":"TEST-07","name":"Flaky Test","text":"test-07 flaky test tests that produce inconsistent results, sometimes passing and sometimes failing, without any changes to the code or test being tested a test that passes and fails by turns with no change to the code or to the test. the fault is in the test (a fixed timeout, an order it relies on) or an intermittent failure under it. flaky test: one unchanged test, red and green by turns through the week testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-08","code":"TEST-08","name":"Showstopper Bug","text":"test-08 showstopper bug a defect so severe that it prevents the system from functioning or blocks further progress (development, testing, or release). work cannot reasonably continue until it is fixed. showstopper bug: the road from development to customers blocked by a barrier testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-09","code":"TEST-09","name":"Self-check","text":"test-09 self-check the application's own check that it is fit to work, as a car checks itself on the dashboard: one lamp per thing it needs, the programs it calls, the variables, the folders, what its build produced, each dark when the thing is there and works, lit when it is not. it reads and reports, changes nothing; one lit lamp names what to fix and stops it the application checks, by itself, that it is fit to work, as a car checks itself on the dashboard: one lamp per thing it needs, dark when that thing is there and works, lit when it is not. the lamps are the programs it calls, the environment variables, the folders it writes, the port, the versions, and what its own build was to produce. a lit lamp is the whole answer: the application is not fit, and the lamp names what to fix. it changes nothing: a self-check reads and reports. when every lamp is dark it says one line or nothing; when one is lit it names it and stops, before any work is half done. it runs where there is something new to check: the last step of the build (proj-30), so an image that fails it is never shipped; and again as the first step of the run when the host may differ from the build, so a host that fails it does no work. one probe per lamp, each in its own strict shell, its output prefixed with the lamp's name, so the log says which one lit. present is not enough: a probe exercises the tool the way the application will use it (a font subset must come out smaller with zopfli; a time zone the data uses must be known to the runtime), for the usual failure is a tool that is there and misconfigured. beside its neighbours: bin/configure makes the environment, the self-check tells whether it is as made (proj-02). a smoke test asks the same question from outside, by a test run now and then; the self-check is asked by the application itself, on every build or start. a health check is the probe of a running service, asked again and again; the self-check runs once. self-check, not self-test: a test is run against the code, by bin/test ; a check is what the application does to itself and its surroundings, as a driver reads the dashboard before setting out. the command is bin/self-check , named for who checks: the application, not a tester from outside. a script named for one lamp, check-env for the environment, is one lamp of it; the self-check is the whole dashboard, and the build's last step adds the lamps of its own output, the folders it was to produce. self-check: a dashboard of lamps, one lit, naming the port to fix testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"TEST-10","code":"TEST-10","name":"Safety net","text":"test-10 safety net a second, independent catch under the first way: when the usual mechanism fails or is forgotten, the net limits the damage. not the plan, but what holds when the plan does not the net under the trapeze: the act is done without it, and nobody falls on purpose, but when someone does, the net is what holds. in software, a second mechanism, independent of the first, that catches what the first lets through and limits the damage: a timeout behind a request that should return, a cap on a loop that should end, a job that puts back what a step should have put back. it is not the way things are meant to work; it is what holds when they do not. an example: an agent is switched to the strongest model for one message, and the reply is meant to switch it back. the safety net: if the agent is still on the strongest model ten minutes after that message's reply, the app switches it back itself and says so in the log. the switch-back after the reply is the plan; the ten-minute check is the net. beside its neighbours: a self-check asks before the work whether the application is fit; an integrity check asks after it whether the data is still whole; a safety net sits under the work itself and acts when the first mechanism did not. a fallback is a second way to the same result; a safety net does not finish the job, it stops a miss from spreading. a good net is simple and independent, so it cannot fail for the same reason as what it guards, and it says when it caught something: every catch is a sign that the first way failed, and worth a look. safety net: the plan as a rope across, the miss falling into the net below testing.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORD","code":"WORD","name":"Documents","text":"word documents words about words."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORD-01","code":"WORD-01","name":"vocabulary","text":"word-01 vocabulary which words we have/use vocabulary: our words as pills, the words we do not use struck through readme.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORD-02","code":"WORD-02","name":"glossary","text":"word-02 glossary the words of a vocabulary, with their description and meaning glossary: a table of terms, each with its meaning readme.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORD-03","code":"WORD-03","name":"dictionary","text":"word-03 dictionary every word of a language, in order, each with all its meanings every word of a language, or of a whole field, in alphabetical order, each with all its meanings, its part of speech and how it is used. where a glossary keeps only our words and the one meaning we give each, a dictionary keeps every word and every meaning; a glossary is chosen, a dictionary is complete. in code a dictionary is something else: a map from keys to values ( user_by_id ). dictionary: process, provider and proxy under p, process with two numbered meanings readme.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP","code":"MAP","name":"Maps and ways","text":"map maps and ways words for the shapes knowledge takes, and for the way a piece of work goes: where we are, where we came from, where we are going."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-01","code":"MAP-01","name":"tree","text":"map-01 tree one root; every other node has exactly one parent; no circles. folders, outlines, an org chart. one root, and every other node has exactly one parent, so there are no circles and exactly one path from the root to any node. folders, an outline, an org chart, a family line. in the agent notes: the folders of a note (agentdir, commondir, messages, roadmaps). a thread is a tree as long as each message answers one message. a tree is a graph with rules; a taxonomy is a tree of kinds. tree: the folders of a note maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-02","code":"MAP-02","name":"graph","text":"map-02 graph nodes and edges, directed or not, with no rule on how they link: several parents, circles. things (nodes) and the links between them (edges), directed or not, with no rule on how they link: a node may have several parents, and links may run in circles. the pages of this site are a graph: the readme links to rules and to glossaries, rules links to glossaries, glossaries to agents, and agents back to rules. glossaries has two parents, and three pages link in a circle; a tree allows neither. trees, taxonomies and ontologies are all graphs with rules added. graph: the pages of this site and the links between them, one page with two parents and three pages in a circle maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-03","code":"MAP-03","name":"map","text":"map-03 map a smaller picture that keeps what matters of something bigger and leaves the rest out on purpose. a smaller picture that keeps what matters of something bigger: where things are and how they connect. \"the map is not the territory\": a map leaves things out on purpose. in code, a map (a dictionary) goes from keys to values, one value per key. also a mind map, a concept map, a site map. in the agent notes: a roadmap is a map of the way; \"the terms and how they relate\" below is a concept map; a glossary maps each term to its meaning. map maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-04","code":"MAP-04","name":"taxonomy","text":"map-04 taxonomy kinds and kinds of kinds, each one \"is a\" the one above it (animal › bird › owl). a tree of kinds. sorting into kinds and kinds of kinds, each one \"is a\" the one above it: animal › bird › owl. linnaeus for living things, the shelves of a library. in the agent notes: a message is the author's or the agent's; a page is a subject file, a cover or a plan; a marker is .working or .cancelled . a taxonomy is a tree, and the backbone of an ontology, which adds the other links. taxonomy: the kinds of things in a note maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-05","code":"MAP-05","name":"ontology","text":"map-05 ontology the concepts of a field, their instances, the relations between them and their rules, written down. in philosophy, the study of what exists. in computing, a written map of what a field talks about, in four parts: the kinds of things (a message, a conversation, a subject file), the things themselves (your message of this morning asking for a glossary is a message), the relations between them (a conversation is made of messages; a reply answers a message) and the rules (every conversation has exactly one subject file). it is written down so that people and programs use the words the same way: rdf and owl are the standards, schema.org the best known on the web. a glossary defines words one by one; an ontology also says how they connect. written down once, a rename (workdir became subjectdir) happens in one place, and an agent can check a word against it. ontology: the words of a note and how they connect the terms of this glossary and how they relate maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-06","code":"MAP-06","name":"knowledge graph","text":"map-06 knowledge graph an ontology filled with real things: not \"a message answers a message\" but \"this reply answers that message\". an ontology filled with real things: not \"a reply answers a message\" in general, but \"my reply of 09:30 answers your message of 09:17\", one link for each pair that has it. what search engines and ai retrieval use. in the agent notes: every message, subject file and plan of a note with their links; the app could build it, and it would feed an agent's context. knowledge graph: the ontology on the left, this note's real messages and links on the right maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-07","code":"MAP-07","name":"roadmap","text":"map-07 roadmap the whole way ahead: where we go and in what order, not exactly when. now / next / later. the whole way ahead: where we are going and in what order, not exactly when. a product roadmap is often three columns, now / next / later. it changes as we learn, and it is meant to be shown, so it stays coarse: themes and stages, not tasks. in the agent notes: an agent's plan for one conversation, roadmaps/<time -agent-plan.md , its parts - [ ] p1 … ticked as each is done, new ones added at the end; and the author's roadmaps in the app, lists of work across projects. a roadmap is laid on a timeline, marked by milestones and fed by a backlog. roadmap maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-08","code":"MAP-08","name":"milestone","text":"map-08 milestone one point on the way, reached or not. takes no time, unlike a task. the roman stone by the road that said how far it was to rome. in a project, a point that is reached or not (\"released\", \"signed\"); it takes no time, unlike a task. a diamond on a gantt chart; on github, a group of issues with a due date. in the agent notes: the goal's steps in a subject file, drawn under the cover: done, now or next. the step that is now waits on the agent while it works and on the author after its reply; a step is named as it stands (\"testing\" while now, \"tested\" once done); the last is always \"done: nothing left, to the archive\". milestone maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-09","code":"MAP-09","name":"breadcrumbs","text":"map-09 breadcrumbs the trail behind: from the root to where you stand, each crumb a way back (home › docs › page). the trail that shows where you are and how you got there, as in hansel and gretel; on a web page, home › docs › page, each crumb a link back up. against roadmap and milestone: breadcrumbs look back (the way behind, from the root to here), a roadmap looks ahead (the whole way, in order), and a milestone is one stone on that way. a milestone passed is, in effect, a breadcrumb. in the agent notes: the path agentdir › commondir › terms , and the chain of re: lines back to a conversation's first message. breadcrumbs, roadmap and milestone side by side maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-10","code":"MAP-10","name":"timeline","text":"map-10 timeline events placed along time. events placed along time. in the agent notes: the change log at the end of each subject file, and the times in message names. a roadmap is laid on one. timeline: the events of this morning along one line maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-11","code":"MAP-11","name":"chronology","text":"map-11 chronology events in the order they happened, each with its time; the gaps between them not drawn. events in the order they happened, each with its date or time, at even steps: the order and the times count, not the distance between them. it fits what a user did over time: first one thing, then another, then a third. a long gap is cut short, its length written on the cut. in the agent notes: a conversation's messages, first to last, each with its time in its name. a timeline puts each event at its moment, so the gaps show; in a chronology a night between two visits takes no more room than a minute. chronology: one user's seven actions evenly along a line, from opening the pricing page to upgrading the next day, the 23-hour night cut short maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-12","code":"MAP-12","name":"backlog","text":"map-12 backlog work known but not yet planned, in no fixed order. the work known but not yet planned, in no fixed order. in the agent notes: the \"what is next\" list of a subject file, the parts not yet on a roadmap. it feeds a roadmap. backlog: a pile of cards in no order, and the three columns of the roadmap they feed maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-13","code":"MAP-13","name":"anatomy","text":"map-13 anatomy the parts of one thing seen whole, each named where it sits (a button: container, icon, label). the parts of one thing, seen whole: each part named where it sits, in the thing it belongs to. borrowed from biology (the anatomy of a hand), it is common in computing: the anatomy of a button (a container, an icon on the left, a label beside it), of a url (scheme, host, path, query, in that order), of a log line or of a file. design systems give each component page an anatomy section: the component drawn once, each part numbered in place, the names beside. in the agent notes: a message as the app shows it, its header on top, its text, a voice comment's player with the transcript under it, a screenshot last. a taxonomy sorts many things into kinds (\"is a\"); an anatomy opens one thing (\"part of\"). the parts taken out and set in a tree are a meronomy, which keeps what holds what but loses where each part sits: the anatomy is the picture of the whole, usually a labelled diagram. anatomy: one message seen whole, its five parts numbered where they sit maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-14","code":"MAP-14","name":"labelled diagram","text":"map-14 labelled diagram a thing drawn whole, a thin line from each part to its name. a thing drawn whole, as it is seen, with a thin line (a callout, or leader line) from each part to its name. nothing is moved and nothing is opened, so only the outside can be named. the common picture of an anatomy: a diagram of a flower in a school book, a device's parts in its manual, a component in a design system. what is inside stays hidden; a cutaway opens it, an exploded view takes it apart. labelled diagram: a hand coffee grinder whole, a line from each part to its name maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-15","code":"MAP-15","name":"exploded view","text":"map-15 exploded view a thing's parts pulled apart along the lines they fit on, numbered for a parts list. the parts of a thing pulled apart along the lines they fit on, each in the place it goes, so that every part is seen and the order of putting them together can be read off the picture. each part has a number; the numbers lead to a parts list (in industry, a bill of materials). an assembly sheet of flat-pack furniture is one. it shows what a labelled diagram cannot: the parts behind other parts, and how they go together. exploded view: the same grinder, its seven parts pulled apart along their axis and numbered, with the parts list maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-16","code":"MAP-16","name":"cutaway","text":"map-16 cutaway a thing drawn with part of its casing taken away, so the inside shows. a thing drawn whole with part of its casing taken away, so the inside shows in its place: the gears of a clock behind its face, the rooms of a ship behind its hull. the edge of the cut is drawn, so it reads as opened, not as missing. unlike an exploded view nothing moves: the inside is seen where it works. only what the cut opens is named; the outside is the labelled diagram's. cutaway: the same grinder with its front cut open, the shaft, the burrs and the ground coffee named maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MAP-17","code":"MAP-17","name":"legend","text":"map-17 legend the key of a picture: what each colour, line and sign means, set apart from the drawing. the key of a picture: a small box, set apart from the drawing, with a sample of each colour, line and sign and what it means. a map's legend says that a blue line is a river and a dashed red one a border; a chart's says which colour is which series. in british english also a key. a label names one thing on the drawing itself; a caption says under the picture what the whole shows; a legend explains the marks, once, for all of them. in the agent notes: the overview's colours (green done, orange waits on the author, the accent at work), and the two samples at the top of this page's terms map. legend: a small map of roads, a river, a border and a park, its legend beside it maps.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"NAME","code":"NAME","name":"Standard names","text":"name standard names plain public names, used in place of private ones: the same word for the same thing in every project, so a reader outside knows at once what it is. several of one kind are numbered from 1: `laptop1`, `laptop2`, `vps1`, `vps2`."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH","code":"MACH","name":"Machines","text":"mach machines "},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-01","code":"MACH-01","name":"laptop","text":"mach-01 laptop a portable computer the author works on names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-02","code":"MACH-02","name":"desktop","text":"mach-02 desktop a computer at a desk, not carried names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-03","code":"MACH-03","name":"phone","text":"mach-03 phone a mobile phone names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-04","code":"MACH-04","name":"tablet","text":"mach-04 tablet a tablet names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-05","code":"MACH-05","name":"server","text":"mach-05 server a machine that serves others, at home or in an office names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-06","code":"MACH-06","name":"vps1, vps2","text":"mach-06 vps1, vps2 a rented virtual server, numbered in the order they appear names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-07","code":"MACH-07","name":"router","text":"mach-07 router the network's router names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-08","code":"MACH-08","name":"nas","text":"mach-08 nas a network storage box names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MACH-09","code":"MACH-09","name":"box","text":"mach-09 box a container or virtual machine an agent or a service runs in names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PLACE","code":"PLACE","name":"Placeholders","text":"place placeholders "},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PLACE-01","code":"PLACE-01","name":"example.com","text":"place-01 example.com a domain; subdomains under it ( api.example.com ) names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PLACE-02","code":"PLACE-02","name":"user@example.com","text":"place-02 user@example.com an e-mail address names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PLACE-03","code":"PLACE-03","name":"user","text":"place-03 user a user name; /home/user a home folder names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"PLACE-04","code":"PLACE-04","name":"192.0.2.x","text":"place-04 192.0.2.x an ipv4 address (rfc 5737, reserved for documentation) names.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MOTION","code":"MOTION","name":"Motion","text":"motion motion words for how things move on a screen. the rules that use them are on the animations page (anim)."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MOTION-01","code":"MOTION-01","name":"morphing","text":"motion-01 morphing one element turns into another: its shape, size and place change through every frame between, so the eye follows it as one. one element turns into another: its shape, size and place change through every frame between, so the eye follows it as the same thing, never one thing swapped for another. a menu grows out of its button and shrinks back into it; a chip opens into its field; a card's avatar shrinks into its folded line while the time moves beside it. it is the ideal animation: almost every animation rule is built on it. what comes from something on the screen, or goes into it, morphs (anim-16); only what has nothing to come from fades. morphing: the menu grows out of its button and back, the toast only fades motion.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"MOTION-02","code":"MOTION-02","name":"fade","text":"motion-02 fade an element comes or goes by its opacity alone, where it stands: the simplest smooth change. an element comes or goes by its opacity alone, where it stands, its shape and place unchanged: the simplest smooth change. it is for what has nothing to morph from or into, a toast or a tooltip, and for the parts of a morph that have no place in the other shape (anim-10). never whole on one frame and gone on the next. fade: the toast comes and goes by its opacity, where it stands motion.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"ARCH","code":"ARCH","name":"Architecture","text":"arch architecture words for what software is made of and for the work it does, each with the words that can stand in for it."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WHOLE","code":"WHOLE","name":"Parts","text":"whole parts from the whole down to a part of the code."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WHOLE-01","code":"WHOLE-01","name":"system","text":"whole-01 system the whole: every part working together for one purpose. also: platform, stack, infrastructure, application, setup, ecosystem. the whole: every part working together for one purpose, the browser, the services, the databases and the providers outside. a word for when the parts matter less than what they do together. also: platform (others build on it), stack (layers on top of each other), infrastructure (what runs the rest), application (what the user touches), setup (one arrangement of it), ecosystem (many owners). on the agents glossary system is the system message, not this. system: a browser, two services, a database and an outside provider in one frame, working for one purpose architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WHOLE-02","code":"WHOLE-02","name":"service","text":"whole-02 service a program that keeps running and answers requests from others. also: server, daemon, backend, endpoint, api, microservice. a program that keeps running and answers requests from others, often over the network: started once, then waiting, serving many callers in turn. also: server (the program or the machine it runs on), daemon (runs in the background, no screen), backend (behind a front end), endpoint or api (its doorway, not the whole), microservice (one small service among many). \"a service\" may also mean what is offered, not the program: then facility or utility . service: three callers send requests to one running service and each gets its answer architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WHOLE-03","code":"WHOLE-03","name":"provider","text":"whole-03 provider what supplies something the rest needs, one of several that could. also: source, supplier, vendor, backend, adapter, driver. what supplies something the rest needs (files, sign-in, e-mail, payments), one of several that could: the code asks for the thing, and whichever provider is plugged in gives it. also: source (where data comes from), supplier , vendor (a company), backend (the one behind a common front), adapter or driver (one swappable piece of code per provider). provider: the app asks for storage; the disk provider is plugged in, the cloud and memory providers could take its place architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WHOLE-04","code":"WHOLE-04","name":"module","text":"whole-04 module one part of the code with a job of its own, used by the rest only through what it exports. also: component, unit, package, library, plugin. one part of the code with a job of its own, used by the rest only through what it exports. inside it may change freely; its exports are its promise. also: component (a part, often of a screen), unit (the smallest part tested alone), package or library (shipped and versioned apart), plugin or extension (added from outside), building block . module: three modules in one codebase; each is used only through its exports, its inside hidden architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK","code":"WORK","name":"Work","text":"work work what gets done and what does it: the first four each hold the next, the last two put work in a row."},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK-01","code":"WORK-01","name":"process","text":"work-01 process a running program, its own memory, from start to exit. also: worker, instance. a running program: its own memory, its own id (pid), from start to exit. the operating system runs it; it runs jobs, tasks and functions. a worker is a process that takes jobs from a queue; an instance is one of several copies of the same program. \"process\" may also mean a way of doing things, not a program: then procedure or workflow . process: one running program with its pid and memory, started, running its jobs, then exiting architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK-02","code":"WORK-02","name":"job","text":"work-02 job work handed over to run later or apart, with an id and a status. also: batch, run. work handed over to run later or apart: it gets an id and a status the caller can look up, queued, running, done or failed, and may be retried or run on a schedule. also: batch (many items in one job), run (one execution of it). tools draw the lines their own way: in ci a job holds steps, in kubernetes a job starts pods. job: queued, running, then done or failed; a failed job is retried architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK-03","code":"WORK-03","name":"task","text":"work-03 task one unit of work, one step of a job. also: step, operation, work item. one unit of work to do: one step of a job, small enough to finish or fail as a whole. often a function call put in a queue. also: step (one in an order), operation , work item . task: a job of three tasks, resize, save and upload, done one after another architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK-04","code":"WORK-04","name":"function","text":"work-04 function code with a name: called with arguments, it returns. also: routine, subroutine, procedure, method. code with a name: called with arguments, it returns a value. no life of its own; it runs for the length of one call. also: routine or subroutine , procedure (often one that returns nothing), method (a function of an object). function: resize is called with an image and a width and returns the smaller image architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK-05","code":"WORK-05","name":"pipeline","text":"work-05 pipeline stages in a row, the output of each the input of the next. also: workflow, flow, stream, assembly line. stages in a row, the output of each the input of the next: data goes in at one end and comes out changed at the other. each stage does one thing and knows only its neighbours, so all of them can be busy at once, each with its own item. a shell's a b c , a build, a ci pipeline. also: workflow (the steps, often with people in them), flow , stream (items one by one, without end), assembly line . pipeline: photos go through resize, compress and upload and come out as thumbnails, each stage busy with its own photo architecture.md"},
{"page":"glossaries.html","id":"WORK-06","code":"WORK-06","name":"chain","text":"work-06 chain links one after another, each handing on to the next or stopping there. also: sequence, series, cascade. links one after another, each handing on to the next or stopping there. what goes along is control, a request or a call, not data being changed: middleware ( next() ), a promise chain ( .then() ), method chaining, a chain of responsibility. where a pipeline changes data stage by stage, a chain passes a turn, and any link may end it. also: sequence , series , cascade (each step sets off the next). chain: a request passes log and auth to the handler; auth may stop it with a 401 architecture.md"},
{"page":"agents.html","id":"consequences_first","code":"","name":"Consequences first","text":"consequences first a developer given a task does not start typing. they first picture what the change leads to: what if this, what if that. only then do they start, and the work follows from that picture. an agent works the same way. the task, the four \"what if\" questions asked before the first edit, and what each one shapes: the scope, what is said before the change, the states to test, the stories to tell why an agent that starts at once changes exactly what was named and stops there. whatever follows from the change is left for the author to find: the other cases built the same way, the states that break the layout, the decision that needed asking. thinking first takes a minute. without it, each consequence costs the author a message, found one at a time. the rule 1. before the first edit, ask \"what if\". what else is built like this? what does the change touch, slow down, leave stale or break? what happens when the data is long, empty, absurd? what does the author not see from where they stand? 2. let the answers shape the work. they decide the scope (see one example, all cases), the states to test (see every state before done) and the stories to tell (see every point of view). 3. tell the author what was found. a consequence that makes the change a poor one is said before the change, with the better way. what was decided along the way is said for information. what is truly ambiguous is asked, once, with the options."},
{"page":"agents.html","id":"one_example_all_cases","code":"","name":"One example, all cases","text":"one example, all cases the author shows one case and means all cases like it. a request that points at a dropdown, a button or a query names a kind of thing through one instance of it. the agent's job is to find the rest of that kind, not to fix the one instance and stop. this page is meant to be handed to an agent as it is: \"work like this\". it is one case of consequences first. the author shows one dropdown that lags: fixing only that one leaves nine lagging; finding every case like it changes all of them, and the one left is named in the reply why the author sees one case because that is the one in front of them. the project holds the other hundred, built the same way, with the same flaw. fixing only the shown one leaves the project inconsistent: one dropdown is fast and the rest lag, one button has the new font and the rest the old. then the author has to find each remaining case and point at it, one message each. finding every case of a pattern is the work a machine does better than a person; leaving it to the author leaves the agent's work to them. a change also has consequences the author may not have weighed. a cache serves stale data after an edit; a moved button may now cover another one or leave a gap. the author would rather hear that before the change than discover it after. the rule 1. an example names a class. unless the author says the change is for this one case only, it applies to every case like it. \"change the font of these buttons\" means every button. \"cache this list\" means every list loaded the same way. 2. find the like cases before changing anything. search the project for the same pattern: the same component, the same kind of request, the same style, the same idiom. the example is the way in, not the boundary. 3. change them all, or say which stay. the change goes to every case found. a case left as it was is named in the reply, with the reason; none is left out silently. 4. ask when the sweep is large or the class unclear. when the like cases are many across a big project, or the example could belong to more than one class, ask one question that carries what was found: \"only this dropdown, or all 37 lists that load on open?\". asking to clarify scope is expected, not a failure. 5. say what the change will cause. before making it, name the consequences seen: what else moves, breaks or goes stale, and what it costs. when a consequence makes the change a poor one, say so first and offer the better way; the author decides."},
{"page":"agents.html","id":"every_state_before_done","code":"","name":"Every state before done","text":"every state before done a piece of ui is not done when it looks right with the one text it was built with. it is done when it holds with every text and value it will be given. the agent tries those before saying \"done\", not the author after. it is one case of consequences first. the close button pushed under a long alert message the alert above was built with a short message. a long one took the whole row, and the close button wrapped to a line of its own, under the text. one long message in a test would have shown it. why the author sees the ui with real data, and real data is never the sample the agent typed: a name is long, a title is missing, a count is zero or a million. each of those breaks a layout made for the sample, and each break costs the author a screenshot and a message. trying the states takes the agent minutes; finding them one by one takes the author days. one alert in six states: the sample, too long, missing, tiny, absurd and error; in each the text wraps inside and the close button stays top right the rule 1. list the edge cases and the states before calling it done. the element's own states: loading, error, disabled, many at once. then, for every text and value it shows, the edge cases: - too long: a title of three lines, a word with no spaces, a path, a url; - missing: empty, null , no title at all; - tiny: one letter, one item; - absurd: zero, negative, a million, a date years away. 2. render each one and look. a screenshot of each state, or one page with all of them side by side. a state that was not rendered was not tested. 3. make the layout hold them. fixed parts keep their place: a close button stays top right however long the text, a long text wraps or ellipsizes inside its own column, an empty one collapses or shows a placeholder. nothing overlaps, nothing is pushed out of view, nothing shows through from behind. 4. keep the states in a test. the crowded case goes into the e2e test (end to end: a browser opens the real page and acts as the user does), with an assertion that nothing overlaps, so the next change does not break it again unseen. 5. report what each state led to. the reply names the states rendered, with the screenshots, and sorts them in two: - decided, for your information. a state with a clear answer is handled and said: \"a title longer than the row is cut with an ellipsis; the full one is in the tooltip\". - ambiguous, needs your confirmation. a state with no clear answer is not guessed at quietly. it is put to the author as one question with the options: \"an empty title: hide the header, or show 'untitled'?\"."},
{"page":"agents.html","id":"every_point_of_view","code":"","name":"Every point of view","text":"every point of view one task is several stories told side by side: how the user uses it, how it is built, how someone would break it. an agent tells each story before calling the work done, and the screen follows the user's story, whatever the implementation does inside. it is one case of consequences first. send is pressed: on the left the comment is nowhere for 2 seconds, then it shows; on the right it shows at once marked sending, and the mark goes when the server has it above, a comment sent under a task. in the implementation's story it goes to the server, waits until it is filed, and comes back with the next refresh, and that story is right. in the user's story they answered under this task, and for two seconds the answer is nowhere. both stories describe the same click; only one was told while building it. why an agent builds from the implementation's story, because that is the one it writes: files, queues, requests. the user lives in another story, of what they did and what they see, and the attacker in a third, of what they can reach. a defect in one story is invisible from the others: a correct queue is a missing message on screen; a convenient endpoint is an open door. the author finds each one by living that story, one message each. the points of view one task, send a comment, and the stories around it: the user's, the implementation's and the attacker's told by the author; the operator's, the next developer's and the newcomer's suggested by the agent told by the author: - user. what i do, what i see, what i expect next. the screen answers my action at once, where i acted; the route inside is not my business. - implementation. how it works: where data goes, in what order, what waits for what, what happens when a step fails. - attacker. what i can reach, read, change or flood that i should not: an input, a url, a file name, a message that is also an instruction. suggested by the agent, for the author to confirm: - operator. it failed at night and i have the logs and the data folder: can i tell what happened, and restart it without losing anything? - next developer. i open the code a year from now: can i find where this lives, read why it is so, and change it without breaking the rest? - newcomer. i see it the first time, with no context: does the screen say what it is, what to do, and what just happened? the rule 1. tell each story before the first edit. one or two lines each, in the person's words: \"i click, my answer is under the last message\". a view that does not apply is skipped, not forgotten. 2. the screen follows the user's story. when the implementation takes a longer way (a queue, an inbox, a second agent), the user still sees the result where they acted, at once, marked as pending until the way ends. the route stays inside. 3. each story gets its test. the user's story is an e2e test (end to end: a browser opens the real page and acts as the user does) of what the screen shows and when; the attacker's is a test of what is refused. 4. report by story. the reply says what each view found, and what was changed for it, so the author need not read the work through each pair of eyes again."},
{"page":"agents.html","id":"data_as_a_picture","code":"","name":"Data as a picture","text":"data as a picture a reply that carries data shows it as a picture. the author takes in a shape at a glance, where the same data as a paragraph takes a minute and still hides how its parts connect. the agent asks what the data answers, picks the kind of diagram for it, and draws that. it is one case of every point of view: the author's story is what they see. six questions about the data, each with the kinds of picture that answer it: how it works, what it is made of, where it stands, what went wrong, how much and how often, what it changes why an agent thinks in text, so it answers in text: steps as a numbered list, states as sentences, a plan as paragraphs, numbers in a row. the author has to rebuild the shape in their head: which step waits on which, where the failure was, what is done and what is not. a picture hands them the shape itself. text that holds data is the author's work left undone. the rule 1. find the data in the reply. steps, calls, states, parts, links, a plan, a failure, numbers over time, a change: anything with a shape. 2. ask what it answers, and take the kind the chooser below gives. two questions, two pictures; never one picture that answers neither well. 3. draw that kind as its page says, on the diagrams page: its parts, its colours, when it does not fit. 4. picture first, words after. the reply opens with the outcome in a line, then the picture, then a line of what it shows; lists and logs come last. 5. check it before it goes: every text inside its box, nothing on top of anything else, every arrow attached, words as big as the text around it, the colours of the page it is shown on. a picture that was not looked at was not checked. 6. no shape, no picture. one fact, a yes or no, a name, a single number: a sentence. which picture for which data - how does it work? steps, calls and decisions: sequence (who talks to whom, in order), flowchart (what is decided where), state (what it can be, what moves it), flywheel (what feeds itself, where it is slow), swimlanes (who does each step), pipeline (files in, steps, files out), call graph (which function calls which). - what is it made of, and where does it run? parts and links: labelled diagram (one thing and its parts), containers (the parts of a system), cloud, tiers, network, isometric (where each part runs), entities (the data and its links), modules and matrix (which file or part needs which), data flow (where data crosses a trust boundary). - where does it stand? progress and plans: milestone (one goal), kanban (many pieces of work), plan graph (what waits on what), roadmap (what is planned when), dashboard (many parts at once), versions (which version runs where). - what went wrong, and why? a failure, a log: timeline (what happened when), chronology (what one user did, in order), cause tree (why), one case (one request, today beside fixed), questions (what is known and what is not), waterfall (where one request spends its time). - how much, how often? numbers: line chart (over time), heatmap (which days and hours), event plot (every event of many actors, at its time), sankey (where it goes, how much), service map (what talks to what, live), flame and treemap (where the time or the size goes). - what does it change, and what first? screen (where on the screen), screenshots before and after (what the author sees), change map (which files), quadrant (what to do first)."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"sequence","code":"","name":"Sequence diagram: who talks to whom","text":"sequence diagram: who talks to whom the course of events: the parts of a system as columns, what they send each other as arrows, top to bottom in time order. a request, a failure, a retry or a fix reads in one look, and today beside proposed shows only the change. its parts: participants with an icon above the name, dashed lifelines under them, messages as arrows top to bottom; blue a request, green a success, red a failure, grey what stays the same fits when - parts pass something along: a person, a browser, a server, a database, a job, an outside service. - the order matters: a failure, a retry, a timeout, a race; what happens where, and after what. - a fix changes the course: a step added, an event dropped, a call moved. today beside proposed. not when - one part alone, nothing passed between: its states are a state diagram, its decisions a flowchart, its look a screenshot. - the order does not matter, only where the parts run or how the data links: a container sketch or an entity-relationship diagram. - the times matter more than who: a timeline. - too many at once: more than five participants or ten messages. split it in two, or say it in a table. - a change the author sees on screen: screenshots before and after say it better. how it is drawn 1. participants: one column each, a plain icon above a short name. a person, a laptop or browser, a server, a database cylinder, a clock for a cronjob, a page for another writer or a file, a cloud for an outside service, a warning triangle for an error tracker. the icons carry most of the reading; the names stay short. 2. lifelines: a dashed vertical line under each participant. 3. messages: arrows between lifelines, top to bottom in time order, each with a few words. a request blue, a failure or an error red, a success or an answer green, what stays the same grey. a note on a step sits on its arrow: 13:05 insert … select , 403 plan limit . 4. a step on a clock or a loop (every minute, a retry until it works): a low rectangle with well-rounded corners (48 high for 18-pixel words, its radius a third of that), spanning the lifelines it involves. one arrowhead sits on its top edge pointing right, one on its bottom edge pointing left, so it reads as a cycle. its period is written inside ( every minute ); what it checks and does goes beside it, right of the lifelines: alive? writing? , then gone, silent 30 s or older: started again . the loop is orange, the waiting colour; its words keep the usual text colours. turned down: a straight arrow reads as one call, not a repeat; an ellipse reads poorly; a uml loop frame around the steps, its tag in the corner, stretches a compact diagram into a long one. a refresh sign, a filled rectangle with an arrow on every side and a path out of a lifeline and back came second. 5. today beside proposed (or before beside after, today beside made): two framed panels, tagged, the same participants in the same order, so only the change differs. the change is drawn, not described: an event crossed out, a step added, a participant greyed. 6. around it: a title line with the item's id and what it is, one line of context under it (where, how often), one line of proof under the panels. 7. in the app's colours, and drawn for the width it is shown at: its words no smaller than the text around it. s4, a plan limit: today the browser sends an error event to the tracker after the upgrade window; proposed, no event examples servermon kept running by the agent: systemd starts the agent, the agent writes and starts servermon, which writes a json line a second and keeps running when the agent restarts; every minute, in an orange rounded loop, the agent asks alive? writing? and starts it again if gone or silent 30 s u2, an upload retried: before, a passing 500 fails the export; after, the job waits and puts the file again, and the export is done d1, a deadlock in a cronjob retried inside the run: today an error event and the rows a run later; made, once more at once and no event d1, the same deadlock with no change proposed: the retry drawn as what would be done only if it grows e7, the exporter's upload: today a failed upload is an error event; proposed, retried up to 3 times, the file arrives, no event one upload through a render service: the browser gets a signed url from the api through cloudflare, puts the file straight to s3, asks for a render; the api queues it and answers 202; later sqs starts lambda, which renders to s3 and marks the job done"},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"case","code":"","name":"One case: today beside fixed","text":"one case: today beside fixed one real request, its answer today beside its answer with the fix: the question on top, two framed panels under it, red for today and green for the fix. big numbers and what works and what is missing follow, so the one case stands for the rest. a shop's help chat asked how much shipping to canada is: today a vague reply, not covered; with a shipping-rates article, a table of four rates, answered; then the numbers, what works and what is missing, by effort fits when - one real request shows the gap: a question a help chat cannot answer, a call that fails, a search that finds nothing. - the fix is content or data, and its effect is the answer itself: a table, a value, a reply. - the author must decide what to do next: the case convinces, the lists say what else is open and what each costs. not when - the course of messages between parts changes, not the answer: a sequence diagram, today beside proposed. - many parts at once, each with its state: a status dashboard. - a change on screen: two screenshots, before and after. how it is drawn 1. the question: quoted big in a soft blue box, where it was asked and when in grey beside it. the real words, not a summary. 2. today: a red frame, a red cross in a circle and \"today:\" with why it fails; inside, the real answer as given and a red tag (\"not_covered\"). 3. with the fix: a green frame, a green tick and \"with\" and the fix; inside, the answer it would give and a green tag (\"answered\"). values go in a small table, a line between rows. 4. a verdict under each: in bold, red or green, what the panel shows; a line under it says where the real answer was seen, or in amber what is still to confirm. 5. kpi cards: four big numbers for the whole, each a few words of what it counts; red or amber for a count that stands for a gap. 6. two lists: \"works\" with green ticks; \"missing, by effort\" with a pill per item, red for work to do (small, medium), amber for what waits on an answer (wait, check, decide). the item the case is about first, in bold. 7. ticks and crosses are drawn as paths, not typed as glyphs, so every font shows them alike."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"screen","code":"","name":"Annotated screen: where on the screen","text":"annotated screen: where on the screen a plain sketch of a screen, grey blocks and a few real labels, with an amber number on each spot that changes; beside it a numbered list says what changes at each. the reader sees where the change is before reading what it is. the checkout page sketched twice, while paying and with the card declined, in grey blocks with real labels for checkout, the total of $84.00, the coupon field and the pay button; amber number 1 sits by the coupon field under the total, 2 by the pay button, faded, with a spinner and the word paying, 3 by a red card declined message right above the blue pay button; beside the sketches a list says 1 the coupon field moves from the top of the page to under the total, 2 pay shows a spinner and stays disabled until the bank answers, 3 the error shows right above the button, not at the top of the page; a now box sums up the three changes fits when - a change to a screen: a field moves, a button gets a new state, a message shows in a new place. - the question is where: words alone would say \"the error moves up\" and leave the reader to guess from where to where. - no screenshots yet: the change is planned, or the screen is still being built. not when - real screenshots exist before and after: one case, the screenshots side by side. - the parts of a system, not of a screen: a container diagram. - a thing's states and what moves it between them: a state diagram. how it is drawn 1. the sketch: the screen as a framed wireframe, its parts as plain grey blocks; only the parts that matter get real labels, such as the total, a field's placeholder or a button's text. 2. states: a frame per state when the change shows in one state only, such as while paying or after an error; the state's name in bold above its frame. one frame when the state does not matter. 3. the numbers: an amber circle with a number beside each spot that changes, in the margin of the frame, never over a label; each number once only, in the frame where it shows best. 4. colour in the sketch: grey for what stays; the changed parts in their real colours, such as a blue button or a red error. 5. the list: beside the sketch, the same amber numbers in order, each with what changes in bold and a line or two of why under it. 6. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence all the changes together."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"state","code":"","name":"State diagram: what moves a thing between its states","text":"state diagram: what moves a thing between its states the states a thing has and what moves it from one to the next: a card's steps, a message's life, a job's run. each state is a box; each arrow says what moves it. a sign-in card: from signed out through the code and the wait to saved; a refused code goes back for a new link, an unread token is shown in its window fits when - a thing has a few states, and the question is how it gets from one to another, or why it stays in one. - a state can be reached by more than one way, or left by a way that goes back. - a bug is a wrong state: stuck, skipped, or reached from where it should not be. not when - the order of calls between parts is the question: that is a sequence diagram. - one decision with its answers, no lasting state: a flowchart. - more than about eight states: draw the part that matters, and say the rest. how it is drawn 1. states: rounded boxes, a short name each. coloured by what they mean: blue a step on the way, green the end that is wanted, orange one that needs a look. 2. transitions: the project's arrows, grey, each with the few words that move it (a click, an answer, a timeout). 3. the start: a filled dot with an arrow into the first state. 4. around it: a title with the thing's name, one line of context, one line under it saying how to read it."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"flywheel","code":"","name":"Flywheel: what feeds itself","text":"flywheel: what feeds itself a loop that grows as it turns: each stage feeds the next, and the last feeds the first, so the next turn starts bigger. a data flywheel is the common case: use makes data, data makes a better model, a better model brings more use. also called a virtuous cycle or a reinforcing loop. search that learns from its users: more users, more searches, clicks logged, clicks as labels, model retrained and a better ranking, evenly on one circle, each with its number; clicks as labels in amber, only 15% usable, the weak link; seed labels by hand come in from outside fits when - growth feeds itself: more users bring more data, more data a better product, a better product more users. - the question is where it is slow: which stage holds back every turn after it. - a start from outside: what was put in by hand to get it turning, seed data or a launch. not when - a thing goes round its states, nothing grows: a state diagram. - a step repeats on a clock (every minute, a retry): the loop on a sequence diagram. - how much each stage moves on: a sankey, its bands as wide as their share. how it is drawn 1. stages: four to six boxes evenly on one circle, clockwise, the outcome on top; each with its name and its number now (40k a day). 2. arrows: blue and bold, arcs of that same circle, each from one stage into the next; the last back into the first. nothing bends off the circle: a wheel that wobbles reads as broken. 3. the weak link: the stage that turns slowest in amber, said in a line under the ring. 4. from outside: what started it or still feeds it, a grey dashed box with its arrow into the stage it feeds. 5. the middle: how long one turn takes."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"flowchart","code":"","name":"Flowchart: what is decided where","text":"flowchart: what is decided where the decisions one action goes through, in order: each question a diamond, each answer its own arrow, each outcome a box. what paste decides: a token, running, this link; each answer leads to its own outcome fits when - one action branches: a click, a request or a job that does different things depending on what it finds. - the question is which way it went, and why: an outcome nobody expected. - a rule has cases, and every case must be seen at once. not when - a thing lives through states and comes back to them: a state diagram. - several parts take turns: a sequence diagram. - more than about six questions: split it, or say it as a table of cases. how it is drawn 1. steps: rectangles; the one that starts it darker. 2. questions: diamonds, a few words ending in a question mark, the words inside the diamond. 3. answers: the project's arrows, one per answer, each labelled (yes, no, or the value). 4. outcomes: boxes coloured by what they mean: green done, orange refused or spent, blue the way on. 5. left to right for the main way, down for the answers that leave it."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"swimlanes","code":"","name":"Swimlanes: who does each step","text":"swimlanes: who does each step a process as its steps in order, a lane per actor, each step a box in the lane of who does it. an arrow leads from each step to the next, and it crosses lanes where the work changes hands. the reader sees who holds the work now and which hand-off waits on them. a refund in four lanes, customer, support, you the shop owner, and payment service: the customer asks for a refund and support checks the order, both done in green; the amber arrow hands it to you, and approves the refund is amber, waiting 2 days; then, dashed grey and still to come, the payment service pays it back and support e-mails the customer; a now box says the refund waits on you to approve fits when - several people or services share one process, each doing a part of it in turn. - the question is who does what, and where the work passes from one to another. - a hand-off is slow or stuck, and the reader sees whose lane it waits in, often their own. not when - messages between parts of a system, in time order: a sequence diagram. - decisions with yes and no branches: a flowchart. - a thing's states and what moves it between them: a state diagram. - many pieces of work by stage, not one process: a kanban board. how it is drawn 1. lanes: one row per actor in one framed panel, the name in bold at the left, thin lines between the rows; two to five lanes, the reader's own named \"you\". 2. steps: one box per step, left to right in order, each in the lane of who does it; a verb in bold and a few words under it. 3. arrows: from each step to the next, down or up out of the box and then right into the next one; the arrowheads touch the boxes. 4. colours: green for steps done, grey dashed for steps to come. a hand-off that waits on the reader is amber, its arrow too, and says for how long. 5. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence who holds the work and what it waits on."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"questions","code":"","name":"Known and open questions: how it works, and the gaps","text":"known and open questions: how it works, and the gaps how something works, stage by stage, with the facts read from the code and the questions no one can answer yet, each where it arises. a panel per stage, left to right; the questions inside the panel they belong to; a row of big numbers under them. gift cards in a small shop: buying, spending and expiring as three green panels of facts; amber questions inside them, a refund, two orders at once, a return, a reminder, the law; 1 240 cards sold, 3 400 left unspent, 5 open questions, 0 reminders sent fits when - someone asks how a thing works, and part of the answer is \"no one knows yet\". - the facts and the gaps must be told apart: what the code says, beside what a person still has to decide or find out. - a meeting needs its questions: each one shown where it bites, not in a list at the end. not when - what is decided where, step by step: a flowchart. - a thing's states and what moves it between them: a state diagram. - where many parts stand: a status dashboard. how it is drawn 1. the title says both halves: \"how it works, and what we don't know\", and the line under it says what green and amber mean. 2. stages: a green panel each, numbered \"1 · buying\", left to right, an arrow between them; a few short lines of facts, the key figures in bold. 3. questions: an amber note inside the panel where the question arises, a thick amber bar on its left; the question in bold, and in grey what is known so far. 4. big numbers: a row under the panels, each a number and two lines of what it counts; amber or red when it stands for a gap. 5. facts only in green: a line in a green panel is read from the code or a log; a guess is a question, and goes in amber."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"causetree","code":"","name":"Cause tree: why it happened","text":"cause tree: why it happened the failure on top, its causes below it as a tree: each row down says why the row above happened. the root cause is amber, the fix green beside it, and the causes that were checked and ruled out stay in grey with the reason. the reader sees why it broke, what one change ends it, and what was already looked at. why customers were charged twice: a red box on top says customers were charged twice, 38 orders on 10-14; below it, the pay button sent twice, two requests 0.4 s apart, beside two grey dashed boxes ruled out, the provider retried (one call each) and a reload resent it (no reloads); below that, the button stays enabled for the 1.8 s it waits, beside a grey box ruled out, the mobile app retried (all 38 on web); at the bottom the root cause in amber, the spinner shows only after the reply, joined by a green line to the fix, disable the button on the first click and show the spinner at once; a now box says a second click gets through while the button waits fits when - something broke and the question is why, down to the one cause a change would end. - several causes were suspected, and the reader sees which were checked and why they are out. - the fix follows from the cause, and the reader sees it is aimed at the root, not at the failure on top. not when - the order of events in one request: a sequence diagram; what happened when, from a log: a timeline. - the cause is not found yet: what is known and what is still open, a questions diagram. - one real request today beside the same with the fix: a case. how it is drawn 1. the failure: a red box on top, what broke in a few words and, under it, how much or since when. 2. the causes: one box per row down, each the \"because\" of the box above it, its name in bold in two or three words and under it what shows it; dark lines join the chain from the failure to the root. 3. ruled out: a grey box with a dashed border beside the cause it was checked against, its name in grey and \"ruled out:\" with the reason; grey dashed lines join it. 4. the root cause: the last box of the chain, amber with an amber border, its name in bold and \"the root cause\" under it. 5. the fix: a green box beside the root cause, joined to it by a green line, the change in bold. 6. now: a blue box under the tree says in one sentence why it broke and what fixes it."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"timeline","code":"","name":"Timeline: what happened when","text":"timeline: what happened when events at their moments, from a log: one line of time, left to right, each event a dot with its time and a few words. a sign-in second by second: started, link opened, code typed, ended with the token unread, then paste starting a new sign-in fits when - a log tells it: times matter, and the gaps between them. - the question is what came before what, or what happened while something else waited. - one event went wrong among others that did not. not when - who sent what to whom matters more than when: a sequence diagram. - the times do not matter, only the order of states: a state diagram. - hundreds of events: pick the few that tell it, and say how many were left out. how it is drawn 1. the line of time: one grey line, left to right. 2. events: a dot on the line, a stem to its label, the time in bold above a few words. the labels go above and below in turn, so close events do not collide. 3. colours: blue for what went as it should, green for an end that is wanted, red for what went wrong. 4. what went wrong gets a note of its own, in red, under its event."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"chronology","code":"","name":"Chronology: what one user did, in order","text":"chronology: what one user did, in order steps in the order they happened, each with its time, at even distances: the order and the times count, not the gaps between them. a long gap is cut short, its length written on the cut. one user's seven steps evenly along a line, from opening the pricing page on monday to upgrading to pro on tuesday, the 23-hour night cut short for more steps than a strip holds, the same chronology goes down a line, the times on the left, one row per step. the same seven steps as a list down a vertical line, the times on the left, the 23-hour night cut short between the fifth and the sixth fits when - one actor's steps: a user, a job, an agent; first this, then that. - the order matters more than the distances: a minute and a night between two steps take the same room. - steps far apart in time: minutes, then a day, then a week; a timeline would squeeze the short ones flat. not when - the gaps are the story: a wait, a timeout, what happened while something else waited: a timeline. - several parts pass something between them: a sequence diagram. - the states matter, not the steps: a state diagram. how it is drawn 1. the line: one grey line, left to right in the strip, top to bottom in the list. 2. steps: a dot each, at even distances. in the strip, a stem to its label, the time in bold above a few words, the labels above and below in turn; in the list, the time on the left, the words on the right. 3. colours: blue for each step, green for the end that is wanted, red for a step that went wrong. 4. a long gap: the line cut by two slanted amber strokes, its length written in amber on the cut. 5. strip or list: a strip up to seven or eight steps; more, the list."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"eventplot","code":"","name":"Event plot: who did what, when","text":"event plot: who did what, when every event of many actors at its moment: a lane per actor, a tick per event at its time, coloured by its kind. also called a raster plot; told as a timeline, a swimlane timeline. who did what today: a lane per account, a tick per save, rename, export, delete or sign-in from 08:00 to 20:00; the busiest account works in bursts all morning, health checks tick evenly all day in a lane of their own fits when - many actors, one log: accounts, users, jobs or services, each with its events over the same hours. - when and by whom matter together: bursts, quiet hours, who works when, what one actor did before another. - every event counts: a single delete in a lane of saves shows as one red tick. not when - one actor's steps: a chronology, or a timeline when the gaps matter. - only how many, by hour and day: a heatmap counts them in cells. - what each actor sends the others: a sequence diagram. how it is drawn 1. lanes: one per actor, its name on the left; the busiest on top. 2. the time: one axis under the lanes, thin grey lines at even hours. 3. ticks: a thin bar per event at its time, coloured by its kind, a legend of the kinds on top. 4. totals: at the end of each lane, how many events it holds. 5. noise: health checks, bots, staff in a lane of their own, under the rest; the many quiet actors folded into one lane, \"31 other accounts\"."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"milestone","code":"","name":"Milestone track: where a goal stands","text":"milestone track: where a goal stands one goal and its steps on a line, left to right: the steps done, the step now, the steps ahead, and who each one waits on. payments open to every shop, 3 of 6 done: the card form, refunds and five shops trying it done with their dates; fees shown at checkout now, waiting on you to approve the text; opening to every shop by you and watching the first week by me ahead fits when - one goal, a few steps: four to seven, each done or not. - the question is what is next and who it waits on: the reader or the agent. - the same track is shown again later, and the reader sees how far it moved. not when - what happened when matters, not what is left: a timeline. - a thing's states and what moves it between them: a state diagram. - many parts at once, each with its own state: a status dashboard. how it is drawn 1. the band: one framed panel; a flag, the goal's name in bold and how many steps are done, in blue, beside it. 2. the steps: dots on one line, left to right. a done step is green with a tick, a green line up to the step now; the step now is a blue ring with its number; the steps ahead are grey, numbered, on a grey line. 3. under each dot: its name in a word or two, and under that its date when done, or who it waits on: \"waits on you\" in amber for the step now, \"by you\" or \"by me\" for the steps ahead. 4. now: a blue box under the line names the step now and what it waits on, in one sentence."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"plangraph","code":"","name":"Plan graph: what waits on what","text":"plan graph: what waits on what a plan's parts as boxes, an arrow from each part to the parts that wait on it. parts that can run side by side stand in one column. the longest chain is bold blue: it sets the day the plan ends. a coupon field at checkout in five parts over seven days: p1 the coupon api, 2 days, done in green; then side by side p2 the form, 3 days, under way in blue, and p3 the e-mail, 1 day, not started in grey; both lead to p4 tests, 1 day, not started; then p5 your go, waiting on you in amber; the arrows p1 to p2 to p4 to p5 are bold blue, the longest chain; a now box says a late form moves the ship day, the e-mail can slip 2 days, and then it waits on your go fits when - a plan in parts, some of which wait on others and some of which can run at the same time. - the question is what holds the end date: which parts must not slip, and which have time to spare. - a part waits on the reader, and the reader sees how much waits behind it. not when - one goal's steps in a line, one after another: a milestone track. - the months ahead, bars against dates: a roadmap. - work by stage today, not by what it waits on: a kanban board. - what is decided where, questions and their answers: a flowchart. how it is drawn 1. the parts: one box per part, its name in bold, p1, p2 and so on with a few words; under it how long it takes and where it stands. three to eight parts. 2. the columns: left to right in the order the parts can start, the days on top. parts that can run side by side are stacked in one column. 3. the arrows: from a part to each part that waits on it, from box edge to box edge, thin grey. 4. the longest chain: its arrows bold blue, so the reader sees which parts move the end date and which can slip. 5. where each stands: green is done, blue under way, grey with a dashed border not started, amber with \"waits on you\" a part the reader must do or approve. 6. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence what holds the end date, what has time to spare and what waits on the reader."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"roadmap","code":"","name":"Roadmap: what is planned when","text":"roadmap: what is planned when the months ahead on one line of time, a lane per area of work, each piece of work a bar from its start to its end; a red line marks today. the reader sees what is done, what is under way, what is at risk and what comes next. the shop from july to december: payments has the card form done and bank payouts under way; checkout has apple pay under way and tax by country at risk in amber after it; help centre has the gift card articles done and search planned in grey; a red dashed line marks today, 10-16; a now box says tax by country is at risk because it needs apple pay fits when - several areas of work over months: what each one does when, side by side. - the question is what comes next and what is late, measured against today. - one piece of work waits on another, and the reader sees the delay push the next one. not when - one goal and its steps, done or not, no dates ahead: a milestone track. - work by stage today, not by month: a kanban board. - what already happened, from a log: a timeline. how it is drawn 1. time: the months left to right in one framed panel, their names on top; four to twelve of them. 2. lanes: one row per area of work, its name in bold on the left, thin lines between the rows; two to five lanes. 3. bars: one per piece of work, from its start to its end, its name inside in two or three words. green is done, blue under way, amber at risk with its name in amber, grey with a dashed border planned. 4. today: a red dashed line down the panel, behind the bars, its date on a red pill at the bottom. 5. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence what is under way and what is at risk, and why."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"kanban","code":"","name":"Kanban board: where each piece of work is","text":"kanban board: where each piece of work is many pieces of work at once, each in the column of its stage: to do, doing, in review, done. the board says what is in flight, where work piles up, and what waits on whom. the checkout's work this week: to do holds saved cards, the receipt e-mail and the coupon field, one more hidden; doing is full at 3 of 3, apple pay, tax by country, and bank payouts blocked for 3 days in red; review holds the fees text, waiting on you in amber; done holds the card form and refunds with their dates, three more hidden; a now box says what is stuck fits when - many pieces of work at once, each in one stage, none of them the whole goal. - the question is what is in flight: what is stuck, what piles up before a stage, what waits on the reader. - a stage has a limit, and the reader sees it is full before more work is started. not when - one goal and its steps in order: a milestone track. - a thing's states and what moves it between them: a state diagram. - parts of a system and their health, not work to do: a status dashboard. - what happened when: a timeline. how it is drawn 1. the columns: one framed column per stage, left to right in the order work moves; three to five of them. 2. the header: the stage's name in bold, its count at the right. a stage with a limit shows \"3 of 3\" in blue when full; red when over. 3. the cards: one per piece of work, its name in bold in two or three words, under it who it is by and how long it has been there. 4. what stands out: a card that waits on the reader is amber with \"waits on you\"; a blocked card has a red border and says for how long; a done card has a green tick and its date. 5. long columns: a few cards are shown, the rest as \"+ 3 more\" under them; done shows the newest first. 6. now: a blue box under the board says in one sentence what is stuck and what waits on whom."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"quadrant","code":"","name":"Priority quadrant: what to do first","text":"priority quadrant: what to do first many pieces of work weighed on two things only: how much each is worth and how much it costs. value goes up, effort goes right, and the square splits in four quarters: do first, plan, later, skip. the reader sees what to start with and what to drop. eight checkout ideas on value and effort: the green do first quarter, high value and low effort, holds apple pay and saved cards; the blue plan quarter, high value and high effort, holds the one-page checkout and tax by country; the grey later quarter, low value and low effort, holds the coupon field and the receipt e-mail; the dashed grey skip quarter, low value and high effort, holds dark mode and gift wrap as hollow dots; a now box says apple pay and saved cards come first and gift wrap and dark mode are dropped fits when - many ideas and room for a few, and the question is which ones to start with. - two things decide it: what a piece of work brings and what it costs, each a rough guess, not a measure. - the reader must say no to some of them, and wants to see why. not when - work by stage today, in flight or stuck: a kanban board. - the months ahead, what is planned when: a roadmap. - options compared on many criteria, not two: a table, a row per option and a column per criterion. how it is drawn 1. the axes: value up the left side, effort along the bottom, each an arrow with its name; no numbers, only more and less. 2. the quarters: four framed boxes with a gap between them, each named in its outer corner in bold, what it means under the name. do first is green, top left; plan is blue, top right; later is grey, bottom left; skip is grey with a dashed border, bottom right. 3. the dots: one per piece of work, where its value and effort put it, in its quarter's colour; a skipped one is hollow. four to twelve of them. 4. the labels: the name in bold on its own light pill beside its dot, clear of the quarter's border and of other pills; right of the dot, or left when the right has no room. 5. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence what comes first and what is dropped."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"containers","code":"","name":"Container sketch (C4): where the parts run","text":"container sketch (c4): where the parts run which parts a system has, where each one runs, and what goes between them: the container level of the c4 model, drawn as a sketch. where the parts of a notes app run: the app, its folder and an agent box on a laptop; a router and agent machines; model and speech services on the internet fits when - the question is where: which machine, which box, which service runs a part. - something crosses a boundary: a laptop to a server, a box to the internet, and what goes across. - a newcomer needs the whole before any one part. not when - the order of the calls matters: a sequence diagram. - the data and how it links is the question: an entity-relationship diagram. - the inside of one part: its states or its decisions, a state diagram or a flowchart. how it is drawn 1. where things run: dashed boxes with their names at the top left (a laptop, other machines, the internet). 2. parts: boxes inside them, a name and one line of what each is; the one the reply is about in blue. 3. what goes between them: the project's arrows, grey, each with a few words. 4. names: the standard ones from the glossary of names (laptop, router, vps1, box), never a private host. examples a render service as c4 containers: a user, cloudflare and stripe outside; inside, the proxy, the api, the render queue, the renderer, the database, the cache and the media store, each with its technology and what it does"},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"entities","code":"","name":"Entity-relationship: what data there is and how it links","text":"entity-relationship: what data there is and how it links the kinds of data a system keeps, their main fields, and how many of one go with one of another: 1 to n, n to n, marked on each line's ends. a note's data: a note holds messages, messages are linked in subjects, a message has files fits when - the question is about data: where something is kept, what points to what. - a change touches a link: one becomes many, or a link goes away. - a newcomer reads the storage before the code. not when - where the data lives (which machine, which service): a container sketch. - what happens to one record over time: a state diagram. - every field of every table: keep the main ones; the schema is in the code. how it is drawn 1. entities: a box per kind of data, its name in bold on a tinted band, its main fields under it. 2. links: plain lines, no arrowheads, a verb under the line (holds, has, linked in). 3. their ends, crow's feet: two short bars across the line where it is one, a three-pronged foot with a bar before it where it is many (one or more). 1 and n in bold beside them, for a reader who does not know the marks. 4. left to right in the order a reader meets them; a child under its parent."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"dashboard","code":"","name":"Status dashboard: where each part stands","text":"status dashboard: where each part stands a rag status dashboard (red, amber, green): the state of many parts at once, each a tile coloured by its state. big numbers on top, the tiles grouped below, the gaps gathered at the end. two more kinds at the end: tiles coloured by where each part is, and a whole beside its gap. the sign-in service: 4 of 8 parts in place, 212 unit tests pass, 1 browser test failed; providers and tests as tiles; the gaps, apple's key, the browser test, the audit log and the docs, gathered at the end fits when - the question is where things stand: many parts, each in place, stale or missing. - the reader must find the gaps fast: a project's checks, a release, an audit of what is covered. - the same view is read again later, and the colours show what moved. not when - one thing and its states over time: a state diagram. - what happened in what order: a timeline or a sequence diagram. - a few parts only: a sentence or a list says it; a dashboard needs enough tiles to scan. how it is drawn 1. the key: green in place and checked, amber stale or unchecked, red missing or failed, in one line under the title. 2. kpi cards: three or four big numbers on top, each a word of what it counts and a line of context, coloured like the worst it stands for. 3. the status board: a panel per area, its name and a line of what it holds; a tile per part, its name in bold and its state in its colour. 4. the gaps: the amber and red tiles again, gathered in a panel at the end, so the reader does not hunt for them. 5. real states only: a tile's word says why it has its colour (\"1 failed\", \"no test\"), never just \"bad\". coloured by where it is when the question is where each part is, not how it is, the colours name places: production, development, the working tree. the list behind a tile opens on a click, in a panel beside the grid. the help centre's 12 topics: 7 on production, 4 on development not released, 1 only in the working tree; coupons and sizes with a red dot; gift cards selected, its questions and sources in the side panel 1. a card per place, in the colour its tiles get: green, on production as it is; blue, on development and not released; amber, only in the working tree and not committed. 2. each tile in the colour of its latest change: its name in bold, a small word for what is not out yet (\"not released\"). 3. a red dot on a tile: a question it cannot answer, its data missing. 4. the key under the grid, the red dot in it too. 5. details on a click: the chosen tile gets a dark outline; the panel beside the grid shows its name, a tag of where it is, and short sections, headed in small caps, of a few bullets each. the tile never grows to hold its list. a whole and its gap when the question is how much is covered, one bar shows the whole and what is missing from it; a row of squares counts the runs. the help centre's 86 articles as one bar, 52 how-tos, 28 faqs and a grey strip of 6, then a dashed red part for coupons and sizes; beside it 24 stories as squares, 19 passed, 1 failed, 4 skipped - the bar: a segment per kind, as wide as its count, dark green, light green, a thin strip for the rest. - the gap: a dashed red segment at the end, hatched soft red, its label on a pill: \"+ coupons, sizes\". - a grey line under the bar names the strip and the dashed part. - units as squares, in rows: filled green passed, filled red failed, empty with a red outline skipped. a grey line under them says which failed, when, and why the rest were skipped."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"linechart","code":"","name":"Line chart: a number over time","text":"line chart: a number over time one number over days or hours, a point for each, joined by a line; a dashed line marks the moment something changed, a deploy or a fix. the reader sees the number before and after, and whether it stayed. the checkout page's p95 load time from 10-01 to 10-14, a point a day, 0 to 3 s up the side: before the deploy the line is red at about 2.4 s, between 2.3 and 2.6; a blue dashed line marks the deploy of 10-08; after it the line is green at about 0.9 s, between 0.8 and 1.0; a grey dashed line marks the goal of 1.5 s; a now box says the deploy cut p95 from about 2.4 s to 0.9 s, well under the goal fits when - one number, measured again and again: a load time, an error rate, a queue's length; two numbers at most. - the question is before and after: did the deploy or the fix move the number, and did it stay moved. - a goal or a limit exists, and the reader sees which side of it the line is on. not when - one run's time by function: a flame graph. - when something happens by hour and day of the week: a heatmap. - work planned over months: a roadmap. - events from a log, not a measured number: a timeline. how it is drawn 1. time: left to right along the bottom, the dates or hours under it, every other one when they crowd; a week to a month of days, or a day of hours. 2. the number: up the side from zero, a few round values with their unit (\"1 s\", \"2 s\"), a thin line across the panel at each. 3. the line: a dot per measurement, joined. red before the change, green after it when the number improved; one colour when nothing changed. 4. the change: a blue dashed line down the panel at its moment, its name and date on a blue pill at the top. 5. the goal: a grey dashed line across, its value at the right end; only when there is one. 6. what it says: the typical value before and after in the line's colour, next to the line, in a few words. 7. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence what changed and by how much."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"heatmap","code":"","name":"Heatmap: when it happens","text":"heatmap: when it happens a week in one grid: the days as rows, the hours as columns, each cell as dark as its count. the reader sees at once which hours of which days pile up, and which stay quiet. checkout errors from 5 to 11 october, a row per day from monday to sunday and a column per two hours from 00 to 22; a legend scale runs from grey for none through five ambers to the darkest for 50 and more; most cells are pale, the weekend almost blank; a blue frame holds monday to friday at 08 and 10, every cell dark with its count inside, from 38 to 63; under the grid the hot spot is named, mon to fri 08:00 to 12:00, 493 of 684 errors; a now box says 7 in 10 errors hit weekday mornings, while the late nightly import still runs fits when - many events, and the question is when: which hours of which days they come at. - a pattern repeats by the day of the week or the hour of the day, and a line of time would hide it. - one spot stands out, and its days and hours point at a cause: a job that runs late, a peak of visitors, a weekly report. not when - one number over time, going up or down: a line chart. - events in their order, each with its moment: a timeline. - parts and their health, not the hours: a status dashboard. how it is drawn 1. the grid: in one framed panel, a row per day from monday to sunday, its name in bold on the left; a column per hour, or per two hours, its start on top. 2. the cells: one per day and hour, as dark as its count: grey for none, then pale to dark amber; five or six steps in all. 3. the scale: on the line under the title, a swatch per step with its range, from none to the darkest. 4. the hot spot: a blue frame around its cells, their counts inside them; under the grid, in blue, its days, its hours and its share of the whole. 5. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence when most of it happens and why."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"versions","code":"","name":"Versions: which version runs where","text":"versions: which version runs where a table of what is deployed: one row per part, its version, its commit and its date, and whether it is the latest. a verdict in a sentence on top, what \"latest\" means at the bottom. what runs on production: api, web and worker run their latest release; the image resizer and the mail sender were deployed by hand, so no one can tell which code they run fits when - the question is what runs now: before a release, after an incident, when a fix should already be live. - some parts are deployed by hand, and the reader must see which of them no one can vouch for. - many parts, each with its own version, too many to keep in mind. not when - how the parts talk to each other: a containers diagram. - where the parts run: a cloud diagram. - how healthy they are now: a service map or a status dashboard. how it is drawn 1. the time it was read, under the title: a version table is true only for the moment it was taken. 2. the verdict first: one sentence, its first half in bold, says what the table shows before the reader reads a row. 3. a row per deployed part: its name in bold, the version big and bold, the commit in the look of a link, the date. 4. latest or not: a green tick and \"latest\", or an amber \"? unknown\"; never a guess. a grey note says why: \"nothing on main is left out\", or how it was deployed and the newest code there is. 5. what \"latest\" means, in a line under the table, and where it was read from. 6. the reader on top, agents below: the summary first; the sources of every version, the files and logs it was read from, go under a line, in a part of their own for agents."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"cloud","code":"","name":"Cloud architecture diagram: what runs where","text":"cloud architecture diagram: what runs where the parts of a system inside the places they run: the internet, a cdn's edge, a cloud region, its network and subnets, the managed services beside it. each part an icon with its name, the links as arrows. a render service on aws: users and stripe on the internet, cloudflare at the edge; in the region a vpc with the load balancer and nginx in its public subnet, the api, postgresql and redis in its private one; sqs, lambda and s3 managed, outside the vpc fits when - the question is where: which region, which network, which subnet a part runs in, and what is managed by the cloud. - a link crosses a boundary: from the internet into the network, out to an outside service. - a design review or a newcomer needs the whole system on one page. not when - what each part does matters more than where: a c4 container diagram. - ports and who may connect: a network diagram. - the order of the calls: a sequence diagram. how it is drawn 1. the places: nested dashed frames, each named inside at its top left: the internet, the edge, the region, the network, the subnets. 2. parts: a small icon over the name and one line of what it is. 3. links: the project's arrows, coloured by kind (requests blue, data green, background jobs orange), a few words on the long ones."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"network","code":"","name":"Network diagram: addresses, ports, who may connect","text":"network diagram: addresses, ports, who may connect the network engineer's view: address ranges as bands, the gateways at their borders, a security group around what it guards with what it lets in, every link with its protocol and port. the render service's network: a vpc of 10.0.0.0/16 with a public subnet for the load balancer and nginx and a private one for the api, postgresql and redis; each security group with its rule, each link with its port: 443, 80, 3000, 5432, 6379 fits when - \"why can't x reach y?\": a port, a rule or a subnet in the way. - a firewall or security-group review: what is open, from where. - addresses matter: ranges, gateways, what has a public address. not when - what each part does: a c4 container diagram. - what data crosses where, for a threat model: a data flow diagram. how it is drawn 1. bands: each subnet a filled band with its name and range. 2. guards: a security group a dashed outline around what it guards, its rule on the outline (\"5432 from sg-api\"). 3. links: every arrow with its protocol and port on a pill."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"tiers","code":"","name":"Layered diagram: the tiers a request passes","text":"layered diagram: the tiers a request passes the parts in columns by tier, left to right in the order a request meets them: the edge, the entry, the compute, the background work, the data. the main path bold, the side paths in their own style. the render service in five tiers: cloudflare at the edge, the load balancer and nginx at the entry, the api in compute, sqs and lambda for background work, postgresql, redis and s3 as data; the main path blue, the render jobs dashed green fits when - how deep a request goes: which tiers it crosses before an answer. - where a part belongs: a new service placed in its tier, or one in the wrong one. - a caching or scaling talk: what each tier absorbs. not when - where things run, in which network: a cloud architecture diagram. - many links between many parts: a dependency matrix. how it is drawn 1. tiers: columns with a name and a line under it, left to right. 2. parts: boxes in their tier; outside parties outside the columns. 3. paths: the main one bold blue, the background one dashed green, the rest thin grey; a key under the drawing."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"isometric","code":"","name":"Isometric diagram: the system as a model","text":"isometric diagram: the system as a model the same parts as blocks on an isometric floor, the networks as raised platforms, the main paths along the grid: a picture to point at on a slide, read before any word. the render service as an isometric model: users and cloudflare on the internet; the region's vpc a slab with a platform for each subnet; the api, postgresql, redis and the replica on the private one; sqs, lambda and s3 beside it fits when - a slide or a talk: the whole system at once, for people who will not read a diagram's notation. - groups matter more than links: what sits together on which platform. not when - exact links, ports or order: any other kind says it better; an isometric picture hides lines behind blocks. - the picture will change often: the layout is slow to redraw. how it is drawn 1. the floor: a faint isometric grid; places as dashed outlines on it. 2. platforms: a raised slab per network, its name on a pill. 3. parts: blocks for services, cylinders for stores, a name on a pill. 4. paths: only the main ones, along the grid, coloured by kind."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"servicemap","code":"","name":"Service map: what talks to what, live","text":"service map: what talks to what, live what a tracing tool draws from real traffic: each service a circle with a ring for its health, each link as thick as its traffic, the numbers on the links. the unwell one stands out at a glance. the render service's map: cloudflare 1000 requests a second, 300 through the load balancer and nginx to the api, 150 to redis, 120 to the replica; lambda ringed red with 3% errors, the replica orange with 0.4 s of lag fits when - on call: which hop is slow or failing, and who depends on it. - traffic matters: where the requests go, how many. - the real system, as measured, not as designed. not when - the design, before it runs: a c4 container diagram. - how much of the traffic stops where: a sankey diagram. how it is drawn 1. services: circles laid out freely, an icon inside, the name and one number under it. 2. health: the ring green, orange when slow, red when failing, dashed grey for what is not traced. 3. links: thickness by traffic, \"n req/s\" on a pill; a legend."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"sankey","code":"","name":"Sankey diagram: where the traffic goes, how much","text":"sankey diagram: where the traffic goes, how much traffic as bands that split and end, each as wide as its share: what a cache absorbs, what reaches each part behind it. 1000 requests a second reach cloudflare; 700 are served from its cache, 300 go on through the load balancer and nginx to the api, which reads 150 from redis, 120 from the replica, 30 from the primary fits when - capacity and cost: how much each layer absorbs and passes on. - a cache's worth: the share that never reaches the origin. not when - who calls whom: a service map or a layered diagram. - flows too small to see beside big ones: say them in a line under it. how it is drawn 1. columns: a thin bar per part, its name above. 2. bands: from bar to bar, widths to one scale, each with its number. 3. ends: what stops early ends at its own bar, in its own colour."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"waterfall","code":"","name":"Trace waterfall: where one request spends its time","text":"trace waterfall: where one request spends its time one request traced across services: a row per span, each a bar from its start to its end on one scale of milliseconds, a called span indented under its caller. the reader sees the slowest span, what runs in turn, and the gaps where the request only waits. a trace of post /checkout, 1840 ms: the api's bar spans it all; under it auth checks the token in 40 ms, the cart service loads the cart in 240 ms with a database read of 180 ms indented under it, then an amber band marks 160 ms of waiting, then the payment provider charges the card in 1200 ms in red, the database saves the order in 80 ms and the mail queue takes the receipt in 30 ms; a now box says the payment provider takes 1200 of the 1840 ms and 160 ms before it is a wait fits when - \"why is this request slow?\": one request, measured across the services it passes. - calls in turn or at once: the reader sees what waits for what, and what could run side by side. - time lost between spans: a gap where nothing runs is as plain as the work. not when - the call stacks of one process, by function: a flame graph. - who talks to whom, in order, with the messages: a sequence diagram. - a number over time, many requests: a line chart. how it is drawn 1. time: milliseconds left to right in one framed panel, the ticks on top, faint dashed lines down from them; zero is the request's start. 2. rows: one per span in the order it starts, the service in bold and what it did after it; a called span indented under its caller. 3. milliseconds: each span's time in a column right before its bar, so the numbers read down one line. 4. bars: one per span from its start to its end, to one scale; a few milliseconds still get a thin bar. the slowest span is solid red, its name and time in red. 5. waits: a gap where no span runs is an amber band behind the rows, its length on an amber pill under them. 6. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence where the time goes."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"dataflow","code":"","name":"Data flow diagram: where data crosses a trust boundary","text":"data flow diagram: where data crosses a trust boundary the threat modeller's view: outside parties, processes and data stores, the flows between them labelled with the data they carry, and the trust boundaries as red dashed lines. each crossing gets a number to discuss. the render service's data flows: a user's login and cookie through cloudflare into the vpc, banner json into postgresql, render jobs to sqs, rendered pngs to s3, a card token to stripe; eight numbered crossings, three noted: tls ends at the load balancer, the upload skips the waf, only a token leaves fits when - a security review or a threat model: each crossing is a place to ask what can go wrong (stride per crossing). - privacy: where personal data goes and what leaves. not when - ports and rules: a network diagram. - the order of one request: a sequence diagram. how it is drawn 1. notation: outside parties as boxes, processes as circles, stores as two open lines; a key. 2. flows: arrows named by their data (\"card token\"), not by protocol. 3. boundaries: red dashed lines; a numbered badge where a flow crosses one, the risky ones noted under the drawing."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"matrix","code":"","name":"Dependency matrix: every link in a grid","text":"dependency matrix: every link in a grid the parts as rows and the same parts as columns; a filled cell where the row calls the column. every link at once, however many, with no lines to cross. twelve parts of the render service in a grid: users call cloudflare on 443, the api calls redis, postgresql, the replica, sqs and stripe; the one call below the diagonal, lambda writing back to postgresql, in orange fits when - many parts: where a graph turns into a hairball. - layers and cycles: with the parts in order, a call below the diagonal goes against the layers. not when - a few parts: a layered diagram reads faster. - the order of the calls: a sequence diagram. how it is drawn 1. rows and columns: the same parts in the same order, the column names turned upright, groups bracketed on the left. 2. cells: filled where the row calls the column, with a port or a verb when it fits. 3. reading: a line under it says how (\"the row calls the column\")."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"callgraph","code":"","name":"Call graph: which function calls which","text":"call graph: which function calls which a program from its entry down: each function a box, an arrow to each one it calls, the related ones framed together, the helpers everything leans on at the bottom. bin/build from main down: the steps for the rules pages, the glossaries and the agents pages, each with its parser and renderer, all leaning on template_fill and html_escape; 16 of its 44 functions drawn, the rest as \"+n more\" fits when - a change is planned: what calls the function, what it touches. - a newcomer reads a script: its steps and its shared helpers. - dead code: a function nothing points at. not when - files, not functions: a module graph. - what the script reads and writes: a data flow of a script. - where the time goes: a flame graph. how it is drawn 1. layers: the entry at the top, each call one row lower. 2. groups: light frames for the functions of one step. 3. too many: the main ones drawn, the rest a \"+n more\" box."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"pipeline","code":"","name":"Data flow of a script: files in, steps, files out","text":"data flow of a script: files in, steps, files out what a script reads on the left, the steps it makes of it in the middle, what it writes on the right; each arrow named by what passes along it. bin/build: docs/rules.md, the templates, the glossaries, the guides and the pictures in; parse, read guides, render and stamp in the middle; docs/rules.html and four more pages out, and the hand-written pages stamped in place fits when - \"where does my edit end up?\": from a source file to the page. - a build, an import, an export: a script whose job is files. not when - the functions inside: a call graph. - a system of services: a layered diagram. how it is drawn 1. files: a page icon and the path; inputs left, outputs right, the ones rewritten in place in their own colour. 2. steps: rounded boxes in the middle. 3. arrows: named by what passes (\"markdown\", \"index\", \"html\")."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"modules","code":"","name":"Module graph: which file needs which","text":"module graph: which file needs which the files of a project as boxes in their folders, an arrow from each file to each one it requires, the packages from outside in their own shape. the rulebook's files: tests require src/make_rules_config.js and plugin.js, which require the rules and helpers; bin/build requires package.json and node-helpers and runs bin/self-check; the npm packages eslint, postcss and playwright below fits when - what breaks when a file changes: everything that points at it. - cycles and layers: an arrow that goes back up. - what a script drags in from outside. not when - functions inside one file: a call graph. - hundreds of files: a dependency matrix. how it is drawn 1. folders: dashed frames named at the top left. 2. files and packages: a box with a folded corner for a file, a rounded one with a cube for a package. 3. arrows: blue for a require, dashed orange for running it as a process; \"+n more\" for the rest."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"changemap","code":"","name":"Change map: what a change touches","text":"change map: what a change touches the project's folder tree, trimmed to the files one change touches: each file a row, marked added, changed or removed, its lines added and removed at the right. the reader sees where the change lands and how big it is before reading the diff. the coupon field commit in the shop: on top 6 files, 3 added, 2 changed, 1 removed, +312 and −148 lines; under src/api, coupons.js added with +64 and old-discounts.js removed and struck through with −92, 4 more files folded; under src/checkout, checkout.vue changed with +18 −27 and coupon-field.vue added with +121; components, pages and store folded; tests/coupon.test.js added with +88; docs/coupons.md changed with +21 −29; bin and public folded; each file has a bar of green for lines added and red for lines removed; a now box says it is mostly new code and checkout.vue is the file to check fits when - one commit or one pull request: which files it adds, changes and removes, and where in the project they sit. - the question is where to look first: the big rows, the removed file, the one changed file in a folder of new ones. - a change spreads over folders: code, tests and docs, and the reader sees that each got its part. not when - which file needs which: a module graph. - how big each part of the code is, not what one change did: a treemap. - a script's data flow, files in and files out: a pipeline. how it is drawn 1. the total: on top of the panel, the number of files, then how many added, changed and removed in their colours; the lines added in green and removed in red at the right. 2. the tree: folders in bold with a slash, each level indented, thin grey lines from a folder to what it holds. 3. the files: one row each, a mark and its name in colour: green + added, blue ~ changed, red − removed and struck through. 4. the lines: at the right of each file, the lines added and removed as numbers, then a bar of green and red as long as they are. 5. what it leaves alone: folders and files the change does not touch fold into one grey row, \"… 4 more files\" or \"… bin/, public/\". 6. now: a blue box under the panel says in one sentence what the change is and which file to check first."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"flame","code":"","name":"Flame graph: where one run spends its time","text":"flame graph: where one run spends its time one measured run: each call stack a column of bars, the caller under the callee, each bar as wide as its share of the time. the widest bars at the top are where the time goes. one run of bin/build, 1531 ms: 889 ms waiting for bin/self-check, 363 ms in build_index_html, most of that reading whole images only to learn their size fits when - \"why is it slow?\": the time of a run by function, measured. - before and after a speed fix: the same bar, narrower. not when - the structure, not the time: a call graph. - time across services: a sequence diagram of a trace. how it is drawn 1. bars: bottom-up, the whole run at the bottom, widths to one scale, the name and the milliseconds in each. 2. too narrow: a letter in the bar, its name in a key; a narrow part that matters zoomed above. 3. self time: the bars that spend it themselves marked in orange."},
{"page":"diagrams.html","id":"treemap","code":"","name":"Code map: how big each part is","text":"code map: how big each part is a treemap of the code: each function a rectangle, its area its lines, grouped by the part of the program it belongs to. bin/build's 44 functions by size: the guides 184 lines, the glossaries 173, the indexes 157, the shared helpers 97; glossary_from_markdown the biggest at 76 fits when - where the code is: the big parts to split, the parts that grow. - a first look at a large file or project. not when - who calls whom: a call graph. - where the time goes: a flame graph; size is not cost. how it is drawn 1. groups: a frame per part, its name and its lines in the header. 2. rectangles: one per function, area by lines, the name and the count when they fit. 3. the small ones: merged into a dashed \"n small functions\" box."},
{"page":"apis.html","id":"compact","code":"","name":"Compact: one resource, every request a row","text":"compact: one resource, every request a row a whole resource on one picture: each request a row, in the order they are sent, with what it sends, what it gets back and what the server keeps after it. which request reads, adds, changes or removes, and what a repeat does, reads down a column. the articles api: get the list, get one, post, put, patch and delete, each a row with what it sends, its answer, the articles stored after it and what sending it twice does; a red line for an article that is not there fits when - a resource and its requests: list, read, add, replace, change, remove; the reader learns the api, or checks what it does. - the difference between requests matters: put beside patch, post beside put, what a repeat does. - the state after each request says more than its answer: what is added, replaced, gone. not when - one request in detail, its headers and body, an error's answer: the expanded form. - the order of calls between parts, a server, a database, a queue: a sequence diagram. - many resources at once: a compact picture each, or a matrix of addresses by methods. how it is drawn 1. a row per request, in the order they are sent, on a card of its own: the method as a badge, the address in a monospace font. 2. the method's colour says what it does to the data: blue reads, green adds, orange changes, red removes. a legend under the picture says so. 3. five columns: the request; what it sends (a body in a code box, else \"nothing\"); what it gets back (the status in green, a line on the body); what is stored after it (each record a chip, the one touched in the method's colour, a removed one faded in a dashed red box); sent twice (\"same\", or what changes more in orange). 4. one small example of data runs through every row (articles 1 and 2, then 3 added, changed and removed), so each row's change shows. 5. what fails goes in a red line under the rows: a record that is not there, fields that are wrong, with their statuses. 6. around it: a title with the resource, one line of how to read it."},
{"page":"apis.html","id":"expanded","code":"","name":"Expanded: one request taken apart","text":"expanded: one request taken apart one request and its answer, every part named: the method and address, the headers, the body, the status. each part sits in a code box as it is sent, its name and what it is for beside it, so a reader learns what a request is made of and what to look at in one. a patch to article 3 and its 200 answer: method and address, headers, a body with only the title, then the status, headers and the whole article as it is now, each part named on the side fits when - one request matters in detail: the one a bug is in, the one a client has to build, the one the docs explain first. - the answer has to be read: what the status says, what the body holds, how an error names the field that is wrong. - a newcomer learns the shape of a request once, to read the compact form after it. not when - the whole resource, all its requests side by side: the compact form. - parts talking to each other, in time order: a sequence diagram. - the difference is one field: the request and the answer as text, the field marked, say it better. how it is drawn 1. two code boxes, the request on top and the answer under it, each in its parts as they are sent: the first line, the headers, the body. a dashed line between the parts. 2. the name of each part beside it, on a coloured bar: the method's colour on the method and address, green on a status that worked and red on one that failed, blue on a body, grey on headers. under the name, a few words on what it is for in this request. 3. real values, short: a token cut with \"…\", a body with only the fields that matter. 4. a failure is drawn the same way, the status and the body that names what is wrong in red, so the reader sees what a client gets back. 5. around it: a title with what the request does, one line of what it shows. a post with no title and its 422 answer: the status and an errors body naming the title as required, both in red"}
];
